window.EXAM_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 4, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A 68-year-old man with coronary artery disease presents with melena and a hemoglobin of 7.8 g/dL. He is hemodynamically stable and asymptomatic at rest. According to current transfusion guidance, what hemoglobin threshold should generally trigger transfusion in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Transfuse only if hemoglobin falls below 5 g/dL regardless of symptoms.", "B": "Transfuse now regardless of hemoglobin given his cardiac history.", "C": "Withhold transfusion entirely unless he becomes hemodynamically unstable.", "D": "Use a slightly higher threshold near 8 g/dL given his cardiovascular disease.", "E": "Transfuse to a target hemoglobin above 10 g/dL to maximize oxygen delivery."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use a slightly higher threshold near 8 g/dL given his cardiovascular disease): Patients with significant cardiovascular disease are generally transfused at a slightly higher hemoglobin threshold than the standard restrictive level of 7 g/dL, often around 8 g/dL, to preserve cardiac oxygen delivery. Waiting until hemoglobin falls to 5 g/dL ignores his cardiac risk. Transfusing regardless of hemoglobin is not evidence based. Targeting above 10 g/dL reflects an outdated liberal strategy shown to worsen outcomes."}, {"id": 7, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A patient with known cirrhosis presents with hematemesis and hypotension. Endoscopy has not yet been performed. Which combination of medications should be started immediately while arrangements for endoscopy are made?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous furosemide and spironolactone for ascites control.", "B": "Octreotide infusion and empiric intravenous antibiotics.", "C": "Oral rifaximin and lactulose for hepatic encephalopathy prevention.", "D": "High dose corticosteroids for suspected alcoholic hepatitis.", "E": "Therapeutic dose enoxaparin for suspected portal vein thrombosis."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Octreotide infusion and empiric intravenous antibiotics): In suspected variceal hemorrhage, octreotide should be started immediately to reduce splanchnic blood flow and portal pressure, and prophylactic antibiotics such as ceftriaxone should be given, since both reduce rebleeding and mortality in cirrhotic patients. Diuretics address ascites, not acute bleeding. Rifaximin and lactulose treat encephalopathy, not the acute hemorrhage. Anticoagulation would be dangerous and is not indicated in this acute bleeding scenario."}, {"id": 8, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A patient with suspected peptic ulcer bleeding is awaiting upper endoscopy. What is the primary benefit of starting an intravenous proton pump inhibitor infusion before the procedure?", "options": {"A": "It eliminates the need for endoscopic therapy in all cases.", "B": "It directly reduces portal venous pressure in the splanchnic circulation.", "C": "It reduces the proportion of ulcers with high risk stigmata seen at endoscopy.", "D": "It prevents post-polypectomy bleeding during a concurrent procedure.", "E": "It eradicates Helicobacter pylori prior to biopsy confirmation."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It reduces the proportion of ulcers with high risk stigmata seen at endoscopy): Preprocedural intravenous PPI therapy raises gastric pH and can downstage ulcer stigmata, reducing the proportion of patients found to have high risk features at the time of endoscopy, which may reduce the need for endoscopic hemostatic therapy. It does not eliminate the need for endoscopy itself in patients with ongoing bleeding. It has no effect on portal pressure, which relates to variceal bleeding management instead. It plays no role in H. pylori eradication, which requires separate antibiotic therapy."}, {"id": 11, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "Which statement correctly compares the perforation risk of diagnostic upper endoscopy to diagnostic colonoscopy?", "options": {"A": "Diagnostic upper endoscopy carries a lower perforation risk than diagnostic colonoscopy.", "B": "Diagnostic upper endoscopy carries a substantially higher perforation risk than colonoscopy.", "C": "Both procedures carry an identical perforation risk near 1 in 500.", "D": "Diagnostic colonoscopy has essentially no perforation risk without polypectomy.", "E": "Perforation risk cannot be estimated for either diagnostic procedure."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Diagnostic upper endoscopy carries a lower perforation risk than diagnostic colonoscopy): Diagnostic upper endoscopy has a perforation risk under 1 in 10,000, which is substantially lower than the roughly 1 in 1,000 to 3,000 risk of diagnostic colonoscopy. The reverse relationship described in option B is incorrect. The two procedures do not share an identical risk, and colonoscopy risk, while lower for diagnostic exams than therapeutic ones, is not zero. Perforation risk for both procedures has been well characterized in large studies."}, {"id": 16, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "Which patient factor is most associated with an increased risk of post-ERCP pancreatitis?", "options": {"A": "Suspected sphincter of Oddi dysfunction with difficult cannulation.", "B": "Prior cholecystectomy performed years earlier.", "C": "Advanced age above 80 years alone.", "D": "History of well controlled type 2 diabetes.", "E": "Presence of a straightforward common bile duct stone."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Suspected sphincter of Oddi dysfunction with difficult cannulation): Suspected sphincter of Oddi dysfunction combined with difficult cannulation is one of the strongest recognized risk factors for post-ERCP pancreatitis. A remote prior cholecystectomy does not meaningfully raise procedural risk. Advanced age alone and well controlled diabetes are not major independent risk factors for this complication. A straightforward stone extraction without difficult cannulation carries comparatively lower risk than the scenario described."}, {"id": 28, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A hospitalized patient with acute severe ulcerative colitis is being evaluated for possible toxic megacolon. Which endoscopic approach is appropriate if endoscopic assessment is required?", "options": {"A": "Full colonoscopy with standard bowel preparation and insufflation.", "B": "ERCP to assess for a coexisting biliary process.", "C": "Limited unprepped flexible sigmoidoscopy with minimal insufflation.", "D": "Capsule endoscopy to evaluate the entire colon safely.", "E": "Deferring any endoscopic evaluation indefinitely regardless of clinical need."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Limited unprepped flexible sigmoidoscopy with minimal insufflation): In suspected toxic megacolon, a limited unprepped flexible sigmoidoscopy with minimal air insufflation is preferred if endoscopic assessment is truly needed, since full colonoscopy with standard insufflation and preparation raises the risk of perforation in a friable, distended colon. ERCP addresses a different anatomic system entirely. Capsule endoscopy is contraindicated in this setting due to obstruction and perforation risk. Complete deferral may not be appropriate if a specific finding would change management."}, {"id": 32, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A patient has a history of an interval colon cancer diagnosed after a colonoscopy that reportedly missed a lesion. For his next surveillance colonoscopy, which approach may improve adenoma detection?", "options": {"A": "Standard forward viewing colonoscopy with a shorter withdrawal time.", "B": "Cap assisted colonoscopy or a wide angle viewing scope.", "C": "Avoiding colonoscopy entirely in favor of CT colonography only.", "D": "Performing the procedure without any bowel preparation.", "E": "Using a pediatric endoscope instead of a standard adult colonoscope."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cap assisted colonoscopy or a wide angle viewing scope): Cap assisted colonoscopy or a wide angle viewing scope can improve mucosal visualization behind haustral folds and flexures, which may improve detection in a patient with a history of a missed lesion. A shorter withdrawal time would likely worsen detection rather than help. CT colonography cannot provide biopsy or resection and is not an adequate substitute in this high risk history. Omitting bowel preparation would significantly reduce visualization and detection rather than improve it."}, {"id": 33, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A subepithelial lesion is identified in the gastric wall on upper endoscopy. Which tool is best suited to obtain a tissue diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Standard cold biopsy forceps through the working channel.", "B": "Capsule endoscopy to characterize the lesion further.", "C": "Echoendoscope with fine needle aspiration or biopsy under real time guidance.", "D": "Argon plasma coagulation applied directly to the lesion surface.", "E": "Chromoendoscopy with dye spraying alone."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Echoendoscope with fine needle aspiration or biopsy under real time guidance): An echoendoscope allows real time ultrasound guided fine needle aspiration or biopsy of a subepithelial lesion, which improves diagnostic yield compared with blind superficial sampling since these lesions often arise below the mucosa. Standard cold forceps typically only sample superficial mucosa and often miss the lesion itself. Capsule endoscopy and chromoendoscopy provide visualization but not tissue diagnosis. Argon plasma coagulation is a therapeutic tool, not a diagnostic sampling technique."}, {"id": 43, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A colonoscopy reveals a single 12mm tubular adenoma with villous features and no high grade dysplasia, completely resected en bloc. What surveillance interval is generally recommended?", "options": {"A": "1 year.", "B": "10 years.", "C": "3 years.", "D": "20 years.", "E": "No surveillance interval change from average risk screening."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (3 years): An adenoma with villous features, even without high grade dysplasia, typically shortens the surveillance interval to 3 years given the associated increased risk of future advanced neoplasia. A 1 year interval is generally reserved for more concerning findings such as incomplete resection or very high risk features. A 10 or 20 year interval reflects average risk screening, which does not apply once advanced histologic features are identified."}, {"id": 44, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A colonoscopy identifies six tubular adenomas throughout the colon, all completely resected, along with a separate 14mm sessile polyp removed piecemeal. What is the most appropriate surveillance approach?", "options": {"A": "Standard 10 year interval since all lesions were removed.", "B": "No surveillance needed if pathology shows only tubular adenomas.", "C": "Immediate surgical colectomy regardless of pathology.", "D": "A shortened surveillance interval, often 3 years or less, with earlier follow up at the piecemeal resection site.", "E": "A single follow up in 15 years given complete removal."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A shortened surveillance interval, often 3 years or less, with earlier follow up at the piecemeal resection site): Five or more adenomas, along with a large polyp removed piecemeal, generally requires a shortened surveillance interval, often 3 years or less, and the piecemeal resection site specifically needs an earlier repeat examination to confirm complete removal. A standard 10 year interval significantly underestimates this patient's risk profile. Surgical colectomy is not warranted for benign adenomatous polyps managed successfully with endoscopic resection. A single follow up at 15 years falls far outside appropriate guidance for this many adenomas and a piecemeal resection."}, {"id": 47, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A patient on warfarin for atrial fibrillation is scheduled for a high risk endoscopic procedure. How should warfarin be managed beforehand?", "options": {"A": "Continue warfarin without any interruption or monitoring.", "B": "Hold warfarin approximately 5 days before the procedure and check INR the day of the procedure.", "C": "Stop warfarin only on the morning of the procedure.", "D": "Double the warfarin dose the week before to allow for a planned break afterward.", "E": "Switch immediately to a direct oral anticoagulant without any transition plan."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hold warfarin approximately 5 days before the procedure and check INR the day of the procedure): For high risk procedures, warfarin is typically held approximately 5 days beforehand, with an INR checked on the day of the procedure to confirm adequate normalization of coagulation status. Continuing warfarin without interruption raises unacceptable bleeding risk for a high risk procedure. Stopping only on the morning of the procedure does not allow adequate time for the anticoagulant effect to resolve given warfarin's pharmacokinetics. Doubling the dose beforehand or switching anticoagulants without a defined plan are not standard, safe approaches."}, {"id": 49, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A patient taking a direct oral anticoagulant with normal renal function is scheduled for a low bleeding risk endoscopic procedure. What is the general approach to perioperative management?", "options": {"A": "Hold the medication for a full 7 days regardless of procedure risk.", "B": "Continue the medication without any interruption regardless of procedure type.", "C": "Bridge with unfractionated heparin during the interruption period.", "D": "Hold the medication for a shorter, defined interval, generally 1 to 2 days.", "E": "Switch to warfarin temporarily before the procedure."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold the medication for a shorter, defined interval, generally 1 to 2 days): Direct oral anticoagulants are typically held for a shorter, defined interval based on the specific drug and renal function, generally around 1 to 2 days for low risk procedures, reflecting their relatively rapid onset and offset compared with warfarin. Holding for a full 7 days is excessive for a low risk procedure with normal renal function. Continuing without any interruption is not the standard approach even for lower risk procedures on DOAC therapy. Bridging with heparin or switching to warfarin are unnecessary and impractical given the pharmacokinetics of these newer agents."}, {"id": 50, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A patient taking aspirin for secondary prevention after a prior myocardial infarction is scheduled for screening colonoscopy with anticipated polypectomy. What is the most appropriate approach to aspirin management?", "options": {"A": "Stop aspirin 7 days before the procedure to reduce bleeding risk.", "B": "Stop aspirin only on the morning of the procedure.", "C": "Replace aspirin with a direct oral anticoagulant before the procedure.", "D": "Double the aspirin dose to compensate for the procedure related stress.", "E": "Continue aspirin through the procedure including polypectomy."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Continue aspirin through the procedure including polypectomy): Aspirin used for secondary cardiovascular prevention is generally continued through endoscopic procedures, including polypectomy, since the cardiovascular risk of stopping it typically outweighs the modest reduction in bleeding risk achieved by interruption. Stopping it 7 days before or even the morning of the procedure introduces unnecessary cardiovascular risk without strong evidence of meaningful bleeding risk reduction. Replacing it with an anticoagulant or increasing the dose are not appropriate or evidence based adjustments for this clinical scenario."}, {"id": 55, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A patient taking a GLP-1 receptor agonist for type 2 diabetes reports nausea and bloating on the morning of a scheduled elective upper endoscopy. What is the most appropriate consideration regarding sedation planning?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with standard fasting instructions with no additional consideration.", "B": "Cancel the procedure permanently since GLP-1 agonists are an absolute contraindication.", "C": "Increase the GLP-1 agonist dose to improve glycemic control before sedation.", "D": "Proceed but omit all sedation regardless of patient preference.", "E": "Consider delayed gastric emptying and adjust fasting time or treat as a full stomach case."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Consider delayed gastric emptying and adjust fasting time or treat as a full stomach case): GLP-1 receptor agonists delay gastric emptying and increase the risk of retained gastric contents and aspiration during sedation, so in a symptomatic patient it is appropriate to consider a longer fasting interval or manage the case with precautions similar to a full stomach patient. Proceeding with standard fasting instructions ignores this real risk, particularly given the patient's active symptoms. These medications are not an absolute contraindication requiring permanent cancellation, and increasing the dose has no role in acute periprocedural planning. Omitting sedation entirely is an extreme step not required simply because of GLP-1 agonist use."}, {"id": 59, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A patient becomes excessively somnolent and develops respiratory depression after receiving a benzodiazepine and an opioid during endoscopic sedation. Which reversal agents correspond to each medication class?", "options": {"A": "Naloxone for the benzodiazepine and flumazenil for the opioid.", "B": "Flumazenil for both medications given simultaneously.", "C": "Naloxone for both medications given simultaneously.", "D": "Flumazenil for the benzodiazepine and naloxone for the opioid.", "E": "No reversal agent exists for either medication class."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Flumazenil for the benzodiazepine and naloxone for the opioid): Flumazenil is the specific reversal agent for benzodiazepines, while naloxone reverses opioid effects, and both should be immediately available during procedures using these medication classes. The reversed pairing in the first option is incorrect. Using only one agent for both medications would fail to address the other drug class's contribution to respiratory depression. Reversal agents do exist for both of these commonly used sedation medication classes."}, {"id": 63, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "For which clinical scenario is percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy tube placement generally indicated?", "options": {"A": "Anticipated need for enteral nutrition lasting less than one week.", "B": "Complete bowel obstruction with a nonfunctional gastrointestinal tract.", "C": "Anticipated need for enteral nutrition expected to last more than 4 to 6 weeks with a functional GI tract.", "D": "Active severe pancreatitis requiring bowel rest.", "E": "Short term postoperative ileus expected to resolve within days."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Anticipated need for enteral nutrition expected to last more than 4 to 6 weeks with a functional GI tract): PEG tube placement is indicated when long term enteral nutrition is anticipated, generally more than 4 to 6 weeks, in a patient with a functional gastrointestinal tract, such as after a stroke causing persistent dysphagia. A need lasting less than one week is better managed with a temporary nasogastric tube rather than a permanent percutaneous device. Complete bowel obstruction or a nonfunctional GI tract are contraindications, since enteral feeding requires a functioning tract distal to the tube. Short term issues expected to resolve within days do not warrant a permanent access device."}, {"id": 64, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "During PEG tube placement using the standard pull technique, which finding confirms safe percutaneous access and helps avoid injury to interposed structures such as the colon or liver?", "options": {"A": "Absence of any resistance when advancing the needle blindly.", "B": "A normal preprocedural complete blood count.", "C": "Successful passage of a nasogastric tube the night before.", "D": "Transillumination of the abdominal wall with a clear finger indentation sign.", "E": "A normal abdominal X-ray obtained immediately before the procedure."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Transillumination of the abdominal wall with a clear finger indentation sign): Transillumination of the abdominal wall combined with a clear finger indentation sign confirms that the stomach is directly apposed to the abdominal wall without an interposed structure like the colon or liver, allowing safe needle placement. Advancing the needle blindly without these confirmatory steps risks injury to interposed organs. A normal blood count, prior nasogastric tube placement, and a preprocedural X-ray do not confirm the specific anatomic relationship needed for safe percutaneous access."}, {"id": 68, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "Which elements must be included for informed consent to be considered adequate before an endoscopic procedure?", "options": {"A": "A signed consent form alone, regardless of patient understanding.", "B": "Only a verbal mention of the procedure name without further detail.", "C": "The indication, procedure details, reasonable alternatives, and material risks specific to the patient.", "D": "Consent obtained after sedation has already been administered.", "E": "A generic risk disclosure identical for every patient regardless of individual factors."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The indication, procedure details, reasonable alternatives, and material risks specific to the patient): Adequate informed consent requires discussion of the indication for the procedure, what the procedure involves, reasonable alternatives, and material risks specific to that particular patient, including sedation related risks. A signed form alone, without a genuine conversation demonstrating patient understanding, does not fulfill this requirement. Consent must be obtained before sedation is given, not afterward, since sedation impairs decision making capacity. Risk disclosure should be tailored to the individual patient rather than delivered as an identical generic script for everyone."}, {"id": 70, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A patient with early cognitive impairment can clearly explain simple aspects of an upcoming colonoscopy, including why it is being done, but struggles to reason through the more complex risks and benefits of a proposed advanced therapeutic intervention discovered during the exam. How should capacity be assessed in this situation?", "options": {"A": "The patient must be judged globally either fully capacitated or fully incapacitated.", "B": "A single capacity determination made years ago should apply to all future decisions.", "C": "Family consent should override the patient's own input regardless of ability.", "D": "Capacity assessment is unnecessary once a diagnosis of cognitive impairment exists.", "E": "Capacity should be assessed as decision specific, recognizing capacity may differ between the simpler and more complex decisions."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Capacity should be assessed as decision specific, recognizing capacity may differ between the simpler and more complex decisions): Capacity is decision specific rather than a single global determination, meaning a patient can retain capacity for a simpler decision while lacking it for a more complex one, so each decision should be assessed on its own terms. Treating capacity as strictly all or nothing ignores this important nuance. A prior capacity determination does not automatically apply to a new and different decision. A diagnosis of cognitive impairment alone does not eliminate the need for an individualized capacity assessment, and family input should not simply override a patient who retains capacity for the specific decision at hand."}, {"id": 71, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A patient arrives unconscious with active massive GI hemorrhage and no available surrogate decision maker or advance directive. What is the appropriate approach to consent for emergency endoscopy?", "options": {"A": "Proceed under the principle of implied consent given the emergency and risk to life.", "B": "Delay the procedure indefinitely until a surrogate can be located.", "C": "Obtain a court order before any intervention can occur.", "D": "Refuse to treat until the patient regains consciousness to consent personally.", "E": "Consent is not necessary or relevant in any part of the process."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Proceed under the principle of implied consent given the emergency and risk to life): In a genuine emergency where a patient lacks capacity, has no available surrogate, and delay would risk life or limb, emergency procedures can proceed under the principle of implied consent. Delaying indefinitely until a surrogate is located could allow a life threatening hemorrhage to continue with catastrophic consequences. Obtaining a court order is not feasible or appropriate in the time frame of an acute life threatening emergency. While formal consent processes matter greatly in nonemergency situations, the ethical and legal framework specifically allows life saving intervention without delay in this scenario."}, {"id": 73, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A sharp object, such as an open safety pin, is lodged in the esophagus on imaging. What is the appropriate approach to timing of removal?", "options": {"A": "Elective removal scheduled for several weeks later.", "B": "Observation only, since sharp objects usually pass without issue.", "C": "Urgent removal given the risk of perforation from a sharp object.", "D": "Removal only if the object migrates into the stomach first.", "E": "No specific urgency compared with a blunt object of similar size."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Urgent removal given the risk of perforation from a sharp object): Sharp objects lodged in the esophagus require urgent removal given their meaningfully increased risk of perforation and other significant tissue injury compared with blunt objects. Elective removal weeks later or simple observation both allow unacceptable time for potential perforation to occur. Waiting for spontaneous migration into the stomach is inappropriate given the ongoing risk while the object remains in the esophagus. Sharp objects carry meaningfully greater urgency than a similarly sized blunt object, contrary to the claim of no difference."}, {"id": 75, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A patient is diagnosed with a small, contained esophageal perforation identified early after the causative endoscopy, without significant mediastinal contamination. How does management typically differ from a larger, later presenting perforation with mediastinal contamination?", "options": {"A": "Both scenarios are always managed identically with immediate surgery.", "B": "Neither scenario requires any intervention beyond observation.", "C": "The larger, later perforation is always managed less aggressively than the smaller one.", "D": "Only antibiotics are needed in both scenarios regardless of severity.", "E": "The small, early, contained perforation may be managed with endoscopic clip or stent placement, while the larger, later presentation often requires surgical repair."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The small, early, contained perforation may be managed with endoscopic clip or stent placement, while the larger, later presentation often requires surgical repair): Management of esophageal perforation depends heavily on size, location, and time to diagnosis, with small, early, contained perforations often amenable to endoscopic clip or stent placement, while larger or later presenting perforations with mediastinal contamination often require surgical repair. Treating both scenarios identically with immediate surgery ignores this important clinical distinction and would be unnecessarily aggressive for a small, early, contained injury. Observation alone is inadequate for either scenario given the seriousness of perforation. Antibiotics play a supportive role in both cases but are not sufficient as the sole treatment for either presentation."}, {"id": 78, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A patient presents with progressive abdominal distension and obstipation, and imaging suggests sigmoid volvulus. There are no signs of peritonitis or bowel ischemia. What is the first line treatment?", "options": {"A": "Immediate emergency laparotomy without any endoscopic attempt.", "B": "Conservative management with bowel rest alone and no intervention.", "C": "Endoscopic detorsion with a flexible sigmoidoscope, leaving a rectal tube afterward.", "D": "Colonic stent placement as the definitive first line therapy.", "E": "High volume enema administered under fluoroscopic guidance as first line."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Endoscopic detorsion with a flexible sigmoidoscope, leaving a rectal tube afterward): In the absence of peritonitis or evidence of bowel ischemia, endoscopic detorsion using a flexible sigmoidoscope, followed by placement of a rectal tube to help maintain decompression, is the first line treatment for sigmoid volvulus. Proceeding immediately to laparotomy skips a safer, effective initial approach appropriate for this presentation. Conservative management alone does not address the mechanical obstruction causing the volvulus. Colonic stenting and enemas are not the accepted first line approach for acute sigmoid volvulus."}, {"id": 79, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A patient with suspected sigmoid volvulus is found on examination to have peritoneal signs and clinical concern for bowel ischemia. What is the appropriate management approach?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with endoscopic detorsion as in an uncomplicated case.", "B": "Attempt endoscopic detorsion first, and only pursue surgery if that fails.", "C": "Discharge home with outpatient follow up given stable vital signs.", "D": "Emergency surgical evaluation, avoiding endoscopic detorsion.", "E": "Perform a barium enema to confirm the diagnosis before any intervention."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Emergency surgical evaluation, avoiding endoscopic detorsion): Peritoneal signs or suspected ischemia are contraindications to endoscopic detorsion and instead mandate emergency surgical evaluation, since attempting endoscopic reduction of potentially nonviable bowel risks perforation and worsens outcomes. Proceeding with detorsion as though this were an uncomplicated case ignores these critical warning signs. Attempting endoscopic reduction first before considering surgery unnecessarily delays definitive treatment of likely ischemic or nonviable bowel. Discharge home is entirely inappropriate given the acute abdominal findings described, and a barium enema is not indicated and could further complicate a compromised bowel segment."}, {"id": 87, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A cirrhotic patient with acute variceal bleeding receives prophylactic ceftriaxone during the acute hospitalization even though she has no signs of active infection. What is the rationale for this practice?", "options": {"A": "It has no proven benefit and is given only out of historical habit.", "B": "It reduces mortality and rebleeding risk in cirrhotic patients with GI bleeding.", "C": "It is given solely to treat presumed spontaneous bacterial peritonitis that is always present.", "D": "It replaces the need for octreotide infusion entirely.", "E": "It is only necessary if the patient already has documented ascites."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It reduces mortality and rebleeding risk in cirrhotic patients with GI bleeding): Prophylactic antibiotics such as ceftriaxone have been shown to reduce mortality and rebleeding risk in cirrhotic patients presenting with GI bleeding, regardless of whether infection is clinically apparent at presentation, and this benefit holds even in patients without ascites. This is an evidence based practice with proven benefit, not simply historical habit. It is not solely a treatment for spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, which may or may not be present, and it does not replace the separate role of octreotide in reducing portal pressure. The benefit is not restricted only to patients with documented ascites."}, {"id": 94, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A patient develops abdominal pain and low grade fever two days after polypectomy. Imaging is obtained specifically to differentiate post-polypectomy syndrome from a true perforation. What finding would favor post-polypectomy syndrome over perforation?", "options": {"A": "Presence of extraluminal free air on imaging.", "B": "Diffuse peritonitis on physical examination.", "C": "Evidence of a large abscess collection requiring drainage.", "D": "Absence of free air with localized wall thickening at the resection site.", "E": "Frank fecal peritonitis identified at surgery."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Absence of free air with localized wall thickening at the resection site): The absence of free intraperitoneal air, along with localized wall thickening at the resection site, favors post-polypectomy syndrome, which reflects a transmural thermal injury rather than a true perforation. The presence of free air, diffuse peritonitis, a large abscess, or frank fecal peritonitis would all instead suggest a true perforation requiring more aggressive management, not the more benign syndrome described in this question."}, {"id": 99, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "Which patient characteristic during upper endoscopy most increases the risk of aspiration requiring extra airway precautions?", "options": {"A": "A well controlled outpatient with mild intermittent heartburn.", "B": "A patient who fasted appropriately before an elective procedure.", "C": "A patient with a remote, fully resolved episode of aspiration pneumonia.", "D": "Altered mental status in the setting of active upper GI bleeding.", "E": "A patient taking a daily antacid for occasional dyspepsia."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Altered mental status in the setting of active upper GI bleeding): Altered mental status combined with active upper GI bleeding significantly increases aspiration risk during upper endoscopy, since airway protective reflexes may be impaired while blood or clot is present, warranting extra airway precautions. A well controlled outpatient with mild heartburn or a patient who appropriately fasted before an elective procedure both carry comparatively low aspiration risk. A remote, fully resolved aspiration event or routine antacid use do not by themselves indicate elevated current procedural aspiration risk."}, {"id": 100, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A patient undergoing a routine, technically uncomplicated screening colonoscopy under moderate sedation suddenly develops bradycardia and hypoxemia, ultimately requiring resuscitation, despite no evidence of bleeding or perforation. What does this scenario best illustrate about the overall risk profile of endoscopy?", "options": {"A": "Mechanical complications like perforation are more dangerous than sedation related events.", "B": "This type of event is essentially impossible during a technically uncomplicated procedure.", "C": "Only therapeutic procedures carry meaningful cardiopulmonary risk.", "D": "Bleeding complications are the most common cause of death in this setting.", "E": "Cardiopulmonary events related to sedation represent the leading cause of endoscopy related mortality."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Cardiopulmonary events related to sedation represent the leading cause of endoscopy related mortality): This scenario, occurring during a technically uncomplicated procedure without bleeding or perforation, illustrates that cardiopulmonary events related to sedation are the leading cause of endoscopy related mortality overall, deserving as much attention as technical endoscopic skill. This directly contradicts the idea that mechanical complications are more dangerous overall. The scenario itself demonstrates that such events are not impossible even during a technically simple, diagnostic procedure. Bleeding complications, while significant, are not the most common cause of death in this specific context."}, {"id": 107, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "Why do elderly patients generally require more conservative sedation dosing compared with younger adults undergoing the same endoscopic procedure?", "options": {"A": "Because weight based dosing formulas already fully correct for age related changes.", "B": "Because of reduced drug clearance and diminished cardiac reserve with age.", "C": "Because elderly patients experience less anxiety and therefore need less medication.", "D": "Because elderly patients metabolize sedatives faster than younger adults.", "E": "Because sedation risk does not actually differ meaningfully by age."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Because of reduced drug clearance and diminished cardiac reserve with age): Elderly patients generally have reduced drug clearance and diminished cardiac reserve compared with younger adults, which is why more conservative sedation dosing and closer monitoring are appropriate in this population. Weight based dosing alone does not fully correct for these underlying age related physiologic changes. Reduced anxiety is not the reason for dose adjustment, and in fact elderly patients often metabolize sedatives more slowly, not faster, than younger adults. Sedation risk does meaningfully differ by age given these physiologic differences, contrary to the claim that it does not."}, {"id": 110, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A team is managing a patient with acute severe ulcerative colitis who develops worsening distension and early signs concerning for toxic megacolon. They must decide whether any endoscopic evaluation should proceed at all. What should weigh most heavily in this decision?", "options": {"A": "Endoscopy should always proceed regardless of clinical severity for diagnostic completeness.", "B": "Endoscopy should never be performed once toxic megacolon is even suspected.", "C": "Surgical consultation should be deferred until endoscopic findings are available.", "D": "Standard bowel preparation should be given to optimize visualization before any exam.", "E": "The decision should balance the diagnostic value of a limited exam against the elevated perforation risk from insufflation in this setting."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The decision should balance the diagnostic value of a limited exam against the elevated perforation risk from insufflation in this setting): The key decision in this scenario involves weighing whether the diagnostic information a limited, minimally insufflated exam would provide is worth the elevated perforation risk inherent to endoscopy in a friable, distended colon, rather than following an absolute rule in either direction. Proceeding with a full standard exam regardless of severity ignores real safety concerns. Completely avoiding any endoscopic evaluation the moment toxic megacolon is suspected may not always be correct if a specific finding would meaningfully change management, so an absolute prohibition is also not quite right. Surgical consultation should run in parallel with, not be delayed until after, any endoscopic evaluation, and standard bowel preparation is specifically avoided in this emergent, high risk setting."}, {"id": 130, "domain": "procedures_periprocedural", "question": "A patient with mild cognitive impairment can clearly articulate the reason for an upcoming routine screening colonoscopy and agrees to proceed, but during the visit becomes confused when asked to weigh the risks and benefits of a newly discovered, complex therapeutic option requiring a separate specialized procedure. How should the team proceed regarding consent for each decision?", "options": {"A": "Assume the patient lacks capacity for both decisions equally based on the diagnosis alone.", "B": "Assume the patient retains full capacity for both decisions since consent was given for the colonoscopy.", "C": "Rely entirely on family consent for both decisions without engaging the patient at all.", "D": "Defer any decision making until formal guardianship is established.", "E": "Assess capacity separately for each decision, since capacity is decision specific and the patient may retain it for the simpler decision."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Assess capacity separately for each decision, since capacity is decision specific and the patient may retain it for the simpler decision): Because capacity is decision specific rather than a single global determination, the team should separately assess the patient's capacity for the simpler screening colonoscopy decision, which he clearly demonstrated understanding of, and for the more complex therapeutic decision, where confusion was apparent, rather than assuming a uniform level of capacity across both. Assuming a lack of capacity for both decisions based on the diagnosis alone ignores the patient's demonstrated understanding of the simpler decision. Assuming full capacity for the complex decision simply because he consented to the simpler one overlooks the specific difficulty he showed with the more complex reasoning task. Bypassing the patient entirely in favor of family consent, or delaying all decisions pending formal guardianship, are both premature steps that skip the more appropriate immediate step of a decision specific capacity assessment."}, {"id": 131, "domain": "esophageal_disorders", "question": "A 58-year-old man with 15 years of heartburn undergoes surveillance endoscopy. The image shows salmon-colored tongues of mucosa extending above the gastroesophageal junction into pale squamous mucosa. Biopsies are pending. No additional past medical history, medication use, or family history is reported that would help explain these findings. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Eosinophilic esophagitis with mucosal furrowing as the most likely explanation for these clinical findings.", "B": "Barrett esophagus with intestinal metaplasia given the constellation of symptoms and findings described here.", "C": "Esophageal candidiasis with mucosal plaques as the most likely explanation for these clinical findings.", "D": "Reflux esophagitis without metaplastic change as the most likely explanation for these clinical findings.", "E": "Squamous cell carcinoma of the distal esophagus based on the endoscopic findings described above."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Barrett esophagus with intestinal metaplasia given the constellation of symptoms and findings described here): The salmon-colored columnar mucosa extending as tongues above the Z-line in a patient with long-standing GERD is the classic appearance of Barrett esophagus. Confirmation requires biopsy showing intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells, since endoscopic appearance alone is not diagnostic. Eosinophilic esophagitis shows rings and furrows rather than salmon-colored tissue, and candidiasis shows white plaques rather than columnar mucosal color change. Plain reflux esophagitis causes erosions or erythema, not this specific columnar appearance.", "image": "question_images/q_131.jpg"}, {"id": 140, "domain": "esophageal_disorders", "question": "A 35-year-old man reports reflux symptoms only in the two hours after eating spicy or fatty meals, without nocturnal symptoms or alarm features. Endoscopy is not yet performed. No additional past medical history, medication use, or family history is reported that would help explain these findings. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Immediate ambulatory pH monitoring as the most appropriate next step in this clinical scenario.", "B": "Urgent esophagogastroduodenoscopy as the best management strategy given these clinical circumstances.", "C": "Empiric trial of proton pump inhibitor therapy as the most appropriate management approach here.", "D": "Esophageal manometry before any treatment as the most appropriate next step in this clinical scenario.", "E": "CT scan of the chest and abdomen as the safest next step in care."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Empiric trial of proton pump inhibitor therapy as the most appropriate management approach here): A young patient with typical postprandial reflux symptoms and no alarm features is appropriately managed with an empiric trial of proton pump inhibitor therapy before pursuing further testing, since this approach is both cost effective and supported by guidelines. pH monitoring and manometry are reserved for cases with an unclear diagnosis or symptoms refractory to empiric therapy. Endoscopy is reserved for alarm features or refractory symptoms, and a CT scan has no role in routine reflux evaluation."}, {"id": 143, "domain": "esophageal_disorders", "question": "A 55-year-old man with Barrett esophagus and confirmed high grade dysplasia limited to the mucosa is evaluated for treatment. No additional past medical history, medication use, or family history is reported that would help explain these findings. Which approach has largely replaced esophagectomy for this stage of disease?", "options": {"A": "Immediate systemic chemotherapy as the most appropriate next step in this clinical scenario.", "B": "Endoscopic mucosal resection combined with ablation as the best management strategy given these clinical circumstances.", "C": "Total esophagectomy as first-line therapy in all cases.", "D": "Observation with repeat biopsy in one year as the most appropriate management approach here.", "E": "Long term high dose proton pump inhibitor therapy alone."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Endoscopic mucosal resection combined with ablation as the best management strategy given these clinical circumstances): Endoscopic mucosal resection of visible lesions combined with ablation of remaining Barrett tissue has become the preferred approach for high grade dysplasia and mucosal cancer confined to the epithelium, offering outcomes comparable to surgery with far less morbidity. Esophagectomy is now reserved for lesions with deeper invasion or when endoscopic therapy fails. Chemotherapy alone is not curative for localized dysplastic disease, and simple observation or medical therapy alone would allow progression to invasive cancer."}, {"id": 145, "domain": "esophageal_disorders", "question": "A 27-year-old man with a history of asthma and seasonal allergies has recurrent dysphagia to solids. Distal and proximal esophageal biopsies show 25 eosinophils per high power field in both locations, after a two month trial of high dose proton pump inhibitor therapy showed no improvement. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "PPI-responsive esophageal eosinophilia as the most likely explanation for these clinical findings.", "B": "Reflux esophagitis with reactive eosinophilia given the constellation of symptoms and findings described here.", "C": "Eosinophilic esophagitis as the most likely explanation for these clinical findings.", "D": "Eosinophilic gastroenteritis limited to the stomach as the most likely explanation for these clinical findings.", "E": "Drug hypersensitivity esophagitis as the most likely explanation for these clinical findings."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Eosinophilic esophagitis as the most likely explanation for these clinical findings): Persistent esophageal eosinophilia at a diagnostic threshold in both the proximal and distal esophagus, despite an adequate trial of proton pump inhibitor therapy, in a patient with an atopic history and dysphagia, meets criteria for eosinophilic esophagitis. PPI-responsive esophageal eosinophilia is excluded by definition because the eosinophilia persisted despite the PPI trial. Reflux related eosinophilia typically clears with acid suppression and is usually confined to the distal esophagus, and eosinophilic gastroenteritis would show gastric rather than isolated esophageal involvement."}, {"id": 146, "domain": "esophageal_disorders", "question": "A 32-year-old woman with eosinophilic esophagitis and a mid-esophageal stricture needs endoscopic dilation. No additional past medical history, medication use, or family history is reported that would help explain these findings. What is the most important safety consideration during this procedure?", "options": {"A": "Perform gradual, small-increment dilation to reduce perforation risk.", "B": "Perform a single large dilation to minimize the number of procedures.", "C": "Avoid biopsies at the time of dilation to prevent bleeding.", "D": "Dilate only the widest portion of the esophagus.", "E": "Use dilation as first-line therapy before any medical treatment."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Perform gradual, small-increment dilation to reduce perforation risk): The esophagus in eosinophilic esophagitis is fibrotic and fragile, so gradual dilation using small incremental increases in diameter across sessions reduces the risk of deep mucosal tearing or perforation compared with aggressive single-session dilation. Performing one large dilation increases perforation risk substantially in this fragile tissue. Biopsies are generally safe to obtain and are not withheld solely because dilation is planned, and dilation is typically reserved for strictures that persist despite medical and dietary therapy, not used as a first step."}, {"id": 149, "domain": "esophageal_disorders", "question": "A 33-year-old woman with dysphagia undergoes endoscopy showing longitudinal furrows and whitish exudates in the esophagus. Biopsy shows 18 eosinophils per high power field in the distal esophagus only, with normal proximal biopsies. Which additional step is needed before diagnosing eosinophilic esophagitis?", "options": {"A": "No further workup is needed since biopsy already confirms the diagnosis.", "B": "Excluding other causes of esophageal eosinophilia such as reflux.", "C": "Immediate initiation of biologic therapy as the most appropriate next step in this clinical scenario.", "D": "Referral for esophagectomy as the best management strategy given these clinical circumstances.", "E": "Repeat endoscopy in five years regardless of symptoms."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Excluding other causes of esophageal eosinophilia such as reflux): Current diagnostic criteria for eosinophilic esophagitis require excluding other causes of esophageal eosinophilia, most notably reflux disease, since eosinophil counts alone are not fully specific to EoE. Isolated distal eosinophilia in particular overlaps with reflux-related patterns, so this step cannot be skipped even with exudates and furrows on endoscopy. Jumping straight to biologic therapy or esophagectomy is inappropriate before confirming the diagnosis, and deferring re-evaluation for five years would leave a symptomatic patient without a clear diagnosis or treatment plan."}, {"id": 153, "domain": "esophageal_disorders", "question": "A 40-year-old woman with iron deficiency anemia, glossitis, and dysphagia is found to have a thin web in the cervical esophagus on barium study. No additional past medical history, medication use, or family history is reported that would help explain these findings. Which additional risk does this triad carry?", "options": {"A": "Increased risk of Barrett esophagus based on the clinical details provided in this vignette.", "B": "Increased risk of esophageal candidiasis given all of the information presented in this case.", "C": "Increased risk of achalasia based on the clinical details provided in this vignette.", "D": "Increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus.", "E": "Increased risk of eosinophilic esophagitis based on the clinical details provided in this vignette."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus): This presentation describes Plummer-Vinson syndrome, the triad of iron deficiency anemia, glossitis, and cervical esophageal webs, which carries a well documented increased long term risk of squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus and hypopharynx. Barrett esophagus is linked to chronic reflux and columnar metaplasia, an unrelated mechanism. Candidiasis relates to immunosuppression, achalasia to loss of myenteric neurons, and eosinophilic esophagitis to atopic inflammation, none of which are the specific long term risk associated with this triad."}, {"id": 163, "domain": "esophageal_disorders", "question": "A 62-year-old man reports recurrent aspiration pneumonia and is found to have a Zenker diverticulum contributing to his dysphagia. No additional past medical history, medication use, or family history is reported that would help explain these findings. Which structural process is believed to underlie the formation of this pouch?", "options": {"A": "Chronic acid reflux causing distal esophageal fibrosis as the mechanism most consistent with these findings.", "B": "Eosinophilic infiltration of the pharyngeal wall as the process best explaining this patient specific presentation.", "C": "Extrinsic compression from an enlarged thyroid gland as the process that best fits this case.", "D": "Increased hypopharyngeal pressure against a poorly relaxing cricopharyngeus muscle.", "E": "Autoimmune destruction of the myenteric plexus as the mechanism that ties together these clinical observations."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Increased hypopharyngeal pressure against a poorly relaxing cricopharyngeus muscle): A Zenker diverticulum forms when increased pressure in the hypopharynx pushes against a cricopharyngeus muscle that fails to relax properly during swallowing, creating a posterior outpouching just above the upper esophageal sphincter over time. Acid reflux and distal fibrosis relate to conditions like scleroderma or peptic strictures in the lower esophagus, an unrelated location and mechanism. Eosinophilic infiltration and myenteric plexus destruction are mechanisms behind eosinophilic esophagitis and achalasia respectively, not Zenker diverticulum formation."}, {"id": 169, "domain": "esophageal_disorders", "question": "A 26-year-old man presents with hematemesis after a night of heavy drinking that included several episodes of forceful, non-bloody vomiting followed by bright red vomiting. Endoscopy shows a linear mucosal tear near the gastroesophageal junction. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Boerhaave syndrome with full thickness rupture as the most likely explanation for these clinical findings.", "B": "Esophageal variceal bleeding given the constellation of symptoms and findings described here.", "C": "Peptic ulcer bleeding as the most likely explanation for these clinical findings.", "D": "Mallory-Weiss tear as the most likely explanation for these clinical findings.", "E": "Caustic esophageal injury as the most likely explanation for these clinical findings."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Mallory-Weiss tear as the most likely explanation for these clinical findings): A longitudinal mucosal tear near the gastroesophageal junction following forceful retching, with an initial episode of non-bloody vomiting preceding hematemesis, is the classic presentation of a Mallory-Weiss tear caused by a sudden rise in intra-abdominal pressure. Boerhaave syndrome involves a full thickness rupture with severe chest pain and mediastinal signs, a more severe and different presentation. Variceal bleeding would be expected in a patient with underlying portal hypertension and typically presents without this retching pattern, and caustic injury requires a specific ingestion history that is not present here."}, {"id": 180, "domain": "esophageal_disorders", "question": "A 52-year-old woman with systemic sclerosis has severe reflux and is found on endoscopy to already have Barrett esophagus at a younger age than typical. What underlying esophageal physiology in scleroderma most directly predisposes to this earlier development of Barrett esophagus?", "options": {"A": "Increased salivary bicarbonate production as the process best explaining this patient specific presentation.", "B": "Excessive esophageal peristaltic strength as the mechanism that ties together these clinical observations.", "C": "Markedly weak lower esophageal sphincter tone combined with poor esophageal clearance.", "D": "Hypertensive lower esophageal sphincter as the process best explaining this patient specific presentation.", "E": "Rapid gastric emptying reducing acid exposure time as the mechanism most consistent with these findings."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Markedly weak lower esophageal sphincter tone combined with poor esophageal clearance): In scleroderma, fibrous replacement of smooth muscle causes both a weak lower esophageal sphincter and poor esophageal clearance of refluxed acid, leading to prolonged and severe acid exposure that accelerates metaplastic change and explains why Barrett esophagus can appear earlier and more severely in these patients. Increased salivary bicarbonate would actually help neutralize acid rather than worsen exposure. Scleroderma causes weak, not excessive, peristalsis, and a hypertensive sphincter or rapid gastric emptying would reduce rather than increase reflux exposure, the opposite of what occurs in this disease."}, {"id": 181, "domain": "esophageal_disorders", "question": "A 27-year-old woman with a two-year history of intermittent dysphagia and one prior food impaction has never had a formal diagnostic workup. Which single test result would most strongly support a diagnosis of eosinophilic esophagitis over a Schatzki ring as the underlying cause?", "options": {"A": "Barium esophagram showing a thin ring at the squamocolumnar junction.", "B": "Normal appearing mucosa on endoscopy with no rings or furrows.", "C": "A single episode of impaction without any endoscopic abnormality.", "D": "Elevated serum gastrin level as the most likely explanation for these clinical findings.", "E": "Esophageal biopsy showing more than fifteen eosinophils per high power field."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Esophageal biopsy showing more than fifteen eosinophils per high power field): A biopsy showing more than fifteen eosinophils per high power field is the defining diagnostic finding for eosinophilic esophagitis and would clearly favor that diagnosis over a Schatzki ring, which is a mechanical mucosal ring without this degree of eosinophilic infiltration. A thin ring at the squamocolumnar junction on barium study actually favors Schatzki ring instead. Normal appearing mucosa without rings or furrows would be less typical of classic eosinophilic esophagitis, and a serum gastrin level is used to evaluate for gastrinoma, unrelated to either of these conditions."}, {"id": 182, "domain": "esophageal_disorders", "question": "A 36-year-old man with dysphagia undergoes endoscopy that shows a smooth extrinsic bulge in the mid esophagus. Chest CT reveals a large posterior mediastinal mass. No additional past medical history, medication use, or family history is reported that would help explain these findings. Which next step is most appropriate before considering any esophageal-directed therapy?", "options": {"A": "Immediate esophageal dilation to relieve symptoms as the most appropriate next step in this clinical scenario.", "B": "Placement of a permanent esophageal stent without further imaging.", "C": "Empiric proton pump inhibitor therapy as definitive treatment.", "D": "Further characterization of the mediastinal mass, since dilation would not address the underlying compressive cause.", "E": "Referral for antireflux surgery as the best management strategy given these clinical circumstances."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Further characterization of the mediastinal mass, since dilation would not address the underlying compressive cause): When dysphagia is caused by extrinsic compression from a mediastinal mass, the underlying mass must be characterized and addressed, because esophageal-directed treatments such as dilation, stenting, antireflux surgery, or acid suppression do not treat a fixed external compressive lesion and could be ineffective or even risky if pursued without understanding the cause. Recognizing extrinsic compression versus an intrinsic esophageal problem is essential to avoid inappropriate esophageal-focused interventions in this setting."}, {"id": 183, "domain": "esophageal_disorders", "question": "A 41-year-old man with no significant past medical history reports a single episode of a chicken bone briefly catching in his throat that resolved on its own within a minute. He has no ongoing symptoms, is tolerating food and liquids normally, and has no chest pain. What is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Emergency endoscopy within the hour as the most appropriate next step in this clinical scenario.", "B": "Barium esophagram before any other evaluation as the best management strategy given these clinical circumstances.", "C": "Reassurance and observation without endoscopy as the most appropriate next step in this clinical scenario.", "D": "Hospital admission for continuous monitoring as the most appropriate next step in this clinical scenario.", "E": "CT scan of the chest for all similar episodes."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reassurance and observation without endoscopy as the most appropriate next step in this clinical scenario): A single, brief, self-resolving episode without ongoing symptoms, difficulty swallowing, drooling, or pain does not require emergency endoscopy, since the object has clearly passed and the patient is asymptomatic, so reassurance and observation are appropriate. Emergency endoscopy is reserved for ongoing obstruction, inability to manage secretions, or high risk objects still lodged in the esophagus. Routine imaging, admission, or CT scanning are not warranted for every minor and fully resolved swallowing sensation without ongoing symptoms or objective findings."}, {"id": 186, "domain": "esophageal_disorders", "question": "A 66-year-old man with achalasia-like symptoms is later confirmed to have Chagas disease based on serology. His clinical team explains that his findings mimic primary achalasia. No additional past medical history, medication use, or family history is reported that would help explain these findings. Which shared manometric feature explains this overlap?", "options": {"A": "Excessively rapid peristalsis throughout the esophageal body as the mechanism most consistent with these findings.", "B": "A hypotensive lower esophageal sphincter with normal peristalsis.", "C": "Isolated upper esophageal sphincter dysfunction only as the process best explaining this patient specific presentation.", "D": "Failure of the lower esophageal sphincter to relax with swallowing and absent peristalsis.", "E": "Increased frequency of transient lower esophageal sphincter relaxations."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Failure of the lower esophageal sphincter to relax with swallowing and absent peristalsis): Both primary achalasia and Chagas disease destroy the myenteric plexus, whether idiopathically or through parasitic infection, resulting in the same manometric picture of a nonrelaxing lower esophageal sphincter and absent peristalsis in the esophageal body. A hypotensive sphincter with normal peristalsis is more characteristic of scleroderma rather than achalasia or Chagas disease. Isolated upper sphincter dysfunction reflects an oropharyngeal process rather than this distal motor disorder, and increased transient lower esophageal sphincter relaxations are the mechanism behind ordinary GERD, not achalasia or Chagas disease."}, {"id": 191, "domain": "esophageal_disorders", "question": "A 42-year-old woman recovering from a difficult upper endoscopy develops worsening chest pain over several hours. A contrast esophagram using barium is being considered to evaluate for a possible perforation. Which modification to this test is important given the suspected diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Using a water-soluble contrast agent instead of barium.", "B": "Using a higher volume of barium than usual.", "C": "Performing the study without any contrast at all.", "D": "Delaying the study until symptoms fully resolve based on the endoscopic findings described above.", "E": "Using barium but repeating the study twice for confirmation."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Using a water-soluble contrast agent instead of barium): When esophageal perforation is suspected, a water-soluble contrast agent is used instead of barium because barium extravasating into the mediastinum can incite significant mediastinitis and inflammation, whereas water-soluble contrast is better tolerated if a leak is present. Using more barium would worsen this risk rather than help, performing the study without any contrast would not allow evaluation for a leak, and delaying the study until symptoms resolve would dangerously postpone diagnosis of a potentially life threatening condition that requires prompt recognition."}, {"id": 195, "domain": "esophageal_disorders", "question": "A 44-year-old man reports that his heartburn has completely resolved on a proton pump inhibitor, but he continues to have intermittent solid food dysphagia. Endoscopy shows a Schatzki ring. No additional past medical history, medication use, or family history is reported that would help explain these findings. Which treatment is most appropriate for his persistent dysphagia?", "options": {"A": "Increasing the proton pump inhibitor dose further as the most appropriate management approach here.", "B": "Switching to a different acid suppressing medication class.", "C": "Adding a prokinetic agent alone as the most appropriate next step in this clinical scenario.", "D": "Referral for esophagectomy as the best management strategy given these clinical circumstances.", "E": "Endoscopic dilation of the ring as the most appropriate next step in this clinical scenario."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Endoscopic dilation of the ring as the most appropriate next step in this clinical scenario): A Schatzki ring causing mechanical dysphagia is primarily treated with endoscopic dilation to disrupt the ring, since acid suppression addresses reflux but does not resolve a fixed mechanical narrowing already present. Increasing the PPI dose or switching acid suppressing medication classes will not correct the structural ring. A prokinetic agent addresses motility rather than a fixed mechanical obstruction, and esophagectomy is a drastic and unnecessary intervention for a benign mucosal ring that responds well to simple dilation."}, {"id": 197, "domain": "esophageal_disorders", "question": "A 58-year-old man with a 30 pack-year smoking history and heavy alcohol use develops progressive dysphagia. Endoscopy in the mid esophagus reveals an irregular, ulcerated, circumferential mass causing luminal narrowing, and biopsy confirms squamous cell carcinoma. Which of his risk factors is most specifically linked to this particular esophageal cancer subtype rather than adenocarcinoma?", "options": {"A": "Chronic tobacco and alcohol use based on the clinical details provided in this vignette.", "B": "Central obesity given all of the information presented in this case.", "C": "Long standing gastroesophageal reflux disease based on the clinical details provided in this vignette.", "D": "Barrett esophagus with intestinal metaplasia based on the clinical details provided in this vignette.", "E": "Male sex and obesity combined based on the clinical details provided in this vignette."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Chronic tobacco and alcohol use based on the clinical details provided in this vignette): Tobacco and alcohol use are the classic and most strongly linked risk factors for esophageal squamous cell carcinoma, in contrast to esophageal adenocarcinoma, which is primarily driven by chronic reflux, obesity, and Barrett esophagus. Central obesity and reflux related Barrett metaplasia are specifically tied to the adenocarcinoma pathway rather than squamous cell carcinoma. While male sex is a shared risk factor across both subtypes to some degree, when combined with obesity it points more toward the adenocarcinoma risk profile rather than the squamous cell carcinoma profile described in this smoking and alcohol history."}, {"id": 204, "domain": "gastric_duodenal_disorders", "question": "A 72 year old woman taking daily ibuprofen for osteoarthritis develops a gastric ulcer on endoscopy with negative H pylori testing. She is switched to an enteric coated NSAID formulation. Which statement best describes her ongoing ulcer risk?", "options": {"A": "Enteric coating fully eliminates all NSAID related ulcer risk going forward.", "B": "Her ulcer risk was due only to direct topical mucosal contact.", "C": "Stopping NSAID exposure entirely would have no effect on healing.", "D": "Enteric coating reduces topical injury but does not eliminate the systemic prostaglandin mediated risk.", "E": "NSAIDs only injure the duodenum and cannot cause gastric ulcers."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Enteric coating reduces topical injury but does not eliminate the systemic prostaglandin mediated risk): Enteric coated NSAID formulations reduce direct topical mucosal contact but do not prevent systemic cyclooxygenase-1 inhibition, which reduces protective prostaglandins throughout the mucosa regardless of formulation. Claiming complete elimination of risk is incorrect since the systemic mechanism persists. Her risk is not due to topical contact alone, since systemic inhibition is the dominant mechanism with any NSAID route. Stopping the NSAID does help healing, and NSAIDs injure both the stomach and duodenum, not just the duodenum."}, {"id": 213, "domain": "gastric_duodenal_disorders", "question": "A patient diagnosed with cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome continues to have breakthrough vomiting despite antiemetics and topical capsaicin cream applied during episodes. Which intervention is most likely to produce durable resolution of her symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Escalating to a higher potency antiemetic regimen alone.", "B": "Complete and sustained cessation of cannabis use.", "C": "Starting a proton pump inhibitor twice daily indefinitely.", "D": "Referral for laparoscopic cholecystectomy to remove a chronically diseased gallbladder.", "E": "Initiating chronic opioid therapy for symptom control."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Complete and sustained cessation of cannabis use): Only sustained cannabis cessation addresses the underlying cause of cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome and leads to full resolution over days to weeks. Antiemetics and capsaicin only provide partial, temporary relief while cannabis use continues. A proton pump inhibitor does not target the underlying receptor dysregulation causing this syndrome. Cholecystectomy and chronic opioids are not indicated since there is no biliary pathology and opioids can themselves worsen nausea and motility."}, {"id": 214, "domain": "gastric_duodenal_disorders", "question": "A 45 year old woman with longstanding type 1 diabetes reports early satiety, postprandial bloating, and vomiting of food eaten many hours earlier that still appears undigested. Which condition best fits this classic symptom triad?", "options": {"A": "Achalasia of the lower esophageal sphincter.", "B": "Acute pancreatitis causing epigastric pain that classically radiates to the back.", "C": "Gastroparesis causing delayed emptying of solid food from the stomach.", "D": "Irritable bowel syndrome with a predominantly constipation related symptom pattern.", "E": "Peptic ulcer disease without any associated mechanical outlet obstruction."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Gastroparesis causing delayed emptying of solid food from the stomach): Early satiety, postprandial fullness, and vomiting of undigested food eaten hours earlier is the classic triad of gastroparesis, and longstanding diabetes is a common cause. Achalasia affects esophageal emptying of swallowed material, not gastric emptying of a meal hours later. Acute pancreatitis presents with epigastric pain radiating to the back, not this vomiting pattern. Irritable bowel syndrome and uncomplicated peptic ulcer disease do not typically cause vomiting of retained, undigested food."}, {"id": 221, "domain": "gastric_duodenal_disorders", "question": "A patient with chronic vomiting from gastric outlet obstruction has labs drawn before rehydration. Which acid base and electrolyte pattern is most characteristic of this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Hyperkalemic, hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis from renal bicarbonate wasting.", "B": "Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with hyperkalemia.", "C": "Respiratory alkalosis with normal serum electrolytes.", "D": "Hypokalemic, hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis from loss of gastric acid.", "E": "Hypercalcemia with a widened anion gap."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hypokalemic, hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis from loss of gastric acid): Loss of gastric acid and chloride from vomiting, combined with volume depletion driving renal potassium wasting, produces a hypokalemic, hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis. A hyperkalemic, hyperchloremic acidosis is the opposite pattern and does not fit vomiting losses. A normal anion gap acidosis with hyperkalemia is more typical of certain renal or adrenal problems, not gastric fluid loss. Respiratory alkalosis and hypercalcemia are not the expected pattern from chronic vomiting."}, {"id": 225, "domain": "gastric_duodenal_disorders", "question": "Endoscopy in a patient with gastric outlet obstruction shows a stricture that appears smooth and consistent with chronic ulcer scarring. Which step should still be performed before assuming a benign cause?", "options": {"A": "Immediate surgical bypass without any tissue sampling.", "B": "Repeat the endoscopy in one year without biopsy now.", "C": "Biopsy of the stricture to exclude an underlying malignancy.", "D": "Empiric H pylori eradication without any biopsy.", "E": "Discharge home with dietary modification only."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Biopsy of the stricture to exclude an underlying malignancy): Even a smooth, benign appearing stricture requires biopsy because malignancy can mimic benign scarring endoscopically, and missing a cancer diagnosis has serious consequences. Proceeding to surgery without any tissue sampling forgoes a diagnosis that could change management. Waiting a year to biopsy delays a potential cancer diagnosis unacceptably. Empiric eradication therapy and simple discharge do not address the need to rule out malignancy now."}, {"id": 226, "domain": "gastric_duodenal_disorders", "question": "A 60 year old woman has macrocytic anemia, low vitamin B12, and endoscopic biopsies showing atrophy limited to the gastric body and fundus with antral sparing. Which diagnosis best fits this pattern?", "options": {"A": "Autoimmune gastritis targeting parietal cells in the gastric body and fundus.", "B": "Helicobacter pylori gastritis limited to the antrum.", "C": "NSAID induced reactive gastropathy with foveolar hyperplasia and minimal inflammation.", "D": "Eosinophilic gastritis with marked eosinophilic infiltration of the gastric mucosa.", "E": "Menetrier disease with giant hypertrophied folds and protein losing gastropathy."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Autoimmune gastritis targeting parietal cells in the gastric body and fundus): Body and fundus predominant atrophy with antral sparing, B12 deficiency, and macrocytic anemia is the classic pattern of autoimmune gastritis from parietal cell destruction. H pylori gastritis typically begins in the antrum rather than sparing it. NSAID gastropathy shows foveolar hyperplasia with minimal inflammation, not this atrophic autoimmune pattern. Eosinophilic gastritis and Menetrier disease have distinct histologic features that do not match isolated body and fundus atrophy with B12 deficiency."}, {"id": 235, "domain": "gastric_duodenal_disorders", "question": "A patient with known systemic sarcoidosis develops early satiety and imaging suggesting a thickened, rigid gastric wall resembling linitis plastica. Which finding on gastric biopsy would confirm sarcoid involvement rather than infiltrating adenocarcinoma?", "options": {"A": "Signet ring cells with mucin production.", "B": "Noncaseating granulomas seen on histology without any malignant cells present.", "C": "Marked eosinophilic infiltration of the mucosa.", "D": "Intranuclear viral inclusion bodies seen within infected gastric stromal cells.", "E": "Amyloid deposits with apple green birefringence."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Noncaseating granulomas seen on histology without any malignant cells present): Noncaseating granulomas on histology confirm sarcoid involvement of the stomach, distinguishing it from malignancy. Signet ring cells with mucin production instead indicate infiltrating adenocarcinoma, the diagnosis being excluded here. Eosinophilic infiltration suggests eosinophilic gastritis rather than sarcoidosis. Viral inclusions and amyloid deposits point toward infectious or amyloid processes, not granulomatous sarcoid disease."}, {"id": 236, "domain": "gastric_duodenal_disorders", "question": "A 24 year old man is found to have numerous fundic gland polyps on endoscopy, along with colonic adenomas on a separate colonoscopy. Which feature should most raise concern for an underlying syndrome rather than sporadic PPI related polyps?", "options": {"A": "A single fundic gland polyp found incidentally in a 70 year old.", "B": "Long term daily proton pump inhibitor use alone.", "C": "A polyp size under five millimeters on endoscopic measurement.", "D": "Absence of any family history of gastrointestinal disease.", "E": "Young age with numerous polyps plus synchronous colonic adenomas."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Young age with numerous polyps plus synchronous colonic adenomas): Young age, numerous fundic gland polyps, and synchronous colonic adenomas together should raise concern for familial adenomatous polyposis rather than sporadic PPI related disease. A single incidental polyp in an older patient is the typical sporadic presentation. Long term PPI use alone explains sporadic polyps without a syndrome. Small polyp size and absent family history do not by themselves raise concern the way this combination of findings does."}, {"id": 237, "domain": "gastric_duodenal_disorders", "question": "A patient has a gastric polyp on a background of active Helicobacter pylori gastritis. Biopsy shows elongated, tortuous foveolar glands with inflammation and edema in the lamina propria. Which polyp type is most likely, and what may happen after eradication therapy?", "options": {"A": "A hyperplastic polyp, which may regress after H pylori eradication.", "B": "An adenomatous polyp, which never regresses with any treatment.", "C": "A fundic gland polyp, which is unrelated to H pylori status.", "D": "A neuroendocrine tumor, which requires resection regardless of H pylori status.", "E": "An inflammatory fibroid polyp, which always requires urgent surgery."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A hyperplastic polyp, which may regress after H pylori eradication): Elongated, tortuous foveolar glands with inflammation in the setting of active H pylori infection describes a hyperplastic polyp, which can regress after successful eradication therapy. Adenomatous polyps do not show this inflammatory histology and are not linked to H pylori regression. Fundic gland polyps are tied to PPI use, not active H pylori infection, and would show different histology. Neuroendocrine tumors and inflammatory fibroid polyps have distinct appearances unrelated to this inflammatory hyperplastic pattern."}, {"id": 238, "domain": "gastric_duodenal_disorders", "question": "Endoscopy identifies a 12 millimeter gastric polyp with tubular adenomatous histology on biopsy. Which management approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Observation alone with no removal since gastric adenomas rarely progress.", "B": "Complete endoscopic removal with attention to synchronous lesions and surveillance.", "C": "Reassurance and discharge with no further endoscopic follow up.", "D": "Treatment with proton pump inhibitors alone to induce regression.", "E": "Repeat biopsy only, deferring removal indefinitely."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Complete endoscopic removal with attention to synchronous lesions and surveillance): Adenomatous gastric polyps carry the highest malignant potential of the common polyp types and should always be completely removed with surveillance for synchronous lesions. Observation alone risks progression to malignancy. Discharge without follow up ignores this cancer risk entirely. Proton pump inhibitors do not cause regression of adenomatous polyps, and repeat biopsy without removal delays definitive management unnecessarily."}, {"id": 240, "domain": "gastric_duodenal_disorders", "question": "During surveillance endoscopy a hyperplastic polyp measuring 1.5 centimeters is identified. Which statement about size and management is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Size has no bearing on dysplasia risk in hyperplastic polyps.", "B": "Only polyps under five millimeters ever require removal.", "C": "Hyperplastic polyps of any size should never be biopsied.", "D": "Polyps of this size carry a small dysplasia risk and should be removed.", "E": "This size mandates emergent gastrectomy rather than endoscopic removal."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Polyps of this size carry a small dysplasia risk and should be removed): Hyperplastic polyps over one centimeter carry a small but real dysplasia risk and should be removed, unlike smaller lesions that are often simply observed. Size does in fact correlate with dysplasia risk in hyperplastic polyps, contrary to the claim that it has no bearing. It is the larger polyps, not only those under five millimeters, that warrant removal. Endoscopic removal, not emergent gastrectomy, is the appropriate approach for a polyp of this size."}, {"id": 242, "domain": "gastric_duodenal_disorders", "question": "Two patients have Helicobacter pylori infection. One has antral predominant gastritis with high acid output and a duodenal ulcer, while the other has more extensive corpus involvement with lower acid output and a gastric ulcer. Which statement best explains this divergence?", "options": {"A": "Duodenal ulcers only occur in patients who have never been infected with H pylori.", "B": "Gastric ulcers always indicate coexisting malignancy rather than H pylori alone.", "C": "The pattern and extent of gastritis determines whether ulcers form in the duodenum or stomach.", "D": "Acid output has no relationship to the location of H pylori associated ulcers.", "E": "Antral predominant gastritis always leads to achlorhydria rather than high acid output."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The pattern and extent of gastritis determines whether ulcers form in the duodenum or stomach): The distribution and extent of H pylori gastritis determines the acid output and, in turn, whether duodenal or gastric ulcers form, exactly as illustrated by these two patients. Duodenal ulcers are actually strongly associated with H pylori infection, not its absence. Gastric ulcers from H pylori do not always indicate malignancy, though biopsy is still needed to exclude it. Acid output does relate to ulcer location, and antral predominant disease is linked to high, not low, acid output."}, {"id": 245, "domain": "gastric_duodenal_disorders", "question": "A patient treated for H pylori five years ago now has recurrent ulcer symptoms. Serology is checked and returns positive. Which statement about this result is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Serology often remains positive after successful eradication and cannot confirm active infection.", "B": "A positive serology always confirms an active, untreated infection.", "C": "Serology is the preferred test to document cure after treatment.", "D": "A positive serology means the treatment definitely failed.", "E": "Serology results are more reliable than a stool antigen test for confirming cure."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Serology often remains positive after successful eradication and cannot confirm active infection): Serology detects antibodies that can remain positive for years after successful eradication, so this positive result does not confirm active infection now. It does not always indicate an active, untreated infection given this persistence. Serology is specifically not the preferred test to document cure, and it is less reliable than a stool antigen or breath test for that purpose. A positive serology alone does not mean treatment failed, since active infection would need to be confirmed with a different test."}, {"id": 257, "domain": "gastric_duodenal_disorders", "question": "A patient with suspected Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is taking a proton pump inhibitor daily. Which step is most important before interpreting a fasting serum gastrin level?", "options": {"A": "Draw the gastrin level immediately after a large protein meal.", "B": "Continue the proton pump inhibitor at an increased dose during testing.", "C": "Measure gastrin only after a 72 hour period of fasting from all fluids.", "D": "Hold the proton pump inhibitor when feasible before drawing the level.", "E": "Serum gastrin does not require any adjustment for concurrent medications."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold the proton pump inhibitor when feasible before drawing the level): Proton pump inhibitors raise gastrin through the normal feedback loop, so holding the medication when feasible allows more accurate interpretation of the fasting gastrin level. Drawing the level after a meal would falsely elevate gastrin from a normal postprandial response. Continuing or increasing the PPI dose would worsen, not help, the confounding effect. Gastrin testing does require this medication adjustment, contrary to the claim that no adjustment is needed."}, {"id": 261, "domain": "gastric_duodenal_disorders", "question": "A patient after gastric bypass has flushing, tachycardia, and abdominal cramping fifteen minutes after eating a sugary meal, while a different patient develops sweating and confusion two hours after a similar meal. Which statement best distinguishes these two presentations?", "options": {"A": "Both presentations reflect the identical underlying mechanism of osmotic fluid shift.", "B": "The first reflects reactive hypoglycemia and the second reflects osmotic fluid shift.", "C": "Neither presentation is related to altered postsurgical gastric anatomy.", "D": "Both represent marginal ulcer bleeding rather than a motility related process.", "E": "The first reflects early dumping from osmotic fluid shifts, the second reflects late dumping from reactive hypoglycemia."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The first reflects early dumping from osmotic fluid shifts, the second reflects late dumping from reactive hypoglycemia): Symptoms within thirty minutes reflect early dumping from osmotic fluid shifts, while symptoms one to three hours later with hypoglycemia reflect late dumping from an exaggerated insulin response, matching the two patients described. The two presentations do not share the identical mechanism, since the timing and pathophysiology differ. The mechanisms are not reversed as stated in the incorrect option. Both are classic complications of altered postsurgical anatomy, not unrelated to it, and neither describes ulcer bleeding."}, {"id": 267, "domain": "gastric_duodenal_disorders", "question": "A patient after gastric bypass surgery develops pain and bleeding from an ulcer at the gastrojejunal anastomosis. Which combination of factors most increases the risk of this marginal ulcer?", "options": {"A": "Strict smoking cessation and small pouch size alone.", "B": "Only advanced patient age with no other risk factors.", "C": "Smoking, NSAID use, and a larger gastric pouch size.", "D": "Low pouch acid exposure from a gastro-gastric fistula.", "E": "Vitamin B12 supplementation without any other risk factor."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Smoking, NSAID use, and a larger gastric pouch size): Smoking, NSAID use, H pylori, and a larger gastric pouch size are the established risk factors that increase marginal ulcer risk after gastric bypass. Smoking cessation and a small pouch actually reduce risk rather than increase it. Age alone is not the dominant risk factor described in the literature. A gastro-gastric fistula increases, not decreases, acid exposure to the pouch, and B12 supplementation has no bearing on marginal ulcer risk."}, {"id": 270, "domain": "gastric_duodenal_disorders", "question": "A patient develops progressive dysphagia and vomiting several weeks after gastric bypass, and endoscopy reveals a narrowed gastrojejunal anastomosis. Which management is most appropriate as an initial step?", "options": {"A": "Endoscopic balloon dilation of the stricture.", "B": "Immediate surgical revision without attempting endoscopic therapy.", "C": "Lifelong liquid diet with no procedural intervention.", "D": "High dose proton pump inhibitor therapy alone without dilation.", "E": "Observation only, since strictures always resolve spontaneously."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Endoscopic balloon dilation of the stricture): Endoscopic balloon dilation is the standard initial management for an anastomotic stricture causing dysphagia and vomiting after gastric bypass. Surgical revision is generally reserved for strictures that fail endoscopic therapy, not as the first step. A lifelong liquid diet does not address the underlying mechanical narrowing. Proton pump inhibitor therapy alone does not relieve mechanical stricture, and strictures do not reliably resolve on their own without intervention."}, {"id": 273, "domain": "gastric_duodenal_disorders", "question": "A patient with longstanding poorly controlled type 2 diabetes develops delayed gastric emptying. Which cellular mechanism most directly explains this diabetic complication?", "options": {"A": "Damage to vagal fibers and interstitial cells of Cajal, the gastric pacemaker cells.", "B": "Autoimmune destruction of gastric parietal cells.", "C": "Mechanical obstruction from a fibrotic pyloric stricture.", "D": "Overgrowth of small bowel bacteria causing osmotic diarrhea.", "E": "Excess somatostatin secretion suppressing gastric motility."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Damage to vagal fibers and interstitial cells of Cajal, the gastric pacemaker cells): Chronic hyperglycemia damages vagal nerve fibers and the interstitial cells of Cajal, the gastric pacemaker cells, directly impairing coordinated motility in diabetic gastroparesis. Parietal cell destruction describes autoimmune gastritis, an unrelated process. This is a functional motility problem, not a mechanical stricture. Small bowel bacterial overgrowth causes diarrhea rather than delayed emptying, and there is no known excess somatostatin mechanism driving diabetic gastroparesis."}, {"id": 280, "domain": "gastric_duodenal_disorders", "question": "A patient with known Crohn disease develops early satiety, vomiting, and imaging showing narrowing at the duodenal bulb. Which statement about Crohn disease involvement of the upper GI tract is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Crohn disease never involves the stomach or duodenum under any circumstance.", "B": "Upper GI involvement in Crohn disease always occurs in isolation from ileal disease.", "C": "Gastric involvement in Crohn disease is more common than ileal involvement.", "D": "Duodenal strictures in Crohn disease cannot cause any obstructive symptoms.", "E": "Gastroduodenal Crohn disease can cause stricturing that leads to outlet obstruction."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Gastroduodenal Crohn disease can cause stricturing that leads to outlet obstruction): Gastroduodenal Crohn disease, usually an extension of duodenal disease, can cause stricturing that produces gastric outlet obstruction, exactly as seen in this patient. Crohn disease can and does involve the stomach and duodenum, contrary to the claim that it never does. Upper GI involvement typically occurs as an extension of disease elsewhere, not in complete isolation. Ileal involvement remains far more common than gastric involvement, and duodenal strictures clearly can cause obstructive symptoms as demonstrated here."}, {"id": 286, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A physician evaluating a patient in a gastroenterology clinic for a suspected inflammatory or immune mediated bowel disorder considers the following question. A 28 year old woman with Crohn disease develops recurrent urinary tract infections with mixed enteric organisms and reports passing small air bubbles in her urine. Which complication of Crohn disease best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Enterocutaneous fistula given this patient's overall clinical presentation and findings.", "B": "Toxic megacolon given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette.", "C": "Enterovesical fistula based on the clinical course and findings described above.", "D": "Perianal abscess based on the history, exam, and diagnostic findings here.", "E": "Colonic stricture based on the history, exam, and diagnostic findings here."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Enterovesical fistula based on the clinical course and findings described above): An enterovesical fistula connects the bowel to the bladder and classically presents with recurrent mixed enteric urinary infections and pneumaturia, which is air in the urine. An enterocutaneous fistula drains through the skin instead. Toxic megacolon and colonic stricture do not cause urinary symptoms, and a perianal abscess presents with perianal pain and swelling."}, {"id": 287, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A 35 year old man with longstanding ileal Crohn disease develops crampy postprandial pain and bloating. Imaging shows a narrowed segment of terminal ileum with proximal dilation and thickened, fibrotic-appearing bowel wall without acute inflammation on labs. Which treatment is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "High dose intravenous corticosteroids based on the history, exam, and diagnostic findings here.", "B": "Broad spectrum antibiotics alone given this patient's overall clinical presentation and findings.", "C": "Endoscopic balloon dilation or surgical resection given this patient's overall clinical presentation and findings.", "D": "Increased fiber intake as the explanation most consistent with this clinical picture.", "E": "Loperamide as needed given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Endoscopic balloon dilation or surgical resection given this patient's overall clinical presentation and findings): A fibrotic stricture without active inflammation generally does not respond to medical anti-inflammatory therapy, so endoscopic dilation or surgical resection is the appropriate approach for an obstructing fixed narrowing. Corticosteroids target active inflammation, which is not present here. Antibiotics treat infection or abscess, not fibrosis, and increasing fiber or using loperamide could worsen an obstructive picture."}, {"id": 288, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A physician evaluating a patient in a gastroenterology clinic for a suspected inflammatory or immune mediated bowel disorder considers the following question. A 26 year old woman with Crohn disease has predominant symptoms of dysphagia and epigastric discomfort. Upper endoscopy shows aphthous ulcers in the duodenum with a cobblestone appearance. Which pattern of Crohn disease does this represent?", "options": {"A": "Isolated perianal disease as the explanation most consistent with this clinical picture.", "B": "Ileocolonic disease based on the history, exam, and diagnostic findings here.", "C": "Upper gastrointestinal Crohn disease as the explanation most consistent with this clinical picture.", "D": "Colonic Crohn disease as the explanation most consistent with this clinical picture.", "E": "Backwash ileitis given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Upper gastrointestinal Crohn disease as the explanation most consistent with this clinical picture): Upper gastrointestinal Crohn disease involves the esophagus, stomach, or duodenum and should be suspected when dysphagia or epigastric symptoms occur alongside typical Crohn findings such as aphthous ulcers or cobblestoning. Perianal disease involves the anorectal region, not the upper tract. Ileocolonic and colonic disease refer to lower gastrointestinal locations, and backwash ileitis is a finding in ulcerative colitis."}, {"id": 289, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A 40 year old man with Crohn disease has a draining perianal fistula with surrounding induration and pain. Pelvic MRI shows a complex fistula tract with an associated fluid collection. What is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Start an anti-TNF agent alone without drainage based on the history, exam, and diagnostic findings here.", "B": "Oral antibiotics only, reassess in one month given the findings and clinical context provided in this.", "C": "Observation with sitz baths given the pattern of symptoms and findings described here.", "D": "Surgical or radiologic drainage of the abscess plus anti-TNF therapy.", "E": "Immediate colectomy given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Surgical or radiologic drainage of the abscess plus anti-TNF therapy): Any associated abscess must be drained before or alongside starting biologic therapy, since anti-TNF agents alone will not resolve a walled-off collection and could theoretically worsen sepsis if given without source control. Antibiotics and sitz baths alone are insufficient for a complex fistula with abscess. Colectomy is not indicated for isolated perianal disease without severe colonic involvement."}, {"id": 300, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A 36 year old man with Crohn disease presents with fever, right lower quadrant pain, and a palpable mass. CT shows a walled-off fluid collection adjacent to an area of active ileitis. Antibiotics alone are started. What is the most important additional step?", "options": {"A": "Increase corticosteroid dose given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette.", "B": "Start a fiber supplement based on the history, exam, and diagnostic findings here.", "C": "Begin an elimination diet given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette.", "D": "Discontinue all Crohn medications immediately based on the history, exam, and diagnostic findings here.", "E": "Percutaneous or surgical drainage of the abscess as the explanation most consistent with this clinical picture."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Percutaneous or surgical drainage of the abscess as the explanation most consistent with this clinical picture): An abscess is a walled-off collection that requires physical drainage, either percutaneously by interventional radiology or surgically, because antibiotics alone rarely penetrate and resolve such a collection. Increasing corticosteroids could worsen an undrained infection. Fiber supplementation and elimination diets do not address an abscess, and abruptly stopping all medications is not an appropriate response to this acute complication."}, {"id": 301, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A 24 year old man with ileocolonic Crohn disease has a stricture on imaging. Labs show elevated inflammatory markers and colonoscopy shows active ulceration at the strictured segment. Which type of stricture is most likely present, and what is the best initial approach?", "options": {"A": "Fibrotic stricture, proceed straight to surgery given this patient's overall clinical presentation and findings.", "B": "Malignant stricture, refer for oncology only given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette.", "C": "Ischemic stricture, anticoagulate immediately based on the history, exam, and diagnostic findings here.", "D": "Inflammatory stricture, optimize medical anti-inflammatory therapy first as the explanation most consistent with this clinical picture.", "E": "Congenital stricture, no treatment needed based on the clinical course and findings described above."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Inflammatory stricture, optimize medical anti-inflammatory therapy first as the explanation most consistent with this clinical picture): Active ulceration and elevated inflammatory markers at the strictured segment suggest an inflammatory rather than purely fibrotic stricture, which is more likely to improve with optimized medical anti-inflammatory therapy before considering mechanical intervention. Proceeding straight to surgery is premature if inflammation is still contributing. A malignant or ischemic process is not suggested by this clinical picture, and congenital strictures would not present with new active inflammation."}, {"id": 303, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A 29 year old woman with known ileal Crohn disease has a colonoscopy showing rectal sparing, patchy ulcers in the terminal ileum, and normal mucosa in the rectum and sigmoid colon. Which finding would argue against a diagnosis of ulcerative colitis instead?", "options": {"A": "Rectal sparing with patchy, discontinuous ulceration given the pattern of symptoms and findings described here.", "B": "Continuous inflammation from the rectum upward as the explanation most consistent with this clinical picture.", "C": "Toxic megacolon given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette.", "D": "Backwash ileitis only given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette.", "E": "Pseudopolyps in the rectum given this patient's overall clinical presentation and findings."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rectal sparing with patchy, discontinuous ulceration given the pattern of symptoms and findings described here): Rectal sparing combined with patchy, discontinuous ulceration is inconsistent with ulcerative colitis, which always begins in the rectum and spreads continuously without skip areas, supporting a Crohn disease diagnosis instead. Continuous rectal involvement, toxic megacolon, backwash ileitis, and rectal pseudopolyps are all findings more typical of or specific to ulcerative colitis."}, {"id": 305, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A 26 year old woman with Crohn disease flares reports that her peripheral joint pains consistently worsen when her bowel symptoms worsen and improve when her Crohn disease is controlled with biologic therapy. Which category of extraintestinal manifestation does this represent?", "options": {"A": "Activity-parallel manifestation given this patient's overall clinical presentation and findings.", "B": "Activity-independent manifestation given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette.", "C": "Paraneoplastic syndrome based on the clinical course and findings described above.", "D": "Drug-induced reaction only given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette.", "E": "Unrelated autoimmune disease given the pattern of symptoms and findings described here."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Activity-parallel manifestation given this patient's overall clinical presentation and findings): Peripheral arthritis that flares and improves along with bowel disease activity is classified as an activity-parallel extraintestinal manifestation, meaning it tracks with the underlying Crohn disease control. Activity-independent manifestations such as ankylosing spondylitis or primary sclerosing cholangitis do not follow this pattern. This scenario does not describe a paraneoplastic, purely drug-induced, or unrelated process."}, {"id": 319, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A physician evaluating a patient in a gastroenterology clinic for a suspected inflammatory or immune mediated bowel disorder considers the following question. A 36 year old woman with toxic megacolon from ulcerative colitis has been on maximal intravenous corticosteroids, antibiotics, and bowel rest for 72 hours without clinical improvement. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Urgent colectomy based on the clinical course and findings described above.", "B": "Continue the same medical therapy for another week.", "C": "Switch to oral corticosteroids and discharge home given this patient's overall clinical presentation and findings.", "D": "Begin a gluten-free diet based on the history, exam, and diagnostic findings here.", "E": "Start an oral fiber supplement based on the clinical course and findings described above."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Urgent colectomy based on the clinical course and findings described above): Failure to improve within 48 to 72 hours of intensive medical therapy for toxic megacolon, or any sign of perforation, is an indication for urgent colectomy because continued delay significantly raises mortality risk. Continuing the same regimen indefinitely, switching to oral steroids for discharge, or starting a fiber supplement or gluten-free diet do not address the urgent surgical need in this scenario."}, {"id": 320, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A 44 year old man with a 12 year history of extensive ulcerative colitis and no other risk factors is being scheduled for cancer surveillance. When should surveillance colonoscopy with extensive biopsies or chromoendoscopy typically begin for extensive disease without primary sclerosing cholangitis?", "options": {"A": "About eight to ten years after diagnosis of extensive disease.", "B": "Immediately at diagnosis, before any symptoms develop as the explanation most consistent with this clinical picture.", "C": "Only after age 60 regardless of disease duration.", "D": "Only if he develops iron deficiency anemia based on the clinical course and findings described above.", "E": "Surveillance is not indicated for ulcerative colitis as the explanation most consistent with this clinical picture."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About eight to ten years after diagnosis of extensive disease): For extensive ulcerative colitis without concurrent primary sclerosing cholangitis, surveillance colonoscopy with extensive biopsies or chromoendoscopy typically begins about eight to ten years after diagnosis. Starting immediately at diagnosis is unnecessary this early, and waiting until age 60 or until symptoms like anemia develop would miss the appropriate risk-based window. Surveillance is clearly indicated given the elevated colorectal cancer risk in extensive disease."}, {"id": 325, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A physician evaluating a patient in a gastroenterology clinic for a suspected inflammatory or immune mediated bowel disorder considers the following question. A 42 year old woman with severe ulcerative colitis requires multiple blood transfusions for ongoing lower gastrointestinal bleeding despite maximal medical therapy. Which management approach is most appropriate at this point?", "options": {"A": "Continue current medical therapy indefinitely without reassessment given the pattern of symptoms and findings described here.", "B": "Switch to an elimination diet only based on the clinical course and findings described above.", "C": "Discharge home with oral iron given the pattern of symptoms and findings described here.", "D": "Consider surgical colectomy given failure of medical therapy with major bleeding.", "E": "Begin antiparasitic therapy empirically given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Consider surgical colectomy given failure of medical therapy with major bleeding): Severe bleeding requiring repeated transfusion despite maximal medical therapy is an indication to consider surgical colectomy, since continued medical management alone has failed to control a life-threatening complication. Continuing the same therapy without reassessment, an elimination diet, discharge with oral iron, or empiric antiparasitic treatment do not address ongoing major hemorrhage in this setting."}, {"id": 328, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A physician evaluating a patient in a gastroenterology clinic for a suspected inflammatory or immune mediated bowel disorder considers the following question. A 33 year old man with ulcerative colitis has a colonoscopy showing multiple raised, regenerative mucosal projections in areas of prior severe ulceration, without evidence of active inflammation elsewhere. What are these lesions called, and are they premalignant?", "options": {"A": "Adenomatous polyps, which are premalignant based on the clinical course and findings described above.", "B": "Pseudopolyps, which are not inherently premalignant based on the history, exam, and diagnostic findings here.", "C": "Hyperplastic polyps, which are always premalignant based on the clinical course and findings described above.", "D": "Serrated polyps, which require immediate resection based on the history, exam, and diagnostic findings here.", "E": "Villous adenomas, which require urgent surgery given the pattern of symptoms and findings described here."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pseudopolyps, which are not inherently premalignant based on the history, exam, and diagnostic findings here): Pseudopolyps are regenerative mucosal tags that form after healing of severe ulceration in ulcerative colitis, and they are not inherently premalignant themselves, though their presence reflects a history of severe inflammation that warrants ongoing surveillance. Adenomatous, hyperplastic, and serrated polyps and villous adenomas are distinct neoplastic or preneoplastic lesion types with different management implications than simple pseudopolyps."}, {"id": 332, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A physician evaluating a patient in a gastroenterology clinic for a suspected inflammatory or immune mediated bowel disorder considers the following question. A 38 year old woman with a severe ulcerative colitis flare is being managed medically. Her treatment team is deciding whether an antimotility agent would be appropriate to reduce her stool frequency. What is the most appropriate consideration?", "options": {"A": "Antimotility agents are always safe and recommended in severe colitis.", "B": "Antimotility agents should be avoided since they can precipitate toxic megacolon.", "C": "Antimotility agents cure the underlying inflammation given the pattern of symptoms and findings described here.", "D": "Antimotility agents are first-line therapy for severe flares.", "E": "Antimotility agents have no effect on colonic motility."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Antimotility agents should be avoided since they can precipitate toxic megacolon): Antimotility agents slow colonic transit and are avoided in severe ulcerative colitis flares because they can precipitate toxic megacolon, a life-threatening complication. They are not universally safe in this setting, do not treat the underlying inflammatory process, are not first-line therapy for severe flares, and by definition do affect colonic motility, which is exactly the concern."}, {"id": 338, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A 24 year old man has chronic malabsorptive diarrhea and iron deficiency anemia. His physician orders serologic testing but the patient has already started a strict gluten-free diet two months earlier on his own. Which statement about this testing strategy is correct?", "options": {"A": "Gluten-free diet has no effect on serologic results.", "B": "Serologic testing is more accurate after starting a gluten-free diet.", "C": "Starting a gluten-free diet before testing can normalize antibodies and biopsy findings, complicating diagnosis.", "D": "Biopsy findings are unaffected by diet given the pattern of symptoms and findings described here.", "E": "Gluten-free diet should always precede testing for accuracy."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Starting a gluten-free diet before testing can normalize antibodies and biopsy findings, complicating diagnosis): Starting a gluten-free diet before serologic testing or biopsy can normalize both antibody levels and villous architecture, making it much harder to confirm the diagnosis, so testing should ideally occur while the patient is still consuming gluten. It is incorrect to say diet has no effect, that testing is more accurate after starting the diet, that biopsy findings are unaffected, or that the diet should precede testing."}, {"id": 341, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A 26 year old woman with celiac disease has intensely itchy, blistering skin lesions on her elbows and knees. Skin biopsy of adjacent skin shows granular IgA deposits at the tips of the dermal papillae. Which condition does this describe?", "options": {"A": "Dermatitis herpetiformis based on the clinical course and findings described above.", "B": "Pyoderma gangrenosum given this patient's overall clinical presentation and findings.", "C": "Erythema nodosum based on the history, exam, and diagnostic findings here.", "D": "Psoriasis given the pattern of symptoms and findings described here.", "E": "Eczema based on the clinical course and findings described above."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Dermatitis herpetiformis based on the clinical course and findings described above): Dermatitis herpetiformis is the classic cutaneous manifestation of celiac disease, presenting as itchy blistering lesions on extensor surfaces like the elbows and knees, with granular IgA deposits at the dermal papillae tips on biopsy. Pyoderma gangrenosum and erythema nodosum are associated with inflammatory bowel disease rather than celiac disease, and psoriasis or eczema have different clinical and histologic features."}, {"id": 343, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A physician evaluating a patient in a gastroenterology clinic for a suspected inflammatory or immune mediated bowel disorder considers the following question. A clinician reviewing a case involving a patient with a chronic gastrointestinal condition affecting the bowel considers the following question. Which nutrient deficiencies are most classically associated with celiac disease due to proximal small bowel damage?", "options": {"A": "Iron and folate deficiency given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette.", "B": "Vitamin B12 and intrinsic factor deficiency based on the clinical course and findings described above.", "C": "Vitamin K and prothrombin deficiency only given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette.", "D": "Zinc toxicity given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette.", "E": "Excess calcium absorption given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Iron and folate deficiency given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette): Iron and folate are absorbed in the duodenum and proximal jejunum, the segments most affected by celiac disease, so deficiencies of these nutrients are classic even before diarrhea becomes prominent. Vitamin B12 is absorbed more distally in the terminal ileum and is not the primary deficiency pattern in celiac disease. Vitamin K deficiency alone, zinc toxicity, and excess calcium absorption do not describe the typical celiac malabsorption pattern."}, {"id": 351, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A 29 year old woman with confirmed celiac disease and known IgA deficiency needs ongoing serologic monitoring of her response to a gluten-free diet. Which antibody test is most appropriate for her long term monitoring given her IgA deficiency?", "options": {"A": "tTG-IgA alone given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette.", "B": "Anti-mitochondrial antibody given this patient's overall clinical presentation and findings.", "C": "Anti-smooth muscle antibody as the explanation most consistent with this clinical picture.", "D": "Rheumatoid factor based on the history, exam, and diagnostic findings here.", "E": "IgG-based deamidated gliadin peptide antibody based on the clinical course and findings described above."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (IgG-based deamidated gliadin peptide antibody based on the clinical course and findings described above): Because this patient has IgA deficiency, tTG-IgA testing is unreliable and IgG-based testing, such as deamidated gliadin peptide IgG, should be used instead for monitoring her response to treatment. Anti-mitochondrial antibody, anti-smooth muscle antibody, and rheumatoid factor are markers for other autoimmune conditions and are not used to monitor celiac disease activity."}, {"id": 355, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A physician evaluating a patient in a gastroenterology clinic for a suspected inflammatory or immune mediated bowel disorder considers the following question. A 22 year old woman with celiac disease asks about the risk of her children developing the disease. Which statement is most accurate regarding celiac disease genetics?", "options": {"A": "Celiac disease has no genetic component given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette.", "B": "First-degree relatives have an increased risk given the strong HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8 association.", "C": "Only sons can inherit celiac disease risk based on the clinical course and findings described above.", "D": "Celiac disease is caused by a single dominant gene with full penetrance.", "E": "Genetic testing alone can definitively diagnose celiac disease without any other testing."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (First-degree relatives have an increased risk given the strong HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8 association): Celiac disease has a strong genetic component through HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8, and first-degree relatives of an affected person have an increased risk of developing the disease themselves. It is not true that there is no genetic component, that only sons are at risk, or that a single dominant gene with full penetrance explains inheritance. HLA testing alone cannot confirm the diagnosis since these alleles are common in the general population and mainly help exclude the disease when absent."}, {"id": 358, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A 48 year old woman with celiac disease well controlled on a gluten-free diet for eight years is found to have new mild anemia on routine labs, but denies any new symptoms and her diet history is reliable. What is the most appropriate initial step?", "options": {"A": "Immediately assume lymphoma without further workup given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette.", "B": "Evaluate for other causes of anemia first, since celiac control appears good.", "C": "Stop the gluten-free diet to retest serology given this patient's overall clinical presentation and findings.", "D": "Refer directly for small bowel resection based on the history, exam, and diagnostic findings here.", "E": "No workup is needed since she is asymptomatic."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Evaluate for other causes of anemia first, since celiac control appears good): With a reliable history of good dietary control and no new alarming symptoms, it is reasonable to first evaluate other common causes of anemia before assuming a rare complication like lymphoma, though refractory disease should remain on the differential if other causes are not found. Immediately assuming lymphoma or proceeding to small bowel resection without further workup would be premature, stopping the gluten-free diet is not an appropriate diagnostic maneuver here, and mild new anemia still warrants some evaluation rather than none at all."}, {"id": 363, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A physician evaluating a patient in a gastroenterology clinic for a suspected inflammatory or immune mediated bowel disorder considers the following question. A clinician reviewing a case involving a patient with a chronic gastrointestinal condition affecting the bowel considers the following question. Which two histologic subtypes make up microscopic colitis?", "options": {"A": "Ischemic colitis and infectious colitis given this patient's overall clinical presentation and findings.", "B": "Ulcerative colitis and Crohn colitis given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette.", "C": "Collagenous colitis and lymphocytic colitis given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette.", "D": "Radiation colitis and diversion colitis given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette.", "E": "Pseudomembranous colitis and eosinophilic colitis given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Collagenous colitis and lymphocytic colitis given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette): Microscopic colitis includes two histologic subtypes, collagenous colitis, defined by a thickened subepithelial collagen band, and lymphocytic colitis, defined by increased intraepithelial lymphocytes, both with a normal-appearing colon on endoscopy. The other listed pairs describe distinct and separate conditions that are not subtypes of microscopic colitis."}, {"id": 369, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A physician evaluating a patient in a gastroenterology clinic for a suspected inflammatory or immune mediated bowel disorder considers the following question. A 24 year old man has recurrent sinopulmonary infections, chronic diarrhea, and low serum immunoglobulin levels. Small bowel biopsy shows nodular lymphoid hyperplasia. Which underlying condition should be considered?", "options": {"A": "Celiac disease based on the clinical course and findings described above.", "B": "Ulcerative colitis given the pattern of symptoms and findings described here.", "C": "Toxic megacolon given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette.", "D": "Common variable immunodeficiency given the pattern of symptoms and findings described here.", "E": "Dermatitis herpetiformis based on the clinical course and findings described above."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Common variable immunodeficiency given the pattern of symptoms and findings described here): Common variable immunodeficiency can produce a gastrointestinal picture overlapping with inflammatory bowel disease, including chronic diarrhea and nodular lymphoid hyperplasia, and should be suspected given the recurrent sinopulmonary infections and low immunoglobulin levels described. Celiac disease, ulcerative colitis, toxic megacolon, and dermatitis herpetiformis do not typically present with recurrent infections and hypogammaglobulinemia as core features."}, {"id": 371, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A physician evaluating a patient in a gastroenterology clinic for a suspected inflammatory or immune mediated bowel disorder considers the following question. Which finding on esophageal biopsy would most specifically support a diagnosis of eosinophilic esophagitis rather than gastroesophageal reflux disease?", "options": {"A": "A high density of eosinophils in the esophageal mucosa.", "B": "Basal cell hyperplasia alone given the pattern of symptoms and findings described here.", "C": "Barrett metaplasia given the findings and clinical context provided in this vignette.", "D": "Candida pseudohyphae given this patient's overall clinical presentation and findings.", "E": "Herpes viral inclusions based on the history, exam, and diagnostic findings here."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A high density of eosinophils in the esophageal mucosa): A high density of eosinophils in the esophageal mucosa, in the absence of another cause, is the finding that specifically supports eosinophilic esophagitis over reflux disease, which typically shows fewer eosinophils along with basal cell hyperplasia. Barrett metaplasia, Candida pseudohyphae, and herpes viral inclusions represent distinct conditions rather than eosinophilic esophagitis."}, {"id": 372, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A physician evaluating a patient in a gastroenterology clinic for a suspected inflammatory or immune mediated bowel disorder considers the following question. A 32 year old woman has chronic non-bloody watery diarrhea with a normal colonoscopy appearance. Her physician obtains random biopsies rather than relying on visual inspection alone. What is the rationale for this approach?", "options": {"A": "Microscopic colitis requires biopsy for diagnosis since the colon appears normal on visual inspection.", "B": "Microscopic colitis cannot be diagnosed without a grossly abnormal colon, so biopsies are unnecessary.", "C": "Random biopsies are never useful in colitis evaluation.", "D": "Visual inspection alone is always sufficient to diagnose all colitides.", "E": "Biopsies are only useful for detecting colorectal cancer."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Microscopic colitis requires biopsy for diagnosis since the colon appears normal on visual inspection): Microscopic colitis is defined by biopsy findings such as a thickened collagen band or increased intraepithelial lymphocytes in a colon that looks completely normal on visual inspection, so random biopsies are essential for diagnosis rather than optional. It would be incorrect to say biopsies are unnecessary, that they are never useful, that visual inspection alone is always sufficient, or that biopsies are useful only for cancer detection."}, {"id": 374, "domain": "ibd_immune_disorders", "question": "A physician evaluating a patient in a gastroenterology clinic for a suspected inflammatory or immune mediated bowel disorder considers the following question. A 45 year old man with Behcet disease and gastrointestinal ulceration is being compared to a similar patient with Crohn disease regarding perforation risk. Which statement is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Behcet ulcers, despite being fewer in number, carry a higher perforation risk than typical Crohn ulcers.", "B": "Behcet ulcers have a lower perforation risk than typical Crohn ulcers.", "C": "Both conditions carry identical perforation risk in all cases.", "D": "Perforation risk is not relevant to either condition.", "E": "Crohn ulcers always perforate more often than Behcet ulcers."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Behcet ulcers, despite being fewer in number, carry a higher perforation risk than typical Crohn ulcers): Behcet disease intestinal ulcers, while fewer in number than typical Crohn ulcers, tend to be deeper and carry a higher risk of perforation, an important distinguishing feature on the exam. It would be incorrect to say Behcet ulcers have a lower perforation risk, that risk is identical or irrelevant in both conditions, or that Crohn ulcers always perforate more often than Behcet ulcers."}, {"id": 385, "domain": "motility_disorders", "question": "A 67-year-old woman has new dysphagia and 20 pound weight loss over six weeks. Manometry shows an elevated integrated relaxation pressure and absent peristalsis, mimicking achalasia. What should be prioritized before starting achalasia-specific therapy?", "options": {"A": "Immediate pneumatic dilation to relieve the outflow obstruction.", "B": "Empiric botulinum toxin injection into the lower sphincter.", "C": "Initiation of a proton pump inhibitor and reassessment in six months.", "D": "Careful endoscopic and imaging evaluation to exclude pseudoachalasia from malignancy.", "E": "Referral for peroral endoscopic myotomy without further workup."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (careful endoscopic and imaging evaluation to exclude pseudoachalasia from malignancy): rapid weight loss and a short symptom course in an older patient raise concern for pseudoachalasia from a tumor infiltrating the gastroesophageal junction, which must be excluded before achalasia-specific therapy. Proceeding directly to dilation, botulinum toxin, acid suppression, or myotomy without this evaluation risks missing a malignant cause that requires entirely different management. Endoscopic ultrasound or cross-sectional imaging is often needed when suspicion is high."}, {"id": 386, "domain": "motility_disorders", "question": "A patient with confirmed type III achalasia and prominent spastic contractions asks about surgical options. Which procedure allows a longer myotomy that can extend into the spastic esophageal body and is often preferred for this subtype?", "options": {"A": "Nissen fundoplication without myotomy.", "B": "Standard pneumatic balloon dilation alone.", "C": "Peroral endoscopic myotomy.", "D": "Botulinum toxin injection only.", "E": "Esophagectomy as first-line therapy."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (peroral endoscopic myotomy): this technique allows the surgeon to extend the myotomy well beyond the standard length used in laparoscopic Heller myotomy, making it particularly suited to type III achalasia where spastic contractions extend up the esophageal body. Pneumatic dilation and botulinum toxin do not address the extended spastic segment. Fundoplication without myotomy and esophagectomy are not appropriate first-line options for this subtype."}, {"id": 387, "domain": "motility_disorders", "question": "A frail 81-year-old man with achalasia has multiple comorbidities and is a poor candidate for myotomy or dilation. Which treatment is most appropriate given that its effect is known to wear off over several months?", "options": {"A": "Botulinum toxin injection into the lower esophageal sphincter.", "B": "Laparoscopic Heller myotomy.", "C": "Peroral endoscopic myotomy.", "D": "Esophageal stenting for permanent relief.", "E": "Long-term proton pump inhibitor therapy."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (botulinum toxin injection into the lower esophageal sphincter): its effect predictably wears off over months, requiring repeat injections, which makes it best suited for frail patients who cannot tolerate more durable procedures like myotomy or dilation. Heller myotomy and peroral endoscopic myotomy carry more procedural risk in a frail patient. Esophageal stenting is not standard therapy for achalasia, and proton pump inhibitors do not address the underlying motor disorder."}, {"id": 389, "domain": "motility_disorders", "question": "A 55-year-old woman is diagnosed with achalasia after manometry confirms elevated integrated relaxation pressure with absent peristalsis. Before proceeding to myotomy, which additional structural evaluation is routinely performed to exclude a mass at the junction?", "options": {"A": "Colonoscopy with terminal ileal intubation.", "B": "Upper endoscopy with careful retroflexed view of the cardia.", "C": "Small bowel follow through series.", "D": "Hepatobiliary iminodiacetic acid scan.", "E": "Magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (upper endoscopy with careful retroflexed view of the cardia): a focused retroflexed endoscopic examination of the gastroesophageal junction is the routine structural step to exclude an infiltrating mass before committing to myotomy. Colonoscopy, a small bowel follow through series, a hepatobiliary scan, and magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography all evaluate structures unrelated to the esophagogastric junction and would not identify a mass causing pseudoachalasia."}, {"id": 400, "domain": "motility_disorders", "question": "A 62-year-old man has dysphagia for solids only, without liquid dysphagia, and manometry is entirely normal. Which of these is the most likely explanation, distinguishing his presentation from a primary esophageal motility disorder?", "options": {"A": "A mechanical lesion such as a ring, web, or stricture is more likely than a motility disorder.", "B": "Type I achalasia is most likely given normal manometry.", "C": "Jackhammer esophagus is most likely given normal manometry.", "D": "Distal esophageal spasm is confirmed by the normal study.", "E": "Ineffective esophageal motility is confirmed by the normal study."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (a mechanical lesion such as a ring, web, or stricture is more likely than a motility disorder): dysphagia limited to solids with normal manometry is the classic pattern of a mechanical narrowing rather than a primary motor disorder, since motility disorders typically also affect liquid transit to some degree. Achalasia, jackhammer esophagus, distal esophageal spasm, and ineffective motility would all be expected to show an abnormal manometric pattern, which is absent here by definition."}, {"id": 405, "domain": "motility_disorders", "question": "A patient with severe refractory gastroparesis has failed dietary therapy, glycemic optimization, and a short course of metoclopramide, with recurrent hospitalizations for dehydration and weight loss. Which intervention is most appropriate at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Gastric electrical stimulation or consideration of a jejunal feeding tube.", "B": "Repeating the same metoclopramide course for a longer duration.", "C": "Immediate total gastrectomy.", "D": "Switching to daily intravenous opioids for nausea control.", "E": "Discharging with reassurance and no further intervention."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (gastric electrical stimulation or consideration of a jejunal feeding tube): once dietary therapy, glycemic optimization, and prokinetic therapy have failed with recurrent hospitalizations, escalation to device-based or enteral feeding options is appropriate. Repeating the same metoclopramide course ignores its boxed warning and limited long-term utility. Total gastrectomy is far too aggressive, daily intravenous opioids would worsen gastrointestinal motility, and discharging without further intervention abandons a patient with a serious refractory condition."}, {"id": 411, "domain": "motility_disorders", "question": "A patient develops crampy abdominal pain, flushing, tachycardia, and diarrhea within twenty minutes of eating, three months after Roux-en-Y gastric bypass. Which mechanism best explains these early symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Delayed gastric emptying causing bacterial overgrowth.", "B": "Rapid fluid shift into the small bowel from hyperosmolar chyme causing early dumping.", "C": "Reactive hypoglycemia from an exaggerated insulin response.", "D": "Bile acid malabsorption alone.", "E": "Anastomotic stricture causing partial obstruction."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (rapid fluid shift into the small bowel from hyperosmolar chyme causing early dumping): this osmotic mechanism explains the cramping, flushing, tachycardia, and diarrhea occurring within twenty minutes of eating after bypass surgery. Delayed gastric emptying and bacterial overgrowth would produce a different symptom pattern with a different time course. Reactive hypoglycemia describes late rather than early dumping, and an anastomotic stricture would cause obstructive symptoms rather than this classic early dumping presentation."}, {"id": 415, "domain": "motility_disorders", "question": "A patient nine months after sleeve gastrectomy reports both early postprandial cramping with flushing and, separately, episodes of confusion and sweating two to three hours after meals. What is the most likely unifying explanation?", "options": {"A": "Two unrelated conditions occurring coincidentally.", "B": "An undiagnosed pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor is the most likely cause.", "C": "Isolated gastroparesis alone.", "D": "Isolated irritable bowel syndrome alone.", "E": "Combined early and late dumping syndrome from the altered gastric anatomy."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (combined early and late dumping syndrome from the altered gastric anatomy): early postprandial cramping with flushing reflects early dumping, while later confusion and sweating reflect late dumping, and both commonly coexist after altered gastric anatomy from sleeve gastrectomy. Treating these as two unrelated conditions misses the unifying mechanism. Isolated gastroparesis or irritable bowel syndrome would not explain both distinct timing patterns, and a neuroendocrine tumor is a much less likely explanation than the well-recognized postsurgical dumping pattern."}, {"id": 417, "domain": "motility_disorders", "question": "A patient is recovering on postoperative day two after an open laparotomy and has minimal bowel sounds with mild distention. What is the most appropriate initial interpretation and management?", "options": {"A": "This indicates Ogilvie syndrome requiring immediate neostigmine.", "B": "This is expected postoperative ileus, and supportive care with electrolyte correction and early ambulation is appropriate.", "C": "This confirms mechanical obstruction requiring surgery.", "D": "This confirms chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction.", "E": "This requires immediate colonoscopic decompression."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (this is expected postoperative ileus, and supportive care with electrolyte correction and early ambulation is appropriate): mild distention with minimal bowel sounds on postoperative day two after a laparotomy is within the expected course of normal postoperative ileus. Diagnosing Ogilvie syndrome, mechanical obstruction, or chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction at this early stage is premature, and immediate colonoscopic decompression is not indicated for expected, self-limited postoperative ileus."}, {"id": 419, "domain": "motility_disorders", "question": "A bedbound hospitalized patient develops marked colonic dilation without a mechanical lesion on imaging, consistent with acute colonic pseudo-obstruction. At what cecal diameter does the risk of perforation become significantly more concerning?", "options": {"A": "Six centimeters.", "B": "Eight centimeters.", "C": "Twelve centimeters.", "D": "Twenty centimeters.", "E": "Four centimeters."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (twelve centimeters): cecal diameter exceeding twelve centimeters is the widely cited threshold at which perforation risk becomes significantly more concerning in acute colonic pseudo-obstruction. Six, eight, and four centimeters are within or near the upper range of normal, and twenty centimeters is well beyond the threshold typically used to escalate management."}, {"id": 425, "domain": "motility_disorders", "question": "A patient with mitochondrial disease develops recurrent pseudo-obstructive episodes. Family asks whether a curative surgery exists. What is the most accurate response regarding the underlying disease process?", "options": {"A": "Total colectomy is curative in most cases.", "B": "There is no cure for the underlying enteric neuromuscular disease, so management is supportive and nutritional.", "C": "Achalasia-type myotomy will resolve the small bowel disease.", "D": "A single course of neostigmine is curative.", "E": "Antibiotic therapy alone reliably cures the condition."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (there is no cure for the underlying enteric neuromuscular disease, so management is supportive and nutritional): chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction from conditions like mitochondrial disease reflects diffuse neuromuscular dysfunction that cannot be surgically corrected. Total colectomy, achalasia-type myotomy, a single neostigmine course, and antibiotics alone do not cure the underlying diffuse enteric disease process, and setting that expectation accurately is important for family counseling."}, {"id": 426, "domain": "motility_disorders", "question": "A patient reports a sensation of incomplete evacuation and excessive straining with bowel movements. Anorectal manometry shows paradoxical contraction of the puborectalis and external anal sphincter during attempted defecation. Which diagnosis fits best?", "options": {"A": "Dyssynergic defecation.", "B": "Hirschsprung disease.", "C": "Chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction.", "D": "Acute colonic pseudo-obstruction.", "E": "Rectal prolapse."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (dyssynergic defecation): paradoxical contraction of the puborectalis and external anal sphincter during attempted defecation, causing incomplete evacuation and excessive straining, defines this functional disorder. Hirschsprung disease involves aganglionosis typically diagnosed earlier in life with different manometric findings. Chronic and acute pseudo-obstruction involve the small bowel or colon more diffusely, and rectal prolapse is a distinct structural diagnosis."}, {"id": 448, "domain": "gut_brain_disorders", "question": "A patient newly diagnosed with functional dyspepsia has not yet been tested for Helicobacter pylori. Why is testing and treatment for this organism recommended for essentially all dyspepsia patients?", "options": {"A": "It is required only in patients over 60 years old.", "B": "Eradication cures a subset of patients who actually have Helicobacter pylori-related dyspepsia rather than true functional disease.", "C": "It has no bearing on dyspepsia management and can be skipped.", "D": "It is only relevant if alarm features are present.", "E": "It replaces the need for endoscopy entirely."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (eradication cures a subset of patients who actually have Helicobacter pylori-related dyspepsia rather than true functional disease): testing and treating essentially all dyspepsia patients for this organism is recommended because a meaningful subset of apparent functional dyspepsia is actually attributable to the infection and resolves with eradication. This recommendation is not limited to patients over 60, it is not only relevant when alarm features are present, it is not something that can simply be skipped, and it does not replace the need for endoscopy when otherwise indicated."}, {"id": 449, "domain": "gut_brain_disorders", "question": "A patient with functional dyspepsia dominated by meal-related fullness and early satiety has not responded to an adequate trial of a prokinetic agent. Which second-line class of medication has randomized trial support in this condition?", "options": {"A": "Low-dose tricyclic antidepressants.", "B": "High-dose proton pump inhibitors added on top.", "C": "Long-term antibiotics.", "D": "Oral corticosteroids.", "E": "Loop diuretics."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (low-dose tricyclic antidepressants): randomized trial data support tricyclic antidepressants as effective second-line therapy across both functional dyspepsia subtypes when a prokinetic trial has failed. Adding a higher dose proton pump inhibitor on top does not address the underlying visceral hypersensitivity, and long-term antibiotics, oral corticosteroids, and loop diuretics have no established role in functional dyspepsia management."}, {"id": 457, "domain": "gut_brain_disorders", "question": "A child has recurrent paroxysmal episodes of moderate to severe periumbilical pain lasting hours, accompanied by headache, photophobia, and nausea, with a family history of migraine. Which diagnosis fits best?", "options": {"A": "Centrally mediated abdominal pain syndrome of adulthood.", "B": "Irritable bowel syndrome.", "C": "Functional dyspepsia.", "D": "Cyclic vomiting syndrome without migraine features.", "E": "Abdominal migraine."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (abdominal migraine): recurrent paroxysmal periumbilical pain with headache, photophobia, nausea, and a family history of migraine in a child is the classic presentation of abdominal migraine. Centrally mediated abdominal pain syndrome of adulthood does not typically present in childhood with these migrainous features. Functional dyspepsia, cyclic vomiting syndrome without migraine features, and irritable bowel syndrome do not capture the specific migraine-associated symptom cluster described."}, {"id": 459, "domain": "gut_brain_disorders", "question": "A patient is confirmed to have narcotic bowel syndrome after escalating opioids worsened rather than improved chronic abdominal pain. What is the cornerstone of appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Further opioid dose escalation under closer supervision.", "B": "Gradual supervised opioid tapering combined with neuromodulator support.", "C": "Immediate opioid discontinuation without any tapering or support.", "D": "Switching to a different opioid at an equivalent dose.", "E": "Adding a second opioid with a different mechanism."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (gradual supervised opioid tapering combined with neuromodulator support): this combined approach addresses both the causative opioid exposure and the underlying altered pain processing in narcotic bowel syndrome. Further dose escalation or switching to a different opioid would perpetuate the same problem. Abrupt discontinuation without support risks withdrawal and poor tolerability, and adding a second opioid with a different mechanism does not address the core pathophysiology."}, {"id": 461, "domain": "gut_brain_disorders", "question": "Which of the following best distinguishes functional biliary-like pain from calculous biliary disease?", "options": {"A": "It always presents with jaundice and fever.", "B": "Episodic biliary-type pain occurs without gallstones or significant structural biliary or pancreatic abnormality.", "C": "It is diagnosed only by liver biopsy.", "D": "It requires elevated bilirubin for diagnosis.", "E": "It is always associated with a dilated common bile duct."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (episodic biliary-type pain occurs without gallstones or significant structural biliary or pancreatic abnormality): this absence of stones or significant structural disease is what distinguishes functional biliary-like pain from calculous biliary disease. It does not always present with jaundice and fever, which would suggest cholangitis instead. It is not diagnosed by liver biopsy, it does not require elevated bilirubin, and it is not always associated with a dilated common bile duct."}, {"id": 466, "domain": "gut_brain_disorders", "question": "A 45-year-old man with diarrhea-predominant irritable bowel syndrome reports new nocturnal diarrhea, five pounds of unintentional weight loss, and a family history of ulcerative colitis. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Continue current symptomatic therapy without any additional evaluation.", "B": "Order gastric emptying scintigraphy.", "C": "Start loperamide alone and reassess in six months.", "D": "Diagnose irritable bowel syndrome and discharge with reassurance.", "E": "Proceed to colonoscopy given the presence of alarm features."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (proceed to colonoscopy given the presence of alarm features): nocturnal diarrhea, unintentional weight loss, and a family history of ulcerative colitis are all alarm features that require colonoscopic evaluation rather than assuming irritable bowel syndrome. Continuing symptomatic therapy, starting loperamide alone, or discharging with reassurance would inappropriately overlook these red flags, and gastric emptying scintigraphy does not address the lower gastrointestinal alarm features present."}, {"id": 467, "domain": "gut_brain_disorders", "question": "Which behavioral therapy has strong evidence for improving global symptoms in irritable bowel syndrome, working through central pain processing rather than direct effects on gut motility?", "options": {"A": "Standard supportive counseling with no specific gut focus.", "B": "Gut-directed cognitive behavioral therapy or hypnotherapy.", "C": "Exposure therapy for unrelated phobias.", "D": "Group therapy focused solely on dietary education.", "E": "Psychoanalysis without behavioral components."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (gut-directed cognitive behavioral therapy or hypnotherapy): these specific behavioral interventions have strong evidence for improving global irritable bowel syndrome symptoms by acting through central pain processing pathways. Standard supportive counseling without a gut-specific focus, exposure therapy for unrelated phobias, generic group dietary education, and psychoanalysis without behavioral components do not have the same targeted evidence base in this condition."}, {"id": 473, "domain": "gut_brain_disorders", "question": "A patient meets Rome IV criteria for irritable bowel syndrome. Which two additional features, beyond frequency and duration, must be associated with the pain for at least two of them to be present?", "options": {"A": "Related to eating only, with no bowel movement association required.", "B": "Related to defecation, or a change in stool frequency, or a change in stool form.", "C": "Associated with fever and weight loss.", "D": "Associated with jaundice and pruritus.", "E": "Related to menstrual cycle exclusively."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (related to defecation, or a change in stool frequency, or a change in stool form): Rome IV requires at least two of these three associated features alongside the frequency and duration criteria to diagnose irritable bowel syndrome. Pain related to eating alone without any bowel movement association does not satisfy this requirement. Fever, weight loss, jaundice, and pruritus are alarm features suggesting an alternative diagnosis rather than part of the Rome IV criteria themselves, and pain limited strictly to menses does not meet the criteria either."}, {"id": 476, "domain": "gut_brain_disorders", "question": "A patient with mixed-type irritable bowel syndrome has predominant abdominal pain with alternating bowel habits. Which class of medication is useful across all subtypes specifically for pain control?", "options": {"A": "Antispasmodics and low-dose tricyclic antidepressants.", "B": "High-dose stimulant laxatives only.", "C": "Loperamide as monotherapy for pain.", "D": "Systemic corticosteroids.", "E": "Proton pump inhibitors."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (antispasmodics and low-dose tricyclic antidepressants): both are useful across all irritable bowel syndrome subtypes specifically for pain control, regardless of the predominant bowel habit. High-dose stimulant laxatives and loperamide as pain monotherapy address bowel habit rather than pain directly. Systemic corticosteroids and proton pump inhibitors have no established role in treating irritable bowel syndrome pain."}, {"id": 480, "domain": "gut_brain_disorders", "question": "A patient with functional dyspepsia overlapping with irritable bowel syndrome with constipation asks about combined symptom management. Which approach addresses both bloating-type discomfort and slow transit together as a reasonable starting point?", "options": {"A": "Strict fasting for symptom control.", "B": "Immediate colectomy.", "C": "Long-term antibiotics as first-line therapy.", "D": "High-dose opioid antidiarrheal therapy.", "E": "Soluble fiber and osmotic laxatives with dietary modification, escalating to gut-specific agents if needed."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (soluble fiber and osmotic laxatives with dietary modification, escalating to gut-specific agents if needed): this stepwise approach reasonably addresses both bloating-type discomfort from functional dyspepsia and slow transit from constipation-predominant irritable bowel syndrome together. Immediate colectomy is drastically disproportionate, long-term antibiotics as first-line therapy is not appropriate for this overlap presentation, high-dose opioid antidiarrheal therapy would worsen constipation, and strict fasting is not a sustainable or evidence-based management strategy."}, {"id": 483, "domain": "gut_brain_disorders", "question": "A patient with severe constipation-predominant irritable bowel syndrome has failed fiber, osmotic laxatives, and a secretagogue. Anorectal manometry and balloon expulsion are normal. What should be considered next?", "options": {"A": "Diagnosing Hirschsprung disease and referring for surgery.", "B": "Immediate subtotal colectomy without further trials.", "C": "Diagnosing dyssynergic defecation despite normal manometry.", "D": "Stopping all medical therapy and observing only.", "E": "Reassessing for an overlapping slow-transit component or escalating medical therapy further before considering surgery."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (reassessing for an overlapping slow-transit component or escalating medical therapy further before considering surgery): when standard constipation-predominant therapies fail and anorectal function is normal, reassessing for a slow-transit component and further optimizing medical therapy is appropriate before considering more invasive options. Immediate subtotal colectomy skips reasonable intermediate steps, diagnosing dyssynergic defecation despite normal manometry is inconsistent with the testing, stopping all therapy abandons the patient, and Hirschsprung disease is a pediatric-onset diagnosis not newly appearing in an adult with normal anorectal manometry."}, {"id": 485, "domain": "gut_brain_disorders", "question": "Which two conditions together make up the classic functional anorectal pain disorders described in the Rome IV framework, distinguished mainly by episode duration?", "options": {"A": "Chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction and Ogilvie syndrome.", "B": "Dyssynergic defecation and fecal incontinence.", "C": "Levator ani syndrome and proctalgia fugax.", "D": "Functional dyspepsia and functional heartburn.", "E": "Abdominal migraine and cyclic vomiting syndrome."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (levator ani syndrome and proctalgia fugax): these two conditions make up the classic Rome IV functional anorectal pain disorders, distinguished mainly by episode duration, with levator ani syndrome lasting minutes to hours and proctalgia fugax lasting seconds to a few minutes. Dyssynergic defecation and fecal incontinence are disorders of defecation and continence rather than pain syndromes. Chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction, Ogilvie syndrome, functional dyspepsia, functional heartburn, abdominal migraine, and cyclic vomiting syndrome are unrelated diagnoses."}, {"id": 488, "domain": "gut_brain_disorders", "question": "A patient with brief, self-terminating episodes of severe rectal pain lasting under a minute asks if any medication can help since episodes are now occurring twice weekly. What is a reasonable option to trial?", "options": {"A": "Systemic corticosteroids.", "B": "Long-term oral opioids.", "C": "Topical nitrates or calcium channel blockers for frequent episodes.", "D": "Sacral nerve stimulation as first-line therapy.", "E": "Immediate surgical referral."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (topical nitrates or calcium channel blockers for frequent episodes): these smooth muscle relaxants are a reasonable pharmacologic option when proctalgia fugax episodes become frequent enough to warrant more than reassurance alone. Long-term oral opioids and systemic corticosteroids are disproportionate for this brief, self-limited condition, sacral nerve stimulation is not first-line therapy here, and immediate surgical referral is not indicated for a benign functional pain syndrome."}, {"id": 490, "domain": "gut_brain_disorders", "question": "Which statement best summarizes the shared underlying concept linking centrally mediated abdominal pain syndrome, narcotic bowel syndrome, and functional anorectal pain?", "options": {"A": "All three require a demonstrable structural lesion for diagnosis.", "B": "All three reflect disordered central or peripheral pain processing rather than an identifiable structural or inflammatory gut lesion.", "C": "All three are cured definitively by surgery.", "D": "All three are diagnosed only by elevated inflammatory markers.", "E": "All three require lifelong opioid therapy for adequate control."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (all three reflect disordered central or peripheral pain processing rather than an identifiable structural or inflammatory gut lesion): centrally mediated abdominal pain syndrome, narcotic bowel syndrome, and functional anorectal pain all share this underlying concept of altered pain processing without a demonstrable structural or inflammatory lesion. None of the three require a structural lesion for diagnosis, none are reliably cured by surgery, none are diagnosed by elevated inflammatory markers, and lifelong opioid therapy is neither required nor appropriate, since it can actually worsen narcotic bowel syndrome specifically."}, {"id": 491, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A 58 year old man with no prior colonoscopy undergoes his first screening exam. During withdrawal through the sigmoid colon, the endoscopist identifies the mucosal lesion pictured, sitting on a long stalk projecting into the lumen. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management during this same procedure?", "options": {"A": "Leave the polyp untouched and repeat colonoscopy again in one year.", "B": "Refer the patient directly for elective segmental colon resection surgery.", "C": "Perform snare polypectomy of the lesion during this same procedure.", "D": "Obtain a CT colonography study before attempting any intervention.", "E": "Tattoo the polyp site only and defer removal to a later session."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Perform snare polypectomy of the lesion during this same procedure): A pedunculated polyp found on screening colonoscopy should be removed at the time it is seen using snare polypectomy, since this is both diagnostic and therapeutic. Leaving it in place risks continued growth and malignant transformation through the adenoma to carcinoma sequence. Surgical resection is reserved for polyps that cannot be removed endoscopically or that show invasive cancer beyond endoscopic control. CT colonography and delayed removal add risk and cost without benefit for an accessible pedunculated lesion.", "image": "question_images/q_491.jpg"}, {"id": 493, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A 64 year old man with 15 years of poorly controlled reflux and known Barrett esophagus presents with progressive solid food dysphagia and 20 pound weight loss. Upper endoscopy shows the finding pictured at the gastroesophageal junction. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma arising from the mid esophagus.", "B": "Eosinophilic esophagitis presenting with a new fibrotic esophageal stricture.", "C": "A benign peptic stricture developing from chronic reflux disease alone.", "D": "Esophageal leiomyoma arising from the muscularis propria layer.", "E": "Esophageal adenocarcinoma arising specifically at the gastroesophageal junction area."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Esophageal adenocarcinoma arising specifically at the gastroesophageal junction area): Long-standing Barrett esophagus from chronic reflux is the dominant risk factor for esophageal adenocarcinoma, which typically arises at the gastroesophageal junction or distal esophagus, matching this patient's location and presentation with progressive dysphagia and weight loss. Squamous cell carcinoma is more linked to tobacco, alcohol, and the mid or upper esophagus, not Barrett changes. Eosinophilic esophagitis and benign peptic strictures do not produce a mass lesion or this degree of weight loss, and leiomyoma is a submucosal benign lesion without ulceration or invasive features.", "image": "question_images/q_493.jpg"}, {"id": 495, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A 71 year old woman reports six months of early satiety, unintentional 25 pound weight loss, and vague epigastric discomfort. Upper endoscopy demonstrates the finding shown in the gastric body. Biopsies confirm malignancy. Which histologic subtype would most likely explain a rigid, thickened stomach on imaging if present?", "options": {"A": "Intestinal type adenocarcinoma showing well-formed gland formation on biopsy.", "B": "Gastric mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue lymphoma arising from chronic gastritis.", "C": "Gastrointestinal stromal tumor arising from the gastric wall muscle.", "D": "Diffuse type adenocarcinoma composed of poorly cohesive signet ring cells.", "E": "Fundic gland polyp showing only focal low-grade dysplasia."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Diffuse type adenocarcinoma composed of poorly cohesive signet ring cells): Diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma, composed of poorly cohesive signet ring cells infiltrating the gastric wall, classically produces a rigid, thickened stomach described as linitis plastica, unlike the gland-forming intestinal type. Intestinal type forms discrete glandular structures and tends to occur as a more localized ulcerative or fungating mass rather than diffuse wall thickening. MALT lymphoma and GIST have distinct histologic appearances and do not typically cause linitis plastica. A fundic gland polyp is a benign lesion and would not present with weight loss or an ulcerated mass.", "image": "question_images/q_495.jpg"}, {"id": 497, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A 45 year old man is diagnosed with colon cancer, and tumor testing shows loss of mismatch repair protein expression. Genetic counseling confirms a germline MLH1 mutation. His sister asks which extracolonic cancer she should be screened for most closely given this same inherited condition.", "options": {"A": "Endometrial cancer, the most common extracolonic malignancy in this syndrome.", "B": "Follicular thyroid cancer, which is not part of this syndrome.", "C": "Adrenocortical carcinoma, an unrelated and unassociated malignancy here.", "D": "Testicular germ cell tumor, which carries no known link here.", "E": "Renal cell carcinoma, which is not part of this tumor spectrum."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Endometrial cancer, the most common extracolonic malignancy in this syndrome): Lynch syndrome, caused by mismatch repair gene mutations such as MLH1, carries the highest extracolonic cancer risk for endometrial cancer among women with this syndrome. The other listed cancers are not part of the classic Lynch syndrome tumor spectrum. Ovarian, gastric, urothelial, and biliary cancers are also elevated in Lynch syndrome, but endometrial cancer is the single most common extracolonic malignancy. Recognizing this association is why women with Lynch syndrome are counseled about gynecologic surveillance and risk-reducing surgery."}, {"id": 506, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A 22 year old with genetically confirmed FAP and hundreds of colonic adenomas on recent colonoscopy is being counseled about surgical timing by his surgical team. Which factor most strongly supports proceeding with proctocolectomy in the near future rather than continuing surveillance colonoscopy alone for several more years?", "options": {"A": "A normal complete blood count on recent labs.", "B": "The patient's personal preference to avoid surgery indefinitely.", "C": "The presence of hundreds of polyps with near-universal cancer risk by age 40 if untreated.", "D": "A recent colonoscopy showing polyps smaller than 5 millimeters.", "E": "Absence of any family history of colon cancer."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The presence of hundreds of polyps with near-universal cancer risk by age 40 if untreated): FAP with hundreds of adenomatous polyps carries essentially universal colorectal cancer risk by age 40 without colectomy, which is why proctocolectomy is recommended in the late teens to twenties rather than continued surveillance alone. A normal blood count or small polyp size does not change the underlying genetic cancer risk. Patient preference matters in shared decision making but does not eliminate the medical indication for surgery given the natural history of untreated FAP."}, {"id": 514, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A 60 year old man with biopsy-proven Barrett esophagus and no dysplasia on two separate endoscopies performed one year apart is being scheduled for his next surveillance exam by his gastroenterologist. Based on current guidelines for nondysplastic disease, what is the appropriate interval before his next surveillance endoscopy?", "options": {"A": "Every 6 months indefinitely regardless of biopsy results.", "B": "Every 10 years, the interval used for average risk colorectal screening.", "C": "Only once symptoms such as dysphagia eventually develop.", "D": "Annually, regardless of what the biopsy findings actually show.", "E": "Every 3 to 5 years for nondysplastic Barrett esophagus."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Every 3 to 5 years for nondysplastic Barrett esophagus): Nondysplastic Barrett esophagus is typically monitored with surveillance endoscopy every 3 to 5 years, reflecting the relatively low but nonzero annual progression rate to dysplasia and cancer. Every 6 months is far more frequent than needed for nondysplastic disease, a 10 year interval is used for average risk colorectal screening rather than Barrett surveillance, and waiting for dysphagia would miss the window for early detection since symptoms often indicate more advanced disease."}, {"id": 515, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A 55 year old man with Barrett esophagus undergoes surveillance endoscopy that identifies a visible nodular area within the Barrett segment. Biopsies from this nodule confirm high-grade dysplasia without evidence of invasive carcinoma on the samples obtained. What is the most appropriate management approach for this specific finding?", "options": {"A": "Observation alone with a repeat biopsy performed in three years.", "B": "Immediate esophagectomy performed without any endoscopic therapy first.", "C": "Proton pump inhibitor therapy alone without any endoscopic intervention.", "D": "Systemic chemotherapy alone without addressing the local dysplastic tissue.", "E": "Endoscopic resection of the nodule followed by radiofrequency ablation."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Endoscopic resection of the nodule followed by radiofrequency ablation): Confirmed high-grade dysplasia, especially with a visible nodular area, is treated with endoscopic eradication therapy, combining resection of the nodularity to rule out invasive cancer with radiofrequency ablation of the remaining flat Barrett segment. Observation alone risks progression to invasive cancer. Immediate esophagectomy is more invasive than needed when endoscopic therapy can achieve eradication. Medical therapy alone does not address the dysplastic tissue itself."}, {"id": 518, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A 58 year old on long-term proton pump inhibitor therapy for reflux undergoes surveillance endoscopy, which reveals several small polyps scattered throughout the gastric body and fundus. Biopsies show no dysplasia. Which gastric polyp type is most strongly associated with chronic proton pump inhibitor use in this setting?", "options": {"A": "Hyperplastic polyp, associated instead with chronic gastritis from H. pylori.", "B": "Fundic gland polyp, strongly linked to chronic PPI therapy.", "C": "Adenomatous polyp, carrying the highest malignant potential instead.", "D": "Inflammatory fibroid polyp, arising from submucosal tissue instead.", "E": "Peutz-Jeghers hamartomatous polyp, seen in a distinct hereditary syndrome."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Fundic gland polyp, strongly linked to chronic PPI therapy): Fundic gland polyps are the most common gastric polyp type and are strongly associated with chronic proton pump inhibitor use, appearing as small multiple lesions in the gastric body and fundus. Hyperplastic polyps relate to chronic gastritis, often from H. pylori, rather than PPI use. Adenomatous polyps carry the highest malignant potential of gastric polyps but are not linked to PPI therapy. Inflammatory fibroid polyps and hamartomatous polyps have different underlying causes."}, {"id": 524, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A pathologist reviewing a resected gastric mass performs immunohistochemical staining to help confirm a suspected diagnosis of gastrointestinal stromal tumor arising from the interstitial cells of Cajal. Which immunohistochemical marker is most useful to confirm this specific diagnosis on the tissue sample?", "options": {"A": "CD20, a marker used instead to identify B cell lymphoma.", "B": "Chromogranin A, a marker used instead for neuroendocrine tumors.", "C": "Cytokeratin 7, a general epithelial marker rather than this tumor.", "D": "CD117 (KIT), confirming the interstitial cell of Cajal origin.", "E": "CA 19-9, a serum marker rather than a tissue stain."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (CD117 (KIT), confirming the interstitial cell of Cajal origin): Gastrointestinal stromal tumors arise from the interstitial cells of Cajal and carry activating KIT or PDGFRA mutations, confirmed by CD117 immunohistochemistry. CD20 marks B cells and would be used for lymphoma, chromogranin A marks neuroendocrine tumors, cytokeratin 7 is an epithelial marker, and CA 19-9 is a serum tumor marker rather than an immunohistochemical stain."}, {"id": 529, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A 40 year old with poorly controlled, long-standing celiac disease develops new abdominal pain and weight loss, and small bowel biopsy reveals a T cell lymphoma. Which specific small bowel lymphoma subtype is classically associated with this history of longstanding, poorly controlled celiac disease?", "options": {"A": "Follicular lymphoma, which is not linked to celiac disease.", "B": "Burkitt lymphoma, which has an entirely different associated risk factor.", "C": "Mantle cell lymphoma, unrelated to chronic celiac disease.", "D": "Hodgkin lymphoma, which does not show this specific association.", "E": "Enteropathy-associated T cell lymphoma, linked to longstanding celiac disease."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Enteropathy-associated T cell lymphoma, linked to longstanding celiac disease): Enteropathy-associated T cell lymphoma is a small bowel lymphoma linked to poorly controlled or long-standing celiac disease. Follicular, Burkitt, mantle cell, and Hodgkin lymphoma are not specifically associated with celiac disease and have different typical presentations and cell origins."}, {"id": 536, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A hepatologist is setting up a surveillance protocol for a newly diagnosed cirrhotic patient to detect hepatocellular carcinoma at an early, potentially curable stage. Which surveillance interval and imaging modality combination is recommended as the standard approach for this ongoing monitoring program?", "options": {"A": "MRI of the liver performed once every five years.", "B": "CT scan of the abdomen performed annually as the sole test.", "C": "An alpha-fetoprotein level checked alone every three months.", "D": "Ultrasound with or without alpha-fetoprotein performed every six months.", "E": "No routine surveillance is recommended for cirrhotic patients at all."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ultrasound with or without alpha-fetoprotein performed every six months): Surveillance in cirrhotic patients for hepatocellular carcinoma uses ultrasound, with or without alpha-fetoprotein, every 6 months, balancing detection sensitivity with cost and radiation exposure concerns. Annual CT and MRI are not the standard first-line surveillance tool. Alpha-fetoprotein alone lacks sufficient sensitivity as a standalone screening test, and cirrhotic patients absolutely require ongoing surveillance given their markedly elevated cancer risk."}, {"id": 537, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A cirrhotic patient undergoing routine six month surveillance ultrasound is found to have a new liver nodule. Follow-up contrast-enhanced MRI shows the nodule demonstrates arterial phase hyperenhancement with washout on the portal venous phase, meeting standardized radiologic diagnostic criteria. What is the most appropriate next step in this patient's care?", "options": {"A": "Perform a biopsy regardless of this already diagnostic imaging pattern.", "B": "Repeat the ultrasound in six months with no further action.", "C": "Assume the lesion is a hemangioma and simply observe it.", "D": "Diagnose hepatocellular carcinoma based on this classic imaging pattern alone.", "E": "Refer for immediate systemic chemotherapy without any formal staging workup."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Diagnose hepatocellular carcinoma based on this classic imaging pattern alone): In a cirrhotic patient, a liver nodule showing arterial phase hyperenhancement with washout on delayed phase imaging meets standardized radiologic criteria allowing a diagnosis of hepatocellular carcinoma without requiring biopsy, avoiding unnecessary procedural risk. Routine biopsy would add bleeding and tumor seeding risk when imaging is already diagnostic. Simply repeating ultrasound would delay appropriate staging and treatment planning, and hemangioma does not show this washout pattern."}, {"id": 539, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A pediatric surgeon recommends elective surgical excision of an asymptomatic choledochal cyst discovered incidentally in a young patient during imaging for unrelated symptoms, despite the absence of any current complications. Excision is recommended primarily to reduce the future risk of which specific malignancy?", "options": {"A": "Cholangiocarcinoma, the specific malignancy driving this surgical recommendation.", "B": "Hepatocellular carcinoma, which is not the specific concern here.", "C": "Gastric adenocarcinoma, which is unrelated to choledochal cyst risk.", "D": "Pancreatic adenocarcinoma, which is not the driving concern here.", "E": "Colorectal adenocarcinoma, which is unrelated to this specific risk."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cholangiocarcinoma, the specific malignancy driving this surgical recommendation): Choledochal cysts, though congenital malformations rather than true neoplasms, carry a significantly increased lifetime risk of cholangiocarcinoma, which is why most are surgically excised rather than simply observed once identified. The other cancers listed are not the specific driving concern behind choledochal cyst excision."}, {"id": 548, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A 45 year old asymptomatic patient undergoing abdominal MRI for an unrelated reason is incidentally found to have a 1.5 cm pancreatic branch duct IPMN without a mural nodule, without main duct dilation, and without any associated symptoms on further evaluation. What is the most appropriate management approach for this specific finding?", "options": {"A": "Immediate pancreaticoduodenectomy for this small, low-risk incidental cyst.", "B": "Total pancreatectomy performed without any further diagnostic workup.", "C": "Systemic chemotherapy started before obtaining any follow-up imaging.", "D": "Periodic surveillance imaging given the absence of worrisome features.", "E": "No follow-up imaging at all despite this incidental cystic finding."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Periodic surveillance imaging given the absence of worrisome features): Low-risk branch duct IPMN without worrisome features such as a mural nodule, dilated main duct, or symptoms can be followed with periodic surveillance imaging rather than immediate resection, since malignant risk is substantially lower than main duct involvement. Proceeding directly to major pancreatic surgery would expose the patient to unnecessary surgical risk, and abandoning follow-up entirely would miss a lesion that could still progress over time."}, {"id": 555, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A researcher describing the stepwise genetic progression from normal colonic mucosa to invasive colorectal cancer explains that one particular tumor suppressor gene mutation occurs earliest in this sequence, allowing epithelial cells to proliferate without the normal growth restraints. Which gene mutation is thought to initiate this classic sequence?", "options": {"A": "TP53, which is thought to act later, enabling invasion instead.", "B": "KRAS, which is thought to act after this initial mutation.", "C": "SMAD4, which is thought to act later in the sequence.", "D": "DCC, which is thought to act later in the sequence.", "E": "APC, allowing unchecked epithelial proliferation early in the sequence."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (APC, allowing unchecked epithelial proliferation early in the sequence): The adenoma to carcinoma sequence begins with APC loss, which allows unchecked epithelial proliferation, followed by KRAS mutation, then loss of chromosome 18q and DCC/SMAD4, and finally TP53 loss enabling invasion. This ordered progression underlies the multi-year timeline that supports colorectal cancer screening intervals."}, {"id": 558, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A pathology report on a resected colon cancer specimen documents tumor invasion through the bowel wall along with metastatic deposits found in two regional lymph nodes, but no evidence of spread to distant organs on staging imaging. Which colorectal cancer stage is defined by this specific combination of findings?", "options": {"A": "Distant organ metastasis, which instead defines stage IV disease.", "B": "Tumor confined to the mucosa only without any nodal involvement.", "C": "Regional lymph node metastasis without any distant organ spread.", "D": "Absence of any nodal or distant spread, seen in earlier stages.", "E": "Involvement of the peritoneum exclusively without nodal involvement."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Regional lymph node metastasis without any distant organ spread): Stage III colorectal cancer is defined by the presence of regional lymph node metastasis without distant spread, and this stage warrants adjuvant chemotherapy after surgical resection. Distant metastasis defines stage IV disease, tumor confined to the mucosa without nodal involvement is characteristic of very early stage disease, and peritoneal involvement alone does not define stage III by the standard TNM nodal criterion."}, {"id": 559, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A patient with newly diagnosed metastatic colorectal cancer undergoes tumor genotyping as part of routine treatment planning, and the results reveal a KRAS mutation within the tumor tissue. Her oncology team uses this specific result to guide selection among the available systemic therapy options. What is the treatment implication of this genotyping finding?", "options": {"A": "The tumor is guaranteed to respond well to anti-EGFR antibody therapy.", "B": "KRAS mutation status has no meaningful effect on treatment selection.", "C": "The tumor should receive checkpoint inhibitors as first-line therapy instead.", "D": "A KRAS mutation indicates microsatellite instability-high tumor status directly.", "E": "The tumor will not respond to anti-EGFR antibody therapy."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The tumor will not respond to anti-EGFR antibody therapy): RAS-mutated colorectal tumors, including KRAS mutations, do not respond to anti-EGFR antibody therapy, so this finding guides oncologists away from that class of targeted treatment. KRAS mutation status is a separate molecular feature from microsatellite instability status, and checkpoint inhibitors are specifically effective in microsatellite instability-high tumors rather than being guided by KRAS status."}, {"id": 560, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "An oncologist orders molecular testing on a patient's metastatic colorectal tumor before selecting systemic therapy, specifically looking for a biomarker known to predict a favorable response to immune checkpoint inhibitor treatment in this cancer type. Which molecular finding is being sought in this test?", "options": {"A": "KRAS wild-type status alone, which does not predict this response.", "B": "Microsatellite instability-high status, predicting favorable checkpoint inhibitor response.", "C": "Low tumor mutational burden, generally associated with poor response instead.", "D": "BRAF wild-type status alone, which does not predict this response.", "E": "Absence of any nodal metastasis, which does not predict this response."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Microsatellite instability-high status, predicting favorable checkpoint inhibitor response): Microsatellite instability-high tumors, often related to Lynch syndrome or sporadic mismatch repair deficiency, respond well to immune checkpoint inhibitors due to their high tumor mutational burden and resulting neoantigen load. KRAS or BRAF status alone does not predict checkpoint inhibitor response, low tumor mutational burden is generally associated with poor checkpoint inhibitor response, and nodal status does not determine immunotherapy response."}, {"id": 564, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A 70 year old undergoing routine screening colonoscopy is found to have an 8 millimeter submucosal-appearing rectal nodule. Endoscopic ultrasound confirms the lesion is confined to the submucosa without invasion of the muscularis propria, and biopsy identifies a well differentiated neuroendocrine tumor. What is the most appropriate management for this lesion?", "options": {"A": "Total proctocolectomy, an excessive approach for this small submucosal lesion.", "B": "Systemic chemotherapy without any local endoscopic or surgical therapy.", "C": "Whole pelvis radiation therapy directed at this small submucosal lesion.", "D": "Observation only, without any attempt at endoscopic removal.", "E": "Endoscopic resection given the small size and submucosal confinement."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Endoscopic resection given the small size and submucosal confinement): Small rectal neuroendocrine tumors under 1 cm confined to the submucosa carry a low risk of metastasis and can often be removed endoscopically, avoiding more invasive surgery. Larger tumors or those invading the muscularis propria would need more extensive surgical evaluation. Total proctocolectomy, systemic chemotherapy, and pelvic radiation are excessive for a small, low-risk, submucosal lesion, and simple observation without removal is not the accepted management for an accessible neuroendocrine tumor."}, {"id": 565, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A pathologist grading a resected gastrointestinal neuroendocrine tumor specimen uses a specific proliferation marker in combination with the mitotic count to classify the tumor as low, intermediate, or high grade. Which immunohistochemical marker is used alongside mitotic count for this grading system?", "options": {"A": "CA 19-9, a serum marker used for other GI cancers instead.", "B": "Alpha-fetoprotein, a serum marker used for hepatocellular carcinoma instead.", "C": "Ki-67 proliferation index, used together with mitotic count for grading.", "D": "CEA, a serum marker used for colorectal cancer monitoring instead.", "E": "CD117, the marker used for gastrointestinal stromal tumors instead."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ki-67 proliferation index, used together with mitotic count for grading): Grading of gastrointestinal neuroendocrine tumors uses the Ki-67 proliferation index along with mitotic count to classify tumors as low, intermediate, or high grade, correlating with behavior more reliably than size alone for many of these lesions. CA 19-9, alpha-fetoprotein, and CEA are serum tumor markers used in other GI cancers, and CD117 is the marker used for gastrointestinal stromal tumors, not neuroendocrine grading."}, {"id": 567, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A nuclear medicine physician explains that a newer positron emission tomography technique using a radiolabeled somatostatin analogue has largely replaced an older nuclear scintigraphy method for staging well differentiated neuroendocrine tumors and selecting candidates for targeted radionuclide therapy. Which imaging modality is being described in this explanation?", "options": {"A": "Technetium-99m bone scan, used instead to evaluate bone metastasis.", "B": "Gallium-68 DOTATATE PET, using a radiolabeled somatostatin analogue for staging.", "C": "Iodine-131 whole body scan, used instead for thyroid cancer staging.", "D": "Standard fluorodeoxyglucose PET alone, less sensitive for these well differentiated tumors.", "E": "Plain abdominal radiograph, lacking adequate functional and anatomic detail here."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gallium-68 DOTATATE PET, using a radiolabeled somatostatin analogue for staging): Gallium-68 DOTATATE positron emission tomography has largely replaced older octreotide scintigraphy for staging well differentiated neuroendocrine tumors and helps identify candidates for peptide receptor radionuclide therapy. Bone scan and iodine-131 whole body scan are used for other tumor types, standard fluorodeoxyglucose PET is less sensitive for well differentiated neuroendocrine tumors, and plain radiographs lack the needed functional and anatomic detail."}, {"id": 569, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "An oncology fellow examines a patient with widely metastatic pancreatic adenocarcinoma and notes a firm, palpable nodule at the umbilicus on abdominal examination. Her attending explains this specific finding has an eponym reflecting metastatic spread to this location. Which eponym describes this periumbilical metastatic nodule?", "options": {"A": "Virchow node, describing a supraclavicular lymph node finding instead.", "B": "Krukenberg tumor, describing a metastatic ovarian tumor instead.", "C": "Courvoisier sign, describing a palpable distended gallbladder instead.", "D": "Trousseau syndrome, the eponym describing migratory superficial thrombophlebitis instead.", "E": "Sister Mary Joseph nodule, the eponym for this periumbilical finding."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Sister Mary Joseph nodule, the eponym for this periumbilical finding): A Sister Mary Joseph nodule describes a palpable periumbilical metastatic nodule, most often originating from gastric, pancreatic, ovarian, or colonic adenocarcinoma, and its presence usually signals widespread metastatic disease with a poor prognosis. Virchow node describes a supraclavicular lymph node, Krukenberg tumor describes an ovarian metastasis, Courvoisier sign describes a distended gallbladder, and Trousseau syndrome describes migratory thrombophlebitis."}, {"id": 572, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A patient develops recurrent, migratory episodes of tender, cord-like superficial vein inflammation in different limbs over several weeks, prompting an extensive malignancy workup given this classic paraneoplastic presentation. Which underlying malignancy is this migratory superficial thrombophlebitis, known as Trousseau syndrome, most classically associated with?", "options": {"A": "Esophageal leiomyoma, a benign lesion without this hypercoagulable association.", "B": "Pancreatic adenocarcinoma, the malignancy classically linked to this presentation.", "C": "Gastric fundic gland polyp, a benign lesion without this association.", "D": "Colonic hyperplastic polyp, a benign lesion without this association.", "E": "Hepatic hemangioma, a benign lesion without this hypercoagulable association."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pancreatic adenocarcinoma, the malignancy classically linked to this presentation): Trousseau syndrome, migratory superficial thrombophlebitis, is classically linked to pancreatic adenocarcinoma and other mucin-producing adenocarcinomas, reflecting a hypercoagulable state driven by tumor-derived mucin and tissue factor. It is not associated with the benign lesions listed, which do not produce this systemic hypercoagulable paraneoplastic effect."}, {"id": 589, "domain": "neoplastic_disorders", "question": "A patient with an early T1 esophageal adenocarcinoma undergoes endoscopic resection that achieves clear margins on final pathology, with no evidence of residual disease on follow-up imaging. She now asks her gastroenterologist about the appropriate long-term surveillance plan going forward. Which of the following best reflects appropriate long-term management for her?", "options": {"A": "Continued endoscopic surveillance of the remaining residual Barrett segment.", "B": "No further endoscopy needed after this single clear endoscopic resection.", "C": "Immediate esophagectomy performed despite the already clear resection margins.", "D": "Annual chest radiograph used as the sole method of surveillance.", "E": "Discontinuing proton pump inhibitor therapy entirely after the resection."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Continued endoscopic surveillance of the remaining residual Barrett segment): After successful endoscopic resection of early esophageal adenocarcinoma with clear margins, continued surveillance of the residual Barrett segment is needed since the underlying metaplastic mucosa remains at risk for recurrent dysplasia or cancer. Stopping all endoscopy after a single clear resection would miss this ongoing risk. Immediate esophagectomy is not needed when endoscopic resection has already achieved clear margins, a chest radiograph does not adequately visualize the esophageal mucosa, and proton pump inhibitor therapy should generally continue to reduce ongoing reflux injury."}, {"id": 591, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A 58-year-old man with a 15-year history of heavy alcohol use undergoes liver biopsy for evaluation of chronic liver disease. The trichrome-stained specimen is shown. Based on this histologic pattern, what is the diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Cirrhosis.", "B": "Simple steatosis without fibrosis.", "C": "Acute viral hepatitis.", "D": "Normal liver architecture.", "E": "Isolated bile duct hyperplasia."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cirrhosis): the trichrome stain highlights blue-green collagen forming thick fibrous septa that surround regenerative nodules of hepatocytes, the defining histologic pattern of cirrhosis. Simple steatosis without fibrosis would show fat droplets without this nodular fibrous architecture, and acute viral hepatitis shows lobular inflammation and necrosis rather than established fibrous septa and nodularity. Normal architecture would show organized hepatic lobules without fibrosis, and isolated bile duct hyperplasia would not account for the diffuse nodular fibrotic pattern seen here.", "image": "question_images/q_591.jpg"}, {"id": 593, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A 54-year-old man with known cirrhosis reports several weeks of progressively worsening abdominal girth and early satiety. On exam he has shifting dullness and a fluid wave. The clinical photograph shows his abdomen. Given this presentation, what is the most likely underlying mechanism?", "options": {"A": "Primary peritoneal malignancy unrelated to liver disease.", "B": "Isolated right heart failure without liver disease.", "C": "Portal hypertension with splanchnic vasodilation and renal sodium retention.", "D": "Acute pancreatitis with third spacing.", "E": "Nephrotic syndrome from primary glomerular disease."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Portal hypertension with splanchnic vasodilation and renal sodium retention): the clinical photograph shows massive ascites with marked abdominal distention, and in a patient with known cirrhosis, this is best explained by portal hypertension driving increased splanchnic capillary pressure combined with splanchnic vasodilation that activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and promotes renal sodium and water retention. Primary peritoneal malignancy, isolated right heart failure without liver disease, acute pancreatitis, and nephrotic syndrome are alternative causes of ascites but are far less consistent with this patient's established cirrhosis and classic decompensation pattern.", "image": "question_images/q_593.jpg"}, {"id": 600, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A patient with terminal ileal Crohn disease and prior resection develops fat-soluble vitamin deficiencies. Which hepatic physiologic process is most directly disrupted by loss of terminal ileum function?", "options": {"A": "Hepatic glycogenolysis.", "B": "Urea cycle ammonia clearance.", "C": "Cytochrome P450 drug oxidation.", "D": "Bilirubin conjugation.", "E": "Enterohepatic circulation of bile acids."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Enterohepatic circulation of bile acids): bile acids are normally reabsorbed in the terminal ileum and recycled back to the liver; loss of ileal function interrupts this circulation, reduces the bile acid pool, and impairs fat and fat-soluble vitamin absorption. Glycogenolysis, ammonia clearance, drug oxidation, and bilirubin conjugation are hepatocyte functions unrelated to ileal bile acid reabsorption. This is why extensive ileal disease or resection produces bile acid diarrhea and fat malabsorption together."}, {"id": 608, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A patient is being evaluated for liver transplant listing. Which laboratory variables are combined in the MELD-Na score used to prioritize allocation?", "options": {"A": "Albumin, ascites, and encephalopathy grade.", "B": "Platelet count, AST, and ALT.", "C": "Creatinine, bilirubin, INR, and sodium.", "D": "Alkaline phosphatase and GGT.", "E": "Ammonia and lactate."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Creatinine, bilirubin, INR, and sodium): the MELD-Na score uses serum creatinine, bilirubin, INR, and sodium to predict 90-day mortality and prioritize patients for transplant allocation. Albumin, ascites, and encephalopathy grade are components of the Child-Pugh score instead. Platelet count with AST and ALT describe fibrosis calculators like FIB-4 or APRI, and ammonia or lactate are not part of either prognostic scoring system."}, {"id": 609, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A patient with cirrhosis and ascites has a creatinine that rises from 0.9 to 1.6 mg/dL over one week. The hepatology team recalculates the MELD-Na score. Why does even a modest creatinine rise meaningfully worsen transplant priority in this scoring system?", "options": {"A": "Creatinine directly measures hepatic synthetic function.", "B": "The formula weighs creatinine less than any other variable.", "C": "Sodium is removed from the equation once creatinine rises.", "D": "Creatinine carries substantial weight in the formula and reflects a key mortality predictor.", "E": "Bilirubin is disregarded once renal function changes."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Creatinine carries substantial weight in the formula and reflects a key mortality predictor): renal function is one of the strongest independent predictors of short-term mortality in cirrhosis, and the MELD formula weights creatinine heavily, so even modest increases can produce a disproportionate rise in score and transplant priority. Creatinine reflects renal, not hepatic synthetic, function. Sodium and bilirubin remain part of the calculation regardless of creatinine changes, they are not removed or disregarded."}, {"id": 611, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A 55-year-old man with suspected fatty liver disease has a FIB-4 index of 3.2, above the high-risk threshold. What is the most appropriate next step in his evaluation?", "options": {"A": "Further fibrosis assessment with elastography or biopsy.", "B": "Immediate liver transplant referral.", "C": "No further workup since FIB-4 alone is diagnostic of cirrhosis.", "D": "Repeat FIB-4 in five years.", "E": "Start antiviral therapy empirically."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Further fibrosis assessment with elastography or biopsy): a high FIB-4 score identifies patients at increased risk of advanced fibrosis and should prompt confirmatory testing with elastography or, if needed, biopsy rather than being treated as diagnostic on its own. Transplant referral is premature without confirmed decompensated cirrhosis. Antiviral therapy is irrelevant without an established viral etiology, and waiting five years to reassess ignores an actionable high-risk result now."}, {"id": 612, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "Vibration-controlled transient elastography estimates liver fibrosis by measuring which physical property?", "options": {"A": "Doppler flow velocity in the portal vein.", "B": "Shear wave velocity through liver tissue.", "C": "Attenuation of gamma radiation.", "D": "T2 relaxation time of hepatocytes.", "E": "Electrical impedance of hepatic parenchyma."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Shear wave velocity through liver tissue): transient elastography generates a mechanical shear wave and measures its propagation velocity through the liver, which correlates with tissue stiffness and therefore fibrosis stage. It does not use Doppler flow, gamma radiation, MRI relaxation times, or electrical impedance. Faster shear wave velocity indicates stiffer, more fibrotic tissue."}, {"id": 616, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A patient has a hepatic venous pressure gradient of 8 mmHg on a research protocol. Based on the accepted definition of clinically significant portal hypertension, how should this value be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "Below the threshold for clinically significant portal hypertension.", "B": "Diagnostic of decompensated cirrhosis.", "C": "Consistent with a high risk of imminent variceal bleeding.", "D": "Indicative of hepatorenal syndrome.", "E": "Equivalent to Child-Pugh class C."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Below the threshold for clinically significant portal hypertension): clinically significant portal hypertension is defined by a gradient of 10 mmHg or greater, so a value of 8 mmHg falls below that threshold even though it exceeds the normal range of 1 to 5 mmHg. It does not by itself indicate decompensation, imminent bleeding risk, hepatorenal syndrome, or a specific Child-Pugh class, since those are determined by separate clinical and laboratory criteria. This distinction matters for deciding when varices or complications are likely to emerge."}, {"id": 630, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "Octreotide is used in acute variceal hemorrhage primarily for which pharmacologic effect?", "options": {"A": "Direct mechanical tamponade of the bleeding vessel.", "B": "Correction of underlying coagulopathy.", "C": "Prevention of spontaneous bacterial peritonitis.", "D": "Stimulation of hepatic regeneration.", "E": "Reduction of splanchnic blood flow and portal pressure."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Reduction of splanchnic blood flow and portal pressure): octreotide is a somatostatin analog that reduces splanchnic arterial blood flow, thereby lowering portal pressure and variceal wall tension during acute bleeding. It provides no mechanical tamponade, does not correct coagulopathy directly, and while antibiotics are given concurrently for SBP prevention, that is not octreotide's mechanism. It also does not stimulate hepatic regeneration."}, {"id": 642, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A cirrhotic patient with a large right pleural effusion and minimal ascites is found to have hepatic hydrothorax. Why is chest tube placement generally avoided in this condition?", "options": {"A": "It is contraindicated due to risk of pneumothorax alone.", "B": "It causes large protein and fluid losses without treating the underlying portal hypertension.", "C": "It permanently cures the hydrothorax.", "D": "It has no associated complications.", "E": "It reduces the need for future thoracentesis entirely."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It causes large protein and fluid losses without treating the underlying portal hypertension): chest tube drainage in hepatic hydrothorax leads to ongoing high volume protein and electrolyte losses because the fluid continually replenishes from the abdomen through diaphragmatic defects, without addressing the portal hypertension driving fluid formation. It is not simply about pneumothorax risk, does not cure the condition, and does not reduce the future need for intervention, making management strategies similar to those for ascites preferred instead."}, {"id": 647, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "Hepatorenal syndrome is best characterized as which type of kidney injury?", "options": {"A": "Acute tubular necrosis from nephrotoxic injury.", "B": "Functional renal failure without intrinsic kidney disease.", "C": "Glomerulonephritis from immune complex deposition.", "D": "Obstructive uropathy from ureteral compression.", "E": "Structural cystic kidney disease."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Functional renal failure without intrinsic kidney disease): hepatorenal syndrome is a hemodynamically mediated, functional form of kidney injury with near-normal renal histology, caused by intense renal vasoconstriction rather than structural damage. Acute tubular necrosis, glomerulonephritis, obstructive uropathy, and cystic kidney disease all involve intrinsic structural or obstructive pathology, which is specifically excluded when diagnosing hepatorenal syndrome."}, {"id": 650, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "Which combination of medications is most commonly used in the United States for hepatorenal syndrome when terlipressin is not available?", "options": {"A": "Furosemide and spironolactone alone.", "B": "Lisinopril and hydrochlorothiazide.", "C": "High dose corticosteroids.", "D": "N-acetylcysteine infusion alone.", "E": "Midodrine, octreotide, and albumin."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Midodrine, octreotide, and albumin): in settings without access to terlipressin, the combination of midodrine, an alpha agonist causing systemic vasoconstriction, octreotide, reducing splanchnic vasodilation, and albumin for volume expansion is the standard regimen used to treat hepatorenal syndrome. Diuretics alone or antihypertensives such as lisinopril and hydrochlorothiazide would worsen the underlying hemodynamic problem. Corticosteroids and N-acetylcysteine alone have no established role in this treatment."}, {"id": 652, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A patient with cirrhosis and ascites is found to have acute kidney injury. Renal ultrasound shows normal-appearing kidneys, urinalysis is bland, and there is no recent NSAID or contrast exposure. Before diagnosing hepatorenal syndrome, what must first be excluded through diuretic withdrawal and albumin challenge?", "options": {"A": "Chronic glomerulonephritis.", "B": "Volume-responsive prerenal azotemia.", "C": "Renal artery stenosis.", "D": "Polycystic kidney disease.", "E": "Diabetic nephropathy."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Volume-responsive prerenal azotemia): the diagnostic criteria for hepatorenal syndrome specifically require that the acute kidney injury fails to improve after volume expansion and diuretic withdrawal, which is done to rule out simple volume-responsive prerenal azotemia before attributing the injury to the vasoconstrictive hepatorenal mechanism. Chronic glomerulonephritis, renal artery stenosis, polycystic kidney disease, and diabetic nephropathy are structural or chronic processes that would typically be evident on imaging or history, not the specific functional process excluded by this bedside maneuver."}, {"id": 654, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "What role does renal replacement therapy play in the management of hepatorenal syndrome?", "options": {"A": "Definitive curative therapy.", "B": "First-line therapy before vasoconstrictors are tried.", "C": "A treatment that reverses the underlying portal hypertension.", "D": "A bridge to liver transplantation rather than definitive treatment.", "E": "A substitute for albumin infusion."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A bridge to liver transplantation rather than definitive treatment): renal replacement therapy supports patients with severe hepatorenal syndrome while awaiting transplant but does not address the underlying splanchnic vasodilation and portal hypertension driving the syndrome, so it is not curative. Vasoconstrictor and albumin therapy are typically trialed as primary treatment before or alongside dialysis, not after it as a last resort exclusively, and dialysis is not a substitute for albumin, which serves a different volume-expanding role."}, {"id": 665, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "Why is routine protein restriction no longer recommended as therapy for hepatic encephalopathy?", "options": {"A": "Protein has no relationship to ammonia production.", "B": "Protein restriction cures encephalopathy permanently.", "C": "Muscle mass has no role in ammonia metabolism.", "D": "Protein restriction improves synthetic liver function.", "E": "It worsens malnutrition and adequate protein supports muscle mass needed for ammonia clearance."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (It worsens malnutrition and adequate protein supports muscle mass needed for ammonia clearance): chronic protein restriction contributes to sarcopenia and malnutrition, which worsens outcomes in cirrhosis, and adequate muscle mass actually helps clear ammonia since skeletal muscle can metabolize ammonia when hepatic clearance is impaired. Protein intake is directly related to ammonia production, restriction does not cure encephalopathy, and it has no beneficial effect on hepatic synthetic function, making it not part of standard therapy."}, {"id": 671, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "Portopulmonary hypertension is distinguished from hepatopulmonary syndrome by which underlying mechanism?", "options": {"A": "Pulmonary arterial vasoconstriction and remodeling rather than vascular dilation.", "B": "Intrapulmonary vascular dilation identical to hepatopulmonary syndrome.", "C": "Isolated left ventricular diastolic dysfunction.", "D": "Pure volume overload from ascites.", "E": "Diaphragmatic elevation restricting lung expansion."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Pulmonary arterial vasoconstriction and remodeling rather than vascular dilation): portopulmonary hypertension arises from vasoconstriction and structural remodeling within the pulmonary arterial circulation, in contrast to the vascular dilation seen in hepatopulmonary syndrome. It is not caused by isolated diastolic dysfunction, volume overload from ascites, or diaphragmatic restriction, which are distinct and separate clinical entities from either pulmonary complication of portal hypertension."}, {"id": 672, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "Right heart catheterization in a patient with suspected portopulmonary hypertension is expected to show which combination of findings?", "options": {"A": "Elevated wedge pressure with normal pulmonary artery pressure.", "B": "Elevated mean pulmonary artery pressure with elevated pulmonary vascular resistance and normal wedge pressure.", "C": "Normal pulmonary artery pressure and normal vascular resistance.", "D": "Low pulmonary artery pressure with elevated cardiac output.", "E": "Elevated right atrial pressure with normal pulmonary vascular resistance only."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Elevated mean pulmonary artery pressure with elevated pulmonary vascular resistance and normal wedge pressure): the hemodynamic definition of portopulmonary hypertension requires elevated mean pulmonary artery pressure and pulmonary vascular resistance with a normal pulmonary capillary wedge pressure, distinguishing it from pulmonary venous hypertension due to left heart disease, where wedge pressure would be elevated. Normal pressures and resistance, or low pressure with high output, would not support this diagnosis."}, {"id": 673, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A liver transplant candidate is found to have severely elevated mean pulmonary artery pressure on right heart catheterization consistent with severe portopulmonary hypertension. How does this finding most directly affect transplant candidacy?", "options": {"A": "It has no effect on transplant risk or candidacy.", "B": "It automatically qualifies the patient for exception MELD points with no further workup.", "C": "It carries prohibitive perioperative mortality and may require pulmonary vasodilator therapy first.", "D": "It guarantees resolution of pulmonary hypertension immediately after transplant.", "E": "It indicates the patient instead has hepatopulmonary syndrome."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It carries prohibitive perioperative mortality and may require pulmonary vasodilator therapy first): severe portopulmonary hypertension significantly increases perioperative mortality risk during liver transplantation, often requiring pretransplant pulmonary vasodilator therapy to reduce pressures before proceeding can even be considered. It is not risk-neutral, and while exception points may be considered in some allocation systems, this is not automatic without demonstrating some hemodynamic response. Pulmonary hypertension does not necessarily resolve immediately after transplant the way hepatopulmonary syndrome typically improves, and the two conditions have distinct, separate pathophysiology."}, {"id": 674, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "Which imaging or bedside finding is most useful for initially assessing intrapulmonary shunting at the bedside before formal contrast echocardiography?", "options": {"A": "Chest x-ray showing a normal cardiac silhouette.", "B": "Abdominal ultrasound.", "C": "Electrocardiogram.", "D": "Pulse oximetry change with position, assessing for orthodeoxia.", "E": "Spirometry."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pulse oximetry change with position, assessing for orthodeoxia): checking oxygen saturation in both supine and upright positions is a simple bedside method to screen for orthodeoxia, a hallmark clue toward hepatopulmonary syndrome, prompting further confirmatory testing. A normal cardiac silhouette on chest x-ray, ascites on ultrasound, a normal electrocardiogram, and a restrictive spirometry pattern do not specifically evaluate for intrapulmonary shunting."}, {"id": 675, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "Why does hepatopulmonary syndrome preferentially worsen oxygenation when a patient is upright rather than supine?", "options": {"A": "Upright posture increases hepatic blood flow directly.", "B": "Supine posture increases intrapulmonary shunting more than upright posture.", "C": "Upright posture reduces cardiac output substantially.", "D": "Gravity has no effect on this condition.", "E": "The vascular dilations are more pronounced in the lung bases where blood flow increases upright."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The vascular dilations are more pronounced in the lung bases where blood flow increases upright): because the dilated pulmonary vessels are concentrated in the lung bases, upright posture directs more blood flow to these poorly oxygenating regions, worsening the ventilation-perfusion mismatch and lowering oxygen saturation. Supine positioning redistributes flow more evenly, improving oxygenation rather than worsening it, and this phenomenon is a direct gravitational effect on regional blood flow rather than a change in hepatic flow or cardiac output."}, {"id": 682, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "In Budd-Chiari syndrome, the caudate lobe is often relatively spared or enlarged because of which anatomic feature?", "options": {"A": "It receives no portal venous supply at all.", "B": "It has separate venous drainage directly into the inferior vena cava.", "C": "It lacks a hepatic arterial blood supply entirely.", "D": "It is anatomically outside the liver capsule.", "E": "It drains exclusively through the splenic vein."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It has separate venous drainage directly into the inferior vena cava): the caudate lobe has its own independent hepatic veins draining directly into the inferior vena cava, separate from the main hepatic veins that are typically obstructed in Budd-Chiari syndrome, allowing it to remain relatively spared and often compensatorily enlarged. It still receives normal portal and arterial supply and remains part of the liver, so the other statements are incorrect."}, {"id": 687, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "Congenital hepatic fibrosis is most classically associated with which other organ system disease?", "options": {"A": "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease.", "B": "Polycystic kidney disease.", "C": "Rheumatoid arthritis.", "D": "Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.", "E": "Type 1 diabetes mellitus."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Polycystic kidney disease): congenital hepatic fibrosis is an inherited disorder frequently associated with autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease, reflecting a shared underlying ductal plate malformation. It has no established classic association with pulmonary disease, rheumatoid arthritis, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, or type 1 diabetes."}, {"id": 690, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A patient from a schistosomiasis-endemic region presents with splenomegaly, esophageal varices, and preserved liver synthetic function. Along with antihelminthic treatment, which additional management strategy is most appropriate given the variceal risk?", "options": {"A": "No endoscopic evaluation is needed since synthetic function is preserved.", "B": "Immediate liver transplant listing.", "C": "Avoidance of beta blockers in all noncirrhotic portal hypertension.", "D": "Permanent withholding of any portal hypertension therapy until cirrhosis develops.", "E": "Standard variceal screening and prophylaxis similar to cirrhotic portal hypertension."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Standard variceal screening and prophylaxis similar to cirrhotic portal hypertension): despite preserved synthetic function, patients with schistosomiasis-related portal hypertension remain at real risk for variceal hemorrhage and should undergo the same endoscopic screening and prophylactic strategies used in cirrhotic portal hypertension. Preserved synthetic function does not eliminate the need for endoscopic evaluation, transplant is not indicated for isolated noncirrhotic portal hypertension with preserved function, and beta blockers and other standard variceal therapies remain appropriate regardless of the underlying etiology being noncirrhotic."}, {"id": 692, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A 54-year-old woman reports fatigue and generalized pruritus that is worse at night for the past year. Alkaline phosphatase is markedly elevated with only mildly elevated transaminases. Antimitochondrial antibody is positive. A liver biopsy is performed and the histology below is obtained. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Primary sclerosing cholangitis, a diagnosis within the differential for liver dysfunction.", "B": "Primary biliary cholangitis, a diagnosis within the differential for liver dysfunction.", "C": "Autoimmune hepatitis type 1, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "D": "Drug-induced cholestatic liver injury, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "E": "Chronic hepatitis C infection, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Primary biliary cholangitis, a diagnosis within the differential for liver dysfunction). The classic demographic, pruritus and fatigue preceding jaundice, a cholestatic liver enzyme pattern, positive antimitochondrial antibody, and biopsy showing lymphocytic infiltration around small bile ducts. Primary sclerosing cholangitis shows large duct beading on cholangiography rather than this histology and is not typically antimitochondrial antibody positive. Autoimmune hepatitis shows interface hepatitis with plasma cells, not bile duct-centered lymphocytic injury. Drug-induced injury and hepatitis C do not explain the positive antimitochondrial antibody.", "image": "question_images/q_692.jpg"}, {"id": 694, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A clinician reviewing liver disease concepts for board preparation considers the following question. A 19-year-old college student develops tremor, slurred speech, and a personality change over several months along with elevated transaminases. Ceruloplasmin is low. Slit-lamp examination shows the finding pictured below. What is the underlying pathophysiologic mechanism of this disease?", "options": {"A": "Excessive intestinal iron absorption from a mutated HFE gene.", "B": "Autoimmune destruction of small intrahepatic bile ducts, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "C": "Accumulation of misfolded protein within hepatocytes, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "D": "Impaired biliary copper excretion from a mutated ATP7B transporter.", "E": "Impaired bilirubin conjugation from reduced UGT1A1 activity, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Impaired biliary copper excretion from a mutated ATP7B transporter). Is the defect underlying Wilson disease and explains the combined liver and neuropsychiatric findings along with the Kayser-Fleischer ring shown. Excessive iron absorption describes hereditary hemochromatosis, not this presentation. Autoimmune bile duct destruction describes primary biliary cholangitis, which does not cause Kayser-Fleischer rings. Misfolded protein accumulation describes alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, and reduced UGT1A1 activity describes Gilbert syndrome, neither of which fits this clinical picture.", "image": "question_images/q_694.jpg"}, {"id": 702, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "In preparing teaching material for a hepatology board review course, a program director includes the following clinical question for trainees to consider. A patient's hepatitis B panel shows HBsAg negative, IgM anti-HBc negative, total anti-HBc negative, and anti-HBs positive. What does this serologic pattern indicate?", "options": {"A": "Acute hepatitis B infection, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "B": "Immunity from hepatitis B vaccination, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "C": "Chronic hepatitis B infection, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "D": "Resolved natural hepatitis B infection, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "E": "Occult hepatitis B infection, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Immunity from hepatitis B vaccination, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology). The vaccine induces only anti-HBs positivity without any anti-HBc positivity, since it contains surface antigen but no core antigen. Acute and chronic infection both require HBsAg or IgM anti-HBc positivity, neither of which is present here. Resolved natural infection would show total anti-HBc positive along with anti-HBs positive, which distinguishes it from vaccination alone. Occult infection is defined by detectable HBV DNA despite negative HBsAg, which is not addressed by this panel."}, {"id": 707, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "In preparing teaching material for a hepatology board review course, a program director includes the following clinical question for trainees to consider. A patient with chronic hepatitis C genotype 1 and no cirrhosis is being considered for treatment. Which class of medication is now standard first-line therapy?", "options": {"A": "Pegylated interferon monotherapy, a diagnosis within the differential for liver dysfunction.", "B": "Direct-acting antivirals, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease.", "C": "Ribavirin monotherapy, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease.", "D": "Nucleos(t)ide analogues used for hepatitis B, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "E": "Corticosteroids combined with azathioprine, a management option relevant to liver disease care."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Direct-acting antivirals, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease). Cure over ninety-five percent of patients with hepatitis C in eight to twelve weeks and have replaced older regimens. Pegylated interferon monotherapy was standard therapy before direct-acting antivirals but is now obsolete due to poor tolerability and lower cure rates. Ribavirin monotherapy is not an effective standalone treatment for hepatitis C. Hepatitis B nucleos(t)ide analogues and immunosuppressive regimens have no role in hepatitis C treatment."}, {"id": 713, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "In preparing teaching material for a hepatology board review course, a program director includes the following clinical question for trainees to consider. A 30-year-old woman newly diagnosed with autoimmune hepatitis has significantly elevated transaminases and interface hepatitis on biopsy. What is the most appropriate initial treatment regimen?", "options": {"A": "Ursodeoxycholic acid alone, a treatment strategy applied in certain hepatobiliary conditions.", "B": "Direct-acting antivirals, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease.", "C": "Corticosteroids combined with azathioprine, a management option relevant to liver disease care.", "D": "Therapeutic phlebotomy, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease.", "E": "Copper chelation with penicillamine, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Corticosteroids combined with azathioprine, a management option relevant to liver disease care). Is standard induction and maintenance therapy for autoimmune hepatitis. Ursodeoxycholic acid alone is first-line for primary biliary cholangitis, not autoimmune hepatitis. Direct-acting antivirals treat hepatitis C, an unrelated viral disease. Therapeutic phlebotomy and copper chelation treat hemochromatosis and Wilson disease respectively, neither of which applies here."}, {"id": 716, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A gastroenterology fellow preparing for board certification reviews the following concept while studying liver disorders. A patient has features of both autoimmune hepatitis and primary biliary cholangitis, including positive ANA and antimitochondrial antibody along with both hepatocellular and cholestatic enzyme elevation. What treatment approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Combination therapy targeting both the hepatitic and cholestatic components.", "B": "Ursodeoxycholic acid alone with no immunosuppression, a management option relevant to liver disease care.", "C": "Corticosteroids alone with no ursodeoxycholic acid, a management option relevant to liver disease care.", "D": "Direct-acting antivirals for presumed hepatitis C overlap, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "E": "Observation alone without any pharmacologic treatment, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Combination therapy targeting both the hepatitic and cholestatic components). Overlap syndrome patients often need both immunosuppression for the autoimmune hepatitis component and ursodeoxycholic acid for the cholestatic component. Ursodeoxycholic acid alone would undertreat the autoimmune hepatitis component. Corticosteroids alone would undertreat the cholestatic component. Direct-acting antivirals are irrelevant since there is no evidence of hepatitis C in this scenario, and observation alone risks progression of both processes."}, {"id": 717, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "While teaching a group of internal medicine residents about liver disease, an attending physician poses the following question for discussion. A 50-year-old woman with primary biliary cholangitis has an inadequate biochemical response to one year of appropriately dosed ursodeoxycholic acid. What is an appropriate next step in therapy?", "options": {"A": "Increase ursodeoxycholic acid far above the standard weight-based dose.", "B": "Add obeticholic acid or a fibrate as second-line therapy.", "C": "Switch to corticosteroids as monotherapy, a management option relevant to liver disease care.", "D": "Begin therapeutic phlebotomy, a treatment strategy applied in certain hepatobiliary conditions.", "E": "Start antiviral therapy targeting occult hepatitis B, a management option relevant to liver disease care."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Add obeticholic acid or a fibrate as second-line therapy). Is the recommended approach for patients with an inadequate biochemical response to standard-dose ursodeoxycholic acid after one year. Excessively high-dose ursodeoxycholic acid beyond standard dosing is not recommended and can be harmful in some populations. Corticosteroid monotherapy is not standard treatment for primary biliary cholangitis. Phlebotomy and hepatitis B antivirals have no role in this cholestatic autoimmune disease."}, {"id": 719, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A gastroenterology fellow preparing for board certification reviews the following concept while studying liver disorders. A patient with ulcerative colitis undergoes cholangiography for elevated alkaline phosphatase, showing multifocal strictures and dilations of both intrahepatic and extrahepatic bile ducts with a beaded appearance. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Primary biliary cholangitis, a diagnosis within the differential for liver dysfunction.", "B": "Choledocholithiasis, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease.", "C": "IgG4-related sclerosing cholangitis, a laboratory or diagnostic tool used in hepatology.", "D": "Primary sclerosing cholangitis, a diagnosis within the differential for liver dysfunction.", "E": "Secondary sclerosing cholangitis from critical illness, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Primary sclerosing cholangitis, a diagnosis within the differential for liver dysfunction). The classic beaded appearance of both intrahepatic and extrahepatic ducts and the strong association with inflammatory bowel disease, especially ulcerative colitis. Primary biliary cholangitis affects small intrahepatic ducts and does not produce this beaded large-duct cholangiographic pattern. Choledocholithiasis typically shows a discrete stone and dilation proximal to an obstruction rather than diffuse beading. IgG4-related sclerosing cholangitis can look similar on imaging but is not defined by this classic IBD association and responds to steroids, unlike primary sclerosing cholangitis."}, {"id": 722, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A clinician reviewing liver disease concepts for board preparation considers the following question. A 35-year-old man with primary sclerosing cholangitis develops a new dominant stricture of the common bile duct with worsening jaundice. What is the most important diagnosis to exclude before treating this as a simple benign stricture?", "options": {"A": "Hepatocellular carcinoma, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease.", "B": "Cholangiocarcinoma, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease.", "C": "Acute viral hepatitis A, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "D": "Wilson disease, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease.", "E": "Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, a diagnosis within the differential for liver dysfunction."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cholangiocarcinoma, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease). Patients with primary sclerosing cholangitis have a significantly increased lifetime risk of this cancer, and any new dominant stricture must be evaluated to exclude malignancy before assuming it is benign. Hepatocellular carcinoma arises from hepatocytes rather than bile ducts and is not the primary concern with a new biliary stricture. Acute hepatitis A does not cause focal biliary strictures. Wilson disease and alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency are unrelated inherited metabolic conditions that do not present this way."}, {"id": 728, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A gastroenterology fellow preparing for board certification reviews the following concept while studying liver disorders. A 44-year-old woman with MASH and a body mass index of 34 asks what lifestyle change would most likely improve both her steatosis and fibrosis. What is the most appropriate recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Complete avoidance of all dietary fat regardless of quality.", "B": "Daily high-dose vitamin A supplementation, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "C": "Weight loss of at least seven to ten percent through diet and exercise.", "D": "Strict avoidance of all carbohydrates including whole grains.", "E": "Bed rest with minimal physical activity to reduce metabolic demand."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Weight loss of at least seven to ten percent through diet and exercise). Has been shown to improve both steatosis and fibrosis in MASH. Complete avoidance of dietary fat is not an evidence-based recommendation and does not target the underlying metabolic drivers. High-dose vitamin A supplementation is not recommended and can itself cause liver injury in excess. Strict carbohydrate avoidance and bed rest are not established, balanced approaches to MASH management."}, {"id": 730, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "During evaluation of several patients on a busy hepatology consult service, a trainee asks the attending the following question for clarification. A 60-year-old man with MASLD and no cirrhosis asks what he is most likely to die from over the coming decades. What is the most accurate answer?", "options": {"A": "Hepatocellular carcinoma, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease.", "B": "Variceal hemorrhage, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease.", "C": "Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, a diagnosis within the differential for liver dysfunction.", "D": "Hepatorenal syndrome, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease.", "E": "Cardiovascular disease, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Cardiovascular disease, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease). Remains the leading cause of death in most patients with MASLD, reflecting the shared metabolic risk factors between fatty liver and cardiovascular disease. Hepatocellular carcinoma risk is real but is a less common cause of death than cardiovascular disease in the overall MASLD population, particularly without cirrhosis. Variceal hemorrhage, spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, and hepatorenal syndrome are complications of decompensated cirrhosis and portal hypertension, which are not present in this patient."}, {"id": 731, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A medical student rotating on the hepatology consult service is asked the following question during attending rounds this morning. A 41-year-old woman with MASLD and an elevated FIB-4 score undergoes further noninvasive testing to clarify her fibrosis stage without a liver biopsy. What test is most appropriate next?", "options": {"A": "Transient elastography, a test or marker relevant to liver disease evaluation.", "B": "Repeat ultrasound alone without elastography, a clinical marker referenced in hepatobiliary evaluation.", "C": "Serum ceruloplasmin testing, a laboratory or diagnostic tool used in hepatology.", "D": "Antimitochondrial antibody testing, a laboratory or diagnostic tool used in hepatology.", "E": "Hepatitis D antibody testing, a clinical marker referenced in hepatobiliary evaluation."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Transient elastography, a test or marker relevant to liver disease evaluation). Is the appropriate next noninvasive step after an elevated FIB-4 score to further clarify fibrosis stage before considering biopsy. Repeat ultrasound alone does not provide the quantitative stiffness measurement needed to stage fibrosis. Ceruloplasmin and antimitochondrial antibody testing evaluate for unrelated conditions, Wilson disease and primary biliary cholangitis. Hepatitis D antibody testing is irrelevant without any evidence of hepatitis B infection."}, {"id": 733, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "During evaluation of several patients on a busy hepatology consult service, a trainee asks the attending the following question for clarification. A patient with alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency and confirmed PiZZ genotype develops progressive cirrhosis and hepatic decompensation despite optimal supportive care. What is the most definitive treatment option?", "options": {"A": "Augmentation therapy with intravenous alpha-1 antitrypsin protein, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "B": "Corticosteroid therapy, an approach sometimes used in the management of liver disease.", "C": "Liver transplantation, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease.", "D": "Ursodeoxycholic acid as disease-modifying therapy, a management option relevant to liver disease care.", "E": "Copper chelation therapy, a diagnosis within the differential for liver dysfunction."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Liver transplantation, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease). Is curative for the liver disease of alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency because the new liver produces normal protein. Augmentation therapy with intravenous alpha-1 antitrypsin protein is used for the lung disease component, not to treat or reverse the liver injury. Corticosteroids and ursodeoxycholic acid have no proven disease-modifying effect on this condition's liver disease. Copper chelation treats Wilson disease, an unrelated condition."}, {"id": 739, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A clinician reviewing liver disease concepts for board preparation considers the following question. A 50-year-old man with hereditary hemochromatosis has completed several years of phlebotomy with normalized iron indices. He now reports new joint pain in his hands. What is the best explanation for this symptom despite adequate iron reduction?", "options": {"A": "Persistent joint pain always indicates inadequate phlebotomy and undertreated iron overload.", "B": "This finding excludes the original diagnosis of hemochromatosis.", "C": "Joint pain in hemochromatosis always resolves completely with phlebotomy.", "D": "Hemochromatosis-related arthropathy can persist even after iron levels normalize.", "E": "This represents a new unrelated autoimmune arthritis with no connection to iron overload."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hemochromatosis-related arthropathy can persist even after iron levels normalize). Joint damage from earlier iron deposition may not be fully reversible even with adequate treatment. Persistent joint pain does not necessarily mean phlebotomy has been inadequate if iron indices are now normal. This finding does not exclude the original diagnosis, and arthropathy is a recognized feature that may not fully resolve with treatment, contrary to always improving completely."}, {"id": 741, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A gastroenterology fellow preparing for board certification reviews the following concept while studying liver disorders. A patient with sickle cell disease develops right upper quadrant pain, jaundice, and evidence of intrahepatic cholestasis from sinusoidal obstruction by sickled cells. What is the most appropriate treatment for this severe presentation?", "options": {"A": "Exchange transfusion, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease.", "B": "Copper chelation therapy, a diagnosis within the differential for liver dysfunction.", "C": "High-dose corticosteroids, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease.", "D": "Therapeutic phlebotomy, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease.", "E": "Direct-acting antiviral therapy, a diagnosis within the differential for liver dysfunction."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Exchange transfusion, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease). Is used for severe sickle cell hepatopathy with significant intrahepatic cholestasis from sinusoidal sickling. Copper chelation and phlebotomy treat unrelated metabolic liver diseases, Wilson disease and hemochromatosis. Corticosteroids do not address the underlying sickling process. Antiviral therapy is irrelevant since this is not a viral condition."}, {"id": 753, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A gastroenterology fellow preparing for board certification reviews the following concept while studying liver disorders. A 30-year-old woman at 34 weeks gestation develops severe pruritus of the palms and soles along with elevated serum bile acids and mildly elevated transaminases. What is the most appropriate management approach?", "options": {"A": "Reassure the patient that this is a benign finding requiring no treatment.", "B": "Start high-dose corticosteroids as first-line therapy, a management option relevant to liver disease care.", "C": "Start ursodeoxycholic acid and consider early delivery, a management option relevant to liver disease care.", "D": "Begin therapeutic phlebotomy, a treatment strategy applied in certain hepatobiliary conditions.", "E": "Delay delivery as long as possible regardless of bile acid trend."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Start ursodeoxycholic acid and consider early delivery, a management option relevant to liver disease care). Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy carries a real risk of stillbirth, and these interventions are standard management to reduce that risk. Reassurance without treatment ignores the genuine fetal risk associated with this condition. Corticosteroids are not first-line therapy for intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy. Phlebotomy is irrelevant, and delaying delivery indefinitely regardless of bile acid levels increases risk rather than reducing it."}, {"id": 755, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A clinician reviewing liver disease concepts for board preparation considers the following question. A pregnant woman in her third trimester develops jaundice, coagulopathy, and hypoglycemia with altered mental status. Laboratory testing shows findings that overlap significantly with HELLP syndrome. What condition should be strongly considered alongside HELLP syndrome?", "options": {"A": "Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "B": "Chronic hepatitis C infection, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "C": "Primary biliary cholangitis, a diagnosis within the differential for liver dysfunction.", "D": "Wilson disease, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease.", "E": "Acute fatty liver of pregnancy, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Acute fatty liver of pregnancy, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology). Can be difficult to distinguish from HELLP syndrome and presents with jaundice, coagulopathy, and sometimes hypoglycemia or altered mental status from evolving liver failure. Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy presents primarily with pruritus and elevated bile acids without this degree of coagulopathy and mental status change. Chronic hepatitis C, primary biliary cholangitis, and Wilson disease do not have this specific pregnancy-associated acute presentation."}, {"id": 756, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "In preparing teaching material for a hepatology board review course, a program director includes the following clinical question for trainees to consider. What laboratory and clinical criteria define acute liver failure in a patient without preexisting cirrhosis? This is a concept frequently emphasized on certifying board examinations.", "options": {"A": "Coagulopathy with INR 1.5 or higher plus any encephalopathy within 26 weeks of illness onset.", "B": "Any elevation of transaminases above the upper limit of normal.", "C": "Ascites and splenomegaly without any coagulopathy, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "D": "Elevated alkaline phosphatase alone with normal INR, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "E": "Isolated hyperbilirubinemia without any other laboratory abnormality, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Coagulopathy with INR 1.5 or higher plus any encephalopathy within 26 weeks of illness onset): Acute liver failure is defined as coagulopathy with an INR of 1.5 or higher and any degree of encephalopathy occurring within 26 weeks of illness onset in a patient without preexisting cirrhosis. Simple transaminase elevation alone does not meet criteria without the coagulopathy and encephalopathy components. Ascites and splenomegaly are more typical of chronic liver disease with portal hypertension, not acute failure. Isolated hyperbilirubinemia or alkaline phosphatase elevation alone do not fulfill the definition."}, {"id": 764, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "In preparing teaching material for a hepatology board review course, a program director includes the following clinical question for trainees to consider. A patient with right heart failure develops elevated liver enzymes, hepatomegaly, and mild ascites. What is the underlying mechanism of this liver injury pattern?", "options": {"A": "Autoimmune destruction of hepatocytes, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "B": "Direct viral cytopathic injury, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "C": "Excessive intestinal iron absorption, a clinical marker referenced in hepatobiliary evaluation.", "D": "Sinusoidal congestion from elevated central venous pressure, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "E": "Impaired biliary copper excretion, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Sinusoidal congestion from elevated central venous pressure, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology). Describes congestive hepatopathy from right heart failure and can progress to cardiac cirrhosis if untreated. Autoimmune destruction and direct viral injury describe unrelated hepatitic processes. Excessive iron absorption and impaired copper excretion describe hereditary hemochromatosis and Wilson disease, neither of which is the mechanism in heart failure-related liver injury."}, {"id": 765, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "A gastroenterology fellow preparing for board certification reviews the following concept while studying liver disorders. A patient in septic shock develops a dramatic but transient rise in transaminases into the thousands that improves rapidly once blood pressure and perfusion are restored. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Chronic hepatitis B infection, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "B": "Primary sclerosing cholangitis, a diagnosis within the differential for liver dysfunction.", "C": "Wilson disease, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease.", "D": "Hereditary hemochromatosis, a condition encountered in the evaluation of hepatobiliary disease.", "E": "Ischemic hepatitis (shock liver), an entity relevant to this area of hepatology."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Ischemic hepatitis (shock liver), an entity relevant to this area of hepatology). Causes dramatic but transient transaminase elevations related to inadequate hepatic perfusion during shock, improving quickly once perfusion is restored. Chronic hepatitis B does not present with this abrupt, transient pattern tied to hemodynamic instability. Primary sclerosing cholangitis is a chronic cholestatic disease, not an acute ischemic process. Wilson disease and hereditary hemochromatosis are chronic metabolic conditions unrelated to acute hypoperfusion."}, {"id": 767, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "In preparing teaching material for a hepatology board review course, a program director includes the following clinical question for trainees to consider. A patient with untreated hyperthyroidism is found to have mild transaminase elevation on routine labs. What is the most appropriate initial management approach?", "options": {"A": "Start immunosuppressive therapy for presumed autoimmune hepatitis, a management option relevant to liver disease care.", "B": "Treat the underlying thyroid disorder and recheck liver enzymes.", "C": "Begin therapeutic phlebotomy, a treatment strategy applied in certain hepatobiliary conditions.", "D": "Start antiviral therapy for presumed viral hepatitis, a management option relevant to liver disease care.", "E": "Proceed directly to liver biopsy before treating the thyroid disease."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Treat the underlying thyroid disorder and recheck liver enzymes). Thyroid disease, both hyper- and hypothyroidism, is associated with mild transaminase abnormalities that typically resolve once the thyroid disorder is corrected. Immunosuppressive therapy is not indicated without evidence of autoimmune hepatitis. Phlebotomy treats iron overload, unrelated to thyroid disease. Antiviral therapy is not indicated without confirmed viral infection, and biopsy is not the first step before addressing an identified, treatable cause."}, {"id": 771, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "While teaching a group of internal medicine residents about liver disease, an attending physician poses the following question for discussion. A patient who received a liver transplant for MASH-related cirrhosis regains significant weight and develops recurrent steatosis on post-transplant biopsy five years later. What does this finding represent?", "options": {"A": "Recurrence of the original liver disease in the transplanted organ.", "B": "A new, unrelated viral hepatitis infection, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "C": "Acute cellular rejection, a diagnosis within the differential for liver dysfunction.", "D": "Hepatic artery thrombosis, a diagnosis within the differential for liver dysfunction.", "E": "Primary graft nonfunction, a diagnosis within the differential for liver dysfunction."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Recurrence of the original liver disease in the transplanted organ). MASLD and MASH are well recognized to recur after transplant, particularly with ongoing metabolic risk factors such as weight regain. A new unrelated viral infection would not typically present as steatosis on biopsy. Acute cellular rejection has a different histologic appearance dominated by inflammatory infiltrate around bile ducts and vessels, not steatosis. Hepatic artery thrombosis and primary graft nonfunction are early postoperative complications, not findings five years after transplant."}, {"id": 774, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "In preparing teaching material for a hepatology board review course, a program director includes the following clinical question for trainees to consider. Which two scoring systems are most commonly used to assess preoperative surgical risk in patients with cirrhosis? This is a concept frequently emphasized on certifying board examinations.", "options": {"A": "Maddrey discriminant function and Rumack-Matthew nomogram, a clinical marker referenced in hepatobiliary evaluation.", "B": "FIB-4 index and Glasgow Coma Scale, a clinical marker referenced in hepatobiliary evaluation.", "C": "Discriminant function and Ranson criteria, a clinical marker referenced in hepatobiliary evaluation.", "D": "Child-Turcotte-Pugh score and MELD score, a clinical marker referenced in hepatobiliary evaluation.", "E": "APACHE II score and Wells score, a clinical marker referenced in hepatobiliary evaluation."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Child-Turcotte-Pugh score and MELD score, a clinical marker referenced in hepatobiliary evaluation). The Maddrey discriminant function assesses alcoholic hepatitis severity, and the Rumack-Matthew nomogram guides acetaminophen overdose management, neither being a surgical risk tool. The FIB-4 index estimates fibrosis, not surgical risk, and the Glasgow Coma Scale assesses neurologic status. Ranson criteria assess pancreatitis severity, and APACHE II and Wells scores address general critical illness and thromboembolism risk, none specific to cirrhotic surgical risk."}, {"id": 781, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "In preparing teaching material for a hepatology board review course, a program director includes the following clinical question for trainees to consider. Why are patients with advanced cirrhosis at increased risk for both bacterial and fungal infections? This is a concept frequently emphasized on certifying board examinations.", "options": {"A": "Impaired immune function that accompanies advanced liver disease.", "B": "Universally elevated white blood cell counts that paradoxically increase infection risk.", "C": "Excess iron deposition throughout the reticuloendothelial system, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "D": "Increased ceruloplasmin production suppressing normal immunity, a clinical marker referenced in hepatobiliary evaluation.", "E": "Chronic hyperthyroidism suppressing immune surveillance, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Impaired immune function that accompanies advanced liver disease). Increases susceptibility to both bacterial and fungal infections. Elevated white blood cell counts are not a universal or causal feature explaining increased infection risk in cirrhosis. Iron deposition and ceruloplasmin changes are not established mechanisms of the immune dysfunction seen in cirrhosis. Chronic hyperthyroidism is unrelated to the immune impairment characteristic of advanced liver disease."}, {"id": 783, "domain": "liver_disorders", "question": "In preparing teaching material for a hepatology board review course, a program director includes the following clinical question for trainees to consider. A patient's hepatitis B panel shows HBsAg negative, IgM anti-HBc negative, total anti-HBc positive, and anti-HBs positive. What does this pattern most likely represent?", "options": {"A": "Acute hepatitis B infection, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "B": "Chronic hepatitis B infection, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "C": "Resolved natural hepatitis B infection, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "D": "Isolated vaccination without natural infection, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology.", "E": "Active hepatitis D superinfection, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Resolved natural hepatitis B infection, an entity relevant to this area of hepatology). Total anti-HBc positivity indicates past natural infection while a negative HBsAg and positive anti-HBs show that the virus has been cleared with resulting immunity. Acute infection requires HBsAg and typically IgM anti-HBc positivity, neither of which is present. Chronic infection requires ongoing HBsAg positivity, which is absent here. Vaccination alone would not produce a positive total anti-HBc, and there is no information here suggesting hepatitis D superinfection."}, {"id": 792, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A 52-year-old man with a long history of alcohol use and recurrent pancreatitis reports chronic abdominal pain and greasy, foul-smelling stools. Axial CT is shown. Which finding on this image best explains his current presentation?", "options": {"A": "Multiple calcifications scattered throughout the pancreatic parenchyma.", "B": "A single filling defect within the mid common bile duct.", "C": "Free air tracking along the anterior abdominal wall.", "D": "A thickened gallbladder wall with pericholecystic fluid.", "E": "An enhancing solid mass in the pancreatic head with vascular invasion."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Multiple calcifications scattered throughout the pancreatic parenchyma): scattered pancreatic calcifications on CT in a patient with chronic pain, steatorrhea, and long-standing alcohol use are the classic imaging hallmark of chronic pancreatitis, reflecting years of fibrosis and ductal calcification. A bile duct filling defect describes a stone, not parenchymal chronic disease. Free air suggests perforation, an unrelated acute process. A gallbladder wall finding would suggest cholecystitis, which does not explain steatorrhea.", "image": "question_images/q_792.jpg"}, {"id": 795, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A physiology lecture reviews pancreatic acinar cell secretion. Trypsinogen released into the duodenal lumen must be converted to active trypsin before it can digest proteins, and this activation step is delayed until the enzyme actually reaches the small bowel. Which mechanism is responsible for this conversion?", "options": {"A": "Direct activation of trypsinogen by bicarbonate secreted from pancreatic ductal cells.", "B": "Cleavage of trypsinogen by enterokinase located on the duodenal brush border.", "C": "Cleavage of trypsinogen by residual pepsin carried over from the stomach.", "D": "Activation of trypsinogen by bile acids released from the gallbladder after eating.", "E": "Autoactivation of trypsinogen triggered by a drop in duodenal pH below three."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cleavage of trypsinogen by enterokinase located on the duodenal brush border): trypsinogen is converted to active trypsin by enterokinase, an enzyme anchored on the duodenal brush border, which is why activation is delayed until pancreatic secretions actually reach the small bowel lumen and cannot digest the pancreas itself beforehand. Bicarbonate neutralizes acid but does not cleave trypsinogen. Pepsin acts on different substrates within the stomach. Bile acids emulsify fat rather than activating protein-digesting enzymes."}, {"id": 797, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A medical student asks which single hormone coordinates both gallbladder contraction and relaxation of the sphincter of Oddi after a fatty meal, allowing bile to reach the duodenum at the same time food arrives there for digestion.", "options": {"A": "Secretin released by duodenal cells mainly in response to acid exposure.", "B": "Vasoactive intestinal peptide acting alone without any other hormonal input.", "C": "Gastrin released from the gastric antrum promoting acid secretion.", "D": "Cholecystokinin released by duodenal cells in response to fat and protein.", "E": "Glucagon-like peptide 1 released to enhance insulin secretion and satiety."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cholecystokinin released by duodenal cells in response to fat and protein): cholecystokinin is released by the duodenum in response to dietary fat and protein, causing gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation so that bile and pancreatic enzymes reach the duodenum together with the meal. Secretin mainly stimulates bicarbonate secretion rather than gallbladder emptying. Gastrin primarily promotes gastric acid release rather than biliary coordination. Glucagon-like peptide 1 affects insulin secretion and satiety, not biliary emptying."}, {"id": 800, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A patient with chronic bile acid diarrhea related to ileal disease is being started on a bile acid sequestrant. The prescribing physician is asked to justify this choice based on the underlying physiology of the disease process being treated.", "options": {"A": "Sequestrants bind unabsorbed bile acids, preventing colonic secretory stimulation and diarrhea.", "B": "Sequestrants directly inhibit cholecystokinin release from duodenal enteroendocrine cells.", "C": "Sequestrants increase pancreatic lipase activity to compensate for fat malabsorption.", "D": "Sequestrants stimulate additional hepatic bile acid synthesis to replace ileal losses.", "E": "Sequestrants neutralize excess gastric acid delivered into the proximal duodenum."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Sequestrants bind unabsorbed bile acids, preventing colonic secretory stimulation and diarrhea): bile acid sequestrants bind excess bile acids in the intestinal lumen that were not reabsorbed because of ileal disease, preventing them from reaching the colon and triggering secretory diarrhea there. They do not act on cholecystokinin release, lipase activity, hepatic synthesis, or gastric acid, so none of those alternative mechanisms explain their therapeutic benefit in this condition."}, {"id": 805, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A 19-year-old woman undergoes abdominal imaging for vague, intermittent abdominal pain and is found to have a fusiform dilation of the extrahepatic bile duct with normal surrounding liver parenchyma. Given the known natural history of this anomaly, what is the most appropriate management step?", "options": {"A": "Reassurance with no further follow-up since these lesions are always benign.", "B": "Immediate liver transplantation as the only definitive available treatment.", "C": "Surgical excision of the cyst given its elevated long-term cancer risk.", "D": "Lifelong ursodeoxycholic acid therapy without any surgical evaluation.", "E": "Percutaneous cyst aspiration alone as a definitive treatment strategy."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Surgical excision of the cyst given its elevated long-term cancer risk): choledochal cysts carry a meaningfully increased long-term risk of cholangiocarcinoma, so surgical excision rather than simple observation or drainage is the standard recommended management once the diagnosis is confirmed. Reassurance alone ignores this malignancy risk entirely. Liver transplantation is not indicated for an isolated bile duct cyst, and medical therapy or simple aspiration do not address the structural lesion or its associated cancer risk."}, {"id": 808, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A hepatobiliary surgeon explains to a trainee that an abnormally long common channel, where the pancreatic and bile ducts join far outside the duodenal wall, allows reflux of activated pancreatic enzymes into the bile duct and predisposes to which specific congenital condition?", "options": {"A": "Annular pancreas, an unrelated anomaly involving duodenal encirclement.", "B": "Pancreas divisum, an unrelated ductal fusion variant of the pancreas.", "C": "Primary sclerosing cholangitis, an autoimmune-associated stricturing bile duct disease.", "D": "Gilbert syndrome, an unrelated disorder of bilirubin conjugation.", "E": "Choledochal cyst formation from chronic enzyme-mediated bile duct wall injury."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Choledochal cyst formation from chronic enzyme-mediated bile duct wall injury): an abnormally long common pancreaticobiliary channel is thought to allow reflux of activated pancreatic enzymes into the bile duct, contributing to bile duct wall injury and choledochal cyst formation over time. Annular pancreas and pancreas divisum are separate congenital anomalies unrelated to channel length. Primary sclerosing cholangitis is an autoimmune-associated stricturing disease, and Gilbert syndrome is a bilirubin conjugation disorder, neither related to ductal channel anatomy."}, {"id": 809, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A hepatobiliary anatomy question asks which two structures join directly to form the common hepatic duct, before that duct then combines with the cystic duct to eventually form the common bile duct further downstream in the biliary tree.", "options": {"A": "The cystic duct and main pancreatic duct joining near the ampulla.", "B": "The right and left hepatic ducts joining just outside the liver.", "C": "The common bile duct and pancreatic duct joining at the ampulla.", "D": "The right and left portal veins joining near the porta hepatis.", "E": "The accessory and main pancreatic ducts joining within the gland itself."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The right and left hepatic ducts joining just outside the liver): the right and left hepatic ducts join to form the common hepatic duct, which then combines with the cystic duct from the gallbladder to form the common bile duct. The cystic duct actually joins the common hepatic duct rather than the pancreatic duct, and the common bile duct and pancreatic duct join further downstream at the ampulla. Portal veins are vascular structures rather than biliary ducts, and accessory pancreatic duct union is a separate anatomic concept."}, {"id": 812, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A 41-year-old woman develops new gallstones eight months after undergoing bariatric surgery with rapid postoperative weight loss. Which mechanism best explains why rapid weight loss is a well-established risk factor for cholesterol gallstone formation in this clinical setting?", "options": {"A": "Rapid cholesterol mobilization into bile outpacing available bile acid solubilization.", "B": "Chronic hemolysis increasing bilirubin pigment load within stored bile.", "C": "Cirrhosis with portal hypertension altering hepatic bile acid synthesis.", "D": "Recurrent biliary tract infection promoting bacterial deconjugation of bile acids.", "E": "Advanced age alone independent of any change in body weight."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rapid cholesterol mobilization into bile outpacing available bile acid solubilization): rapid weight loss mobilizes cholesterol into bile faster than bile acids and phospholipids can keep it in solution, promoting cholesterol supersaturation and stone formation. Chronic hemolysis and biliary infection are more associated with pigment stone formation rather than cholesterol stones. Cirrhosis is itself more of a pigment stone risk factor, and age alone without a weight change does not explain this particular postoperative risk."}, {"id": 823, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A hepatobiliary surgery conference discusses a patient whose imaging shows a gallstone impacted within the cystic duct causing extrinsic compression and narrowing of the adjacent common hepatic duct, resulting in obstructive jaundice. Which named clinical syndrome does this specific scenario represent?", "options": {"A": "A stone freely migrating from the gallbladder directly into the duodenum.", "B": "Mirizzi syndrome from extrinsic common hepatic duct compression by a cystic duct stone.", "C": "Intrahepatic bile duct strictures resulting from autoimmune inflammatory disease.", "D": "A congenital web causing obstruction of the distal common bile duct.", "E": "Pneumobilia resulting from a chronic cholecystoenteric fistula."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Mirizzi syndrome from extrinsic common hepatic duct compression by a cystic duct stone): Mirizzi syndrome occurs when a stone impacted in the cystic duct or gallbladder neck extrinsically compresses the adjacent common hepatic duct, causing obstructive jaundice from external compression rather than an intraluminal duct stone. A stone passing into the duodenum instead describes gallstone ileus, a different entity. Autoimmune intrahepatic strictures describe primary sclerosing cholangitis, and a congenital web or pneumobilia from a fistula are distinct findings unrelated to Mirizzi syndrome's mechanism."}, {"id": 829, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A hepatology fellow preparing a patient education handout for primary sclerosing cholangitis needs to summarize the malignancies for which this disease significantly increases lifetime risk, given its known associations with both the biliary tree and underlying inflammatory bowel disease.", "options": {"A": "Hepatocellular carcinoma and renal cell carcinoma as the primary concerns.", "B": "Gastric cancer and esophageal adenocarcinoma as the primary concerns.", "C": "Cholangiocarcinoma and colorectal cancer, reflecting biliary and IBD-related risk.", "D": "Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor and thyroid cancer as the primary concerns.", "E": "Testicular cancer and lymphoma as the primary associated malignancies."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cholangiocarcinoma and colorectal cancer, reflecting biliary and IBD-related risk): primary sclerosing cholangitis carries an increased lifetime risk of cholangiocarcinoma from the diseased bile ducts and colorectal cancer related to its strong association with inflammatory bowel disease. Hepatocellular and renal cell carcinoma are not the classic associated malignancies for this disease. Gastric, esophageal, pancreatic neuroendocrine, thyroid, testicular, and lymphoma risks are not the recognized hallmark cancer associations of primary sclerosing cholangitis."}, {"id": 832, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A medical student preparing for rounds is asked to describe the typical demographic profile of patients diagnosed with primary biliary cholangitis, since recognizing this pattern helps distinguish it clinically from primary sclerosing cholangitis, which tends to affect a different population.", "options": {"A": "Young adult men, similar in demographic pattern to sclerosing cholangitis.", "B": "Elderly men with a significant history of alcohol use disorder.", "C": "Adolescent females presenting typically before the age of eighteen.", "D": "Neonates with congenital biliary atresia diagnosed shortly after birth.", "E": "Middle-aged women, in contrast to the younger male predominance of PSC."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Middle-aged women, in contrast to the younger male predominance of PSC): primary biliary cholangitis predominantly affects middle-aged women, distinguishing its typical demographic from primary sclerosing cholangitis, which more often affects younger men with inflammatory bowel disease. Young adult men and elderly men with alcohol use are not the characteristic population for primary biliary cholangitis. Adolescent females and neonates with biliary atresia represent entirely different age groups and disease processes unrelated to this specific condition."}, {"id": 836, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A patient with confirmed choledocholithiasis and associated mild acute pancreatitis undergoes ERCP with successful stone removal and improves clinically over the following two days. What is the most appropriate definitive management step to address the underlying source of his gallstones before hospital discharge?", "options": {"A": "Cholecystectomy performed during this same hospital admission.", "B": "No further intervention needed since the duct stone has already been removed.", "C": "Delayed cholecystectomy scheduled electively six months later regardless of severity.", "D": "Long-term ursodeoxycholic acid therapy used instead of surgical removal.", "E": "Repeat ERCP alone performed without any subsequent cholecystectomy."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cholecystectomy performed during this same hospital admission): in mild gallstone pancreatitis, cholecystectomy during the same admission is recommended because delaying it increases the risk of recurrent gallstone-related events, including further pancreatitis or cholangitis, before a later elective surgery date. Doing nothing further leaves the gallbladder as an ongoing stone source. Routinely delaying surgery six months in mild disease is not the preferred approach, ursodeoxycholic acid does not dissolve most stones effectively enough to replace surgery, and repeat ERCP alone does not remove the gallbladder reservoir of stones."}, {"id": 841, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A 30-year-old woman with poorly controlled diabetes presents with acute pancreatitis, and her serum triglyceride level returns markedly elevated at 1400 mg/dL. Despite this clear metabolic abnormality, which additional evaluation should still be pursued before attributing her pancreatitis solely to hypertriglyceridemia as the cause?", "options": {"A": "Iron studies to evaluate for underlying hereditary hemochromatosis.", "B": "Serum ceruloplasmin testing to evaluate for underlying Wilson disease.", "C": "Alpha-1 antitrypsin phenotype testing for an unrelated genetic deficiency.", "D": "Coagulation factor level testing to evaluate for underlying hemophilia.", "E": "Right upper quadrant ultrasound to exclude a coexisting gallstone etiology."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Right upper quadrant ultrasound to exclude a coexisting gallstone etiology): even with markedly elevated triglycerides, gallstones remain common enough that right upper quadrant ultrasound should still be performed to exclude a coexisting biliary cause requiring its own specific management approach. Hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency are causes of chronic liver disease rather than typical acute pancreatitis triggers, and hemophilia is a bleeding disorder entirely unrelated to pancreatitis etiology."}, {"id": 843, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A hospitalist reviewing the Revised Atlanta Classification with a resident asks which specific combination of features defines moderately severe acute pancreatitis, distinguishing it from both the mild category and the more severe category based on organ failure duration and associated complications.", "options": {"A": "Persistent organ failure of 48 hours or more with local complications.", "B": "Transient organ failure under 48 hours with local or systemic complications.", "C": "Complete absence of both organ failure and any complications whatsoever.", "D": "Any degree of pancreatic necrosis regardless of organ function duration.", "E": "A single elevated white blood cell count without organ dysfunction."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Transient organ failure under 48 hours with local or systemic complications): moderately severe acute pancreatitis is defined by transient organ failure, meaning it resolves in less than 48 hours, occurring alongside local complications such as fluid collections or systemic complications like exacerbation of an existing comorbidity. Persistent organ failure of 48 hours or more defines severe disease instead, and the complete absence of organ failure and complications defines mild disease. Necrosis alone without regard to organ failure duration, and an isolated white count elevation, do not by themselves define this category."}, {"id": 846, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A radiology teaching file explains the time-based terminology used for peripancreatic collections in acute pancreatitis. At what minimum time point after symptom onset may an acute peripancreatic fluid collection begin to evolve into a mature pancreatic pseudocyst with a defined wall, according to standard Atlanta terminology?", "options": {"A": "Twenty four hours after the initial onset of symptoms.", "B": "Seventy two hours after the initial onset of symptoms.", "C": "Six months after the initial onset of symptoms.", "D": "One week after the initial onset of symptoms.", "E": "Four weeks after the initial onset of acute pancreatitis symptoms."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Four weeks after the initial onset of acute pancreatitis symptoms): acute peripancreatic fluid collections are classified within the first four weeks, and if they persist beyond that point and develop a defined wall, they are then reclassified as pancreatic pseudocysts under standard terminology. Timeframes as short as 24 hours, 72 hours, or one week are too early for this specific structural evolution to occur, and six months substantially overstates the four-week threshold actually used in the Atlanta framework."}, {"id": 859, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A patient with severe acute pancreatitis and extensive pancreatic necrosis identified on CT imaging remains clinically stable, afebrile, and without any worsening laboratory abnormalities throughout the first two weeks of hospitalization. What is the most appropriate management approach toward the necrosis itself at this particular point in the clinical course?", "options": {"A": "Immediate necrosectomy performed regardless of the patient's current clinical stability.", "B": "Empiric broad-spectrum antibiotics started for all necrosis regardless of infection signs.", "C": "Percutaneous drainage of all necrotic collections regardless of associated symptoms.", "D": "Continued supportive care and close observation without any active intervention.", "E": "Total pancreatectomy performed prophylactically to prevent any future infection risk."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Continued supportive care and close observation without any active intervention): sterile necrosis in a clinically stable patient without signs of infection is generally managed conservatively with observation, since intervention is reserved specifically for infected necrosis or symptomatic, complicated collections. Proceeding directly to necrosectomy in a stable patient without infection is unnecessarily invasive, empiric antibiotics are not recommended for sterile necrosis without evidence of infection, and draining all collections regardless of symptoms or performing prophylactic total pancreatectomy both represent overly aggressive management not supported by current practice."}, {"id": 863, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A 24-year-old patient without any history of alcohol use or gallstones develops recurrent episodes of acute pancreatitis that eventually progress to established chronic pancreatitis on imaging. Given her young age and absence of typical risk factors, which category of underlying causes should be specifically prioritized in her further evaluation?", "options": {"A": "BRCA1 and BRCA2 mutations, which relate to breast and ovarian cancer risk.", "B": "Factor V Leiden mutation, which causes an inherited thrombophilia instead.", "C": "Genetic mutations in PRSS1, SPINK1, or CFTR should be evaluated.", "D": "JAK2 mutation, which relates to myeloproliferative disorders rather than pancreatitis.", "E": "HFE mutation, which causes hereditary hemochromatosis rather than pancreatitis."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Genetic mutations in PRSS1, SPINK1, or CFTR should be evaluated): PRSS1, SPINK1, and CFTR mutations are the genetic causes classically considered in younger patients with chronic pancreatitis lacking typical risk factors like alcohol use. BRCA mutations relate to breast and ovarian cancer risk rather than pancreatitis. Factor V Leiden causes thrombophilia and JAK2 mutation relates to myeloproliferative disorders, neither of which cause chronic pancreatitis, and HFE mutation causes hereditary hemochromatosis, a different disease process affecting iron metabolism entirely."}, {"id": 864, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A 55-year-old man with a 20-year history of heavy alcohol use presents with chronic abdominal pain and new-onset bulky, foul-smelling stools, and his fecal elastase level returns markedly low on outpatient testing. What is the underlying pathophysiologic explanation for his new diarrhea symptoms in this specific clinical context?", "options": {"A": "Excess pancreatic bicarbonate secretion producing an osmotic diarrhea pattern.", "B": "Overproduction of pancreatic lipase resulting in unusually rapid fat digestion.", "C": "Increased gastric acid secretion from underlying parietal cell hyperplasia.", "D": "Progressive loss of exocrine enzyme output below the threshold for digestion.", "E": "Enhanced bile acid reabsorption occurring within the terminal ileum."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Progressive loss of exocrine enzyme output below the threshold for digestion): chronic pancreatitis eventually depletes functional exocrine tissue below the threshold needed for adequate digestion, and since lipase activity is lost earliest among the major enzymes, fat malabsorption and steatorrhea result, consistent with this patient's low fecal elastase. Excess bicarbonate secretion is not a feature of pancreatic insufficiency, which instead reflects reduced secretory capacity overall. Overproduction of lipase is the opposite of what occurs in this disease, and increased gastric acid or enhanced bile acid reabsorption do not explain steatorrhea from pancreatic exocrine failure."}, {"id": 866, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A patient with clinical suspicion for early chronic pancreatitis undergoes a standard abdominal CT scan that returns entirely normal, despite ongoing symptoms suggestive of the disease. Given the known limitations of CT sensitivity in early-stage disease, what is the most appropriate next diagnostic option to pursue?", "options": {"A": "Endoscopic ultrasound or MRCP for more sensitive detection of subtle changes.", "B": "Simply repeating the identical CT scan protocol again in one week.", "C": "Obtaining a plain abdominal radiograph as the next diagnostic study.", "D": "Doppler ultrasound evaluation specifically of the portal venous system.", "E": "A nuclear medicine bone scan performed to evaluate for metastatic disease."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Endoscopic ultrasound or MRCP for more sensitive detection of subtle changes): endoscopic ultrasound and MRCP are more sensitive than CT for detecting the subtle ductal and parenchymal changes of early chronic pancreatitis before calcifications have had time to develop. Simply repeating the same CT protocol a week later would not meaningfully add sensitivity. A plain abdominal film has poor sensitivity for pancreatic detail, and Doppler evaluation of the portal vein or a bone scan are not relevant tools for evaluating early chronic pancreatitis."}, {"id": 867, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A specialized pancreatology center is asked which specific diagnostic test is considered the most sensitive available method for detecting early functional pancreatic impairment, despite this test's limited availability outside of a small number of highly specialized referral centers nationally.", "options": {"A": "Fecal elastase measurement, which is widely available but less sensitive early on.", "B": "Secretin stimulation testing, the most sensitive test despite limited availability.", "C": "Serum amylase level, which is not a sensitive measure of functional reserve.", "D": "Abdominal ultrasound, which is not a sensitive measure of functional reserve.", "E": "A complete blood count, which provides no information about pancreatic function."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Secretin stimulation testing, the most sensitive test despite limited availability): secretin stimulation testing is the most sensitive available test for early functional pancreatic impairment, though it requires specialized equipment and expertise that limits its widespread clinical use. Fecal elastase is more widely available but becomes abnormal only after a substantial portion of function is already lost. Serum amylase, ultrasound, and a complete blood count are not sensitive measures of early functional pancreatic reserve."}, {"id": 871, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A patient with chronic pancreatitis and debilitating pain has already tried lifestyle modification, complete alcohol cessation, and maximally dosed non-opioid analgesics without any meaningful symptom relief over several months. Which commonly attempted oral therapy is typically tried next but has not reliably demonstrated a clear pain benefit across most published clinical trials?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous antibiotics, which have no established role in this specific setting.", "B": "Pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy, specifically when used for pain control.", "C": "Systemic corticosteroids, generally reserved for autoimmune pancreatitis rather than this disease.", "D": "High-dose proton pump inhibitor therapy used as primary pain treatment.", "E": "Insulin therapy initiated regardless of the patient's current glucose status."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy, specifically when used for pain control): pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy is often tried for pain in chronic pancreatitis but most trials have not reliably shown it reduces pain, even though it remains important for treating associated malabsorption. Antibiotics have no established role in chronic pancreatitis pain unless there is a specific identified infection. Corticosteroids are specifically used in autoimmune pancreatitis rather than typical chronic pancreatitis broadly, and high-dose proton pump inhibitors and insulin address different problems entirely and are not standard pain therapies here."}, {"id": 881, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A patient with confirmed exocrine pancreatic insufficiency continues to experience persistent steatorrhea despite being prescribed an adequate dose of pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy that he reports taking correctly and consistently with every meal. Which additional condition should be specifically evaluated next given this lack of expected clinical response?", "options": {"A": "Immediate discontinuation of all current pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy.", "B": "Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth or celiac disease as a concurrent condition.", "C": "Referral for consideration of total pancreatectomy as the next treatment step.", "D": "Assumption that no further diagnostic workup is needed since enzymes were prescribed.", "E": "Switching the patient exclusively to a fat-free diet without any other testing."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth or celiac disease as a concurrent condition): when adequate enzyme dosing fails to control symptoms, clinicians should evaluate for concurrent conditions like small intestinal bacterial overgrowth or celiac disease that can mimic or worsen malabsorption independent of pancreatic enzyme levels. Discontinuing enzyme therapy would likely worsen his digestion further rather than help. Total pancreatectomy is not an appropriate response to persistent symptoms already on therapy, assuming no further workup is needed ignores a persistent clinical problem, and a fat-free diet alone does not address a possible concurrent underlying diagnosis."}, {"id": 888, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A pancreatic physiology review explains why lipase activity is consistently lost earlier than protease or amylase activity as pancreatic exocrine tissue is progressively damaged over the course of chronic disease, a pattern that helps explain why fat malabsorption is typically the earliest clinical symptom to appear.", "options": {"A": "Lipase is not actually produced by the pancreas at all under normal physiology.", "B": "Proteases are destroyed essentially instantly upon secretion into the duodenal lumen.", "C": "Amylase is produced exclusively outside the pancreas in essentially all individuals.", "D": "Lipase secretory reserve declines earlier than other enzymes with progressive gland damage.", "E": "Lipase requires no cofactors at all and should theoretically be the most resistant enzyme."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lipase secretory reserve declines earlier than other enzymes with progressive gland damage): lipase secretory reserve tends to decline earlier relative to protease and amylase activity as pancreatic exocrine tissue is progressively damaged, which is why fat malabsorption is typically the earliest clinical manifestation of this process. Lipase is indeed produced by the pancreas, contrary to that option. Proteases are not destroyed instantly upon secretion under normal physiology. Amylase does have a separate salivary counterpart, but pancreatic amylase is still produced by the gland itself, and lipase is not inherently the most resistant enzyme, since clinical experience actually shows the opposite pattern."}, {"id": 890, "domain": "pancreas_biliary_disorders", "question": "A patient with acute pancreatitis has organ failure documented on hospital day one that resolves by hour 40, but a new episode of organ failure then develops on day four and persists through day seven before finally resolving. Considering the overall hospital course rather than either episode alone, how should this presentation be classified under the Revised Atlanta Classification?", "options": {"A": "Severe acute pancreatitis, since the total course reflects persistent organ dysfunction.", "B": "Mild acute pancreatitis, since the first episode alone resolved within 48 hours.", "C": "Moderately severe acute pancreatitis, since neither episode individually lasted 48 hours.", "D": "This case cannot be classified since two separate episodes of organ failure occurred.", "E": "Chronic pancreatitis with an acute exacerbation rather than a single acute episode."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Severe acute pancreatitis, since the total course reflects persistent organ dysfunction): when organ failure recurs and the overall pattern reflects dysfunction extending well beyond 48 hours cumulatively during a single hospitalization for acute pancreatitis, the clinical course is treated as severe disease given the persistent, non-transient nature of the organ dysfunction overall. Classifying this as mild ignores the second, more prolonged episode entirely. Calling it moderately severe by only counting each episode in isolation misses the overall persistent pattern across the admission, the case can and should be classified using the Atlanta framework rather than left unclassified, and this is an acute rather than chronic disease process given the timeline described."}, {"id": 891, "domain": "other_disorders", "question": "A 68-year-old woman reports six months of chronic watery, non-bloody diarrhea occurring several times daily, including at night. Colonoscopy shows a normal-appearing mucosa throughout. Random biopsies are obtained. The photomicrograph shows the histologic finding responsible for her symptoms. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Collagenous colitis, a subtype of microscopic colitis with a thickened collagen band.", "B": "Lymphocytic colitis, a microscopic colitis subtype without a thickened collagen band.", "C": "Celiac disease with villous atrophy seen on small bowel biopsy specimens.", "D": "Ulcerative colitis with grossly visible mucosal inflammation and friability on colonoscopy.", "E": "Irritable bowel syndrome with a structurally and histologically normal colon lining."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Collagenous colitis, a subtype of microscopic colitis with a thickened collagen band): The thickened subepithelial collagen band on histology in a woman with chronic watery diarrhea and a grossly normal colonoscopy is diagnostic of collagenous colitis, a subtype of microscopic colitis. Lymphocytic colitis is ruled out because it lacks the thickened band, showing increased intraepithelial lymphocytes instead. Celiac disease and ulcerative colitis would show different histologic patterns, and irritable bowel syndrome is a diagnosis of exclusion that cannot explain an abnormal biopsy finding.", "image": "question_images/q_891.jpg"}, {"id": 892, "domain": "other_disorders", "question": "An 82-year-old nursing home resident with chronic constipation presents with progressive abdominal distention and no bowel movement for four days. An abdominal x-ray is obtained and shown. Based on the imaging finding, which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Place a nasogastric tube and repeat the abdominal x-ray in one week.", "B": "Attempt endoscopic decompression with a flexible sigmoidoscope if no ischemia is suspected.", "C": "Perform immediate exploratory laparotomy without attempting any endoscopic intervention first.", "D": "Administer a large volume tap water enema to relieve the obstruction.", "E": "Start intravenous antibiotics alone and observe for spontaneous resolution overnight."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Attempt endoscopic decompression with a flexible sigmoidoscope if no ischemia is suspected): The x-ray finding described, a dilated loop of colon bending back on itself, is the coffee bean sign of sigmoid volvulus. In a stable patient without peritoneal signs or evidence of ischemia, endoscopic decompression is the appropriate first step. Immediate surgery is reserved for signs of strangulation or perforation. Enemas and antibiotics alone do not address the mechanical obstruction, and delaying intervention risks bowel ischemia.", "image": "question_images/q_892.jpg"}, {"id": 894, "domain": "other_disorders", "question": "A 71-year-old woman on chronic omeprazole and sertraline develops four months of watery, non-bloody diarrhea without weight loss. Colonoscopy is macroscopically normal. Biopsies confirm microscopic colitis. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial step in management?", "options": {"A": "Refer immediately for total colectomy given the chronicity of symptoms.", "B": "Start a biologic agent used for moderate to severe ulcerative colitis.", "C": "Reassure the patient that no treatment is needed for this condition.", "D": "Discontinue proton pump inhibitor and SSRI use and start budesonide therapy.", "E": "Begin high dose systemic prednisone as first line maintenance therapy."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Discontinue proton pump inhibitor and SSRI use and start budesonide therapy): Proton pump inhibitors and SSRIs are both strongly associated with microscopic colitis, so stopping the offending agent is an important adjunct to therapy. Budesonide is first line treatment because its high first pass hepatic metabolism limits systemic steroid exposure compared with prednisone. Colectomy is not indicated for this benign, medically responsive condition. A biologic is reserved for refractory inflammatory bowel disease, not typical microscopic colitis."}, {"id": 896, "domain": "other_disorders", "question": "A 70-year-old woman with a history of hypothyroidism presents with several months of chronic watery, non-bloody diarrhea. She takes NSAIDs regularly for joint pain. Colonoscopy shows normal-appearing mucosa. Which of the following best describes the typical demographic and risk profile for her most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Older men, with associations to prostate disease and testosterone therapy.", "B": "Older women, with associations to NSAID use and autoimmune thyroid disease.", "C": "Young men, with associations to heavy alcohol use and smoking.", "D": "Infants, with associations to cow's milk protein allergy exposure.", "E": "Pregnant women, with associations to iron supplementation during pregnancy."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Older women, with associations to NSAID use and autoimmune thyroid disease): Microscopic colitis classically affects older women and is strongly associated with NSAID use, proton pump inhibitors, SSRIs, and autoimmune conditions such as thyroid disease and celiac disease. It is not a disease of young men, infants, or pregnant women. Older men are affected far less often than older women in most reported cohorts."}, {"id": 899, "domain": "other_disorders", "question": "A 45-year-old woman has chronic watery diarrhea that persists even when she fasts for 48 hours in the hospital. Stool osmotic gap is calculated and found to be low. Which of the following mechanisms best explains her diarrhea?", "options": {"A": "Osmotic diarrhea from unabsorbed lactose or a poorly absorbed laxative.", "B": "Motility related diarrhea from rapid transit without a secretory or osmotic cause.", "C": "Factitious diarrhea from surreptitious laxative use with a normal osmotic gap.", "D": "Inflammatory diarrhea explained entirely by mucosal ulceration without a secretory component.", "E": "Secretory diarrhea from a process such as a VIPoma or bacterial toxin."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Secretory diarrhea from a process such as a VIPoma or bacterial toxin): Secretory diarrhea persists during fasting and has a normal or low stool osmotic gap, consistent with a secretory process such as a VIPoma, carcinoid syndrome, or bacterial toxin. Osmotic diarrhea improves with fasting and produces an elevated osmotic gap, which does not match this presentation. Motility related and factitious diarrhea are less consistent with a low osmotic gap that persists despite fasting, and inflammatory diarrhea usually shows additional signs such as blood or elevated calprotectin."}, {"id": 900, "domain": "other_disorders", "question": "A 52-year-old woman develops new onset chronic watery diarrhea eight weeks after an uncomplicated laparoscopic cholecystectomy. Stool studies, celiac serology, and colonoscopy with biopsies are all unremarkable. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for her diarrhea?", "options": {"A": "Bile acid diarrhea from continuous bile flow overwhelming ileal reabsorption capacity.", "B": "New onset celiac disease that was missed on the initial serologic testing.", "C": "Microscopic colitis that was missed because biopsies were not obtained.", "D": "Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth from an anatomic stricture at the surgical site.", "E": "Factitious diarrhea related to psychological stress after the surgical procedure."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Bile acid diarrhea from continuous bile flow overwhelming ileal reabsorption capacity): After cholecystectomy the gallbladder no longer buffers bile release, so continuous bile flow into the duodenum can overwhelm ileal reabsorption capacity and cause chronic watery diarrhea, a diagnosis supported by an otherwise unremarkable workup. Celiac disease and microscopic colitis were already excluded by serology and biopsy in this case. Bacterial overgrowth from a stricture is possible but far less common after a routine laparoscopic cholecystectomy, and there is no indication this is factitious."}, {"id": 901, "domain": "other_disorders", "question": "A gastroenterologist is evaluating a patient with chronic diarrhea and orders stool electrolytes to calculate the stool osmotic gap, a tool used to separate osmotic from secretory causes. Which of the following formulas correctly calculates this value?", "options": {"A": "Two times stool chloride divided by two hundred ninety minus sodium.", "B": "Serum sodium minus stool sodium divided by stool potassium concentration.", "C": "290 minus two times the sum of stool sodium and potassium.", "D": "Stool sodium divided by stool potassium multiplied by two hundred ninety.", "E": "Stool osmolality minus serum osmolality measured simultaneously on the same day."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (290 minus two times the sum of stool sodium and potassium): The stool osmotic gap is calculated as 290 minus two times the sum of stool sodium and potassium, using an assumed stool osmolality of 290. A high gap points to an osmotic cause, while a low gap points to a secretory cause. The other formulas listed do not reflect the accepted calculation and would not correctly classify the diarrhea."}, {"id": 902, "domain": "other_disorders", "question": "A 30-year-old woman taking a magnesium-containing antacid for heartburn develops watery diarrhea that resolves completely when she stops eating. Stool osmotic gap is markedly elevated. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Order a colonoscopy with biopsies before any further evaluation.", "B": "Start a bile acid sequestrant for presumed bile acid diarrhea.", "C": "Check methylmalonic acid to evaluate for vitamin B12 deficiency.", "D": "Discontinue the magnesium-containing antacid and reassess her symptoms.", "E": "Begin empiric antibiotic therapy for suspected bacterial overgrowth."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Discontinue the magnesium-containing antacid and reassess her symptoms): The clinical picture, diarrhea that resolves with fasting and an elevated stool osmotic gap, is classic for osmotic diarrhea from an unabsorbed solute such as magnesium, so stopping the likely culprit is the appropriate first step. Antibiotics, colonoscopy, and a bile acid sequestrant are not indicated without further evidence pointing to those causes. Checking methylmalonic acid addresses B12 status, which is unrelated to this presentation."}, {"id": 908, "domain": "other_disorders", "question": "A 55-year-old woman with long standing type 1 diabetes and diabetic autonomic neuropathy presents with bloating and diarrhea. Her physician suspects small intestinal bacterial overgrowth. Which of the following best explains her increased risk for this condition?", "options": {"A": "Diabetic neuropathy slows small bowel motility, allowing bacteria to accumulate.", "B": "Diabetes directly destroys small bowel mucosa, mimicking celiac disease.", "C": "Diabetes increases gastric acid production, promoting bacterial overgrowth.", "D": "Diabetes causes rapid transit, which increases bacterial clearance.", "E": "Diabetes has no established mechanistic link to bacterial overgrowth."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Diabetic neuropathy slows small bowel motility, allowing bacteria to accumulate): Diabetic autonomic neuropathy slows small bowel motility, which allows bacteria to accumulate rather than being cleared normally, predisposing to small intestinal bacterial overgrowth. Diabetes does not directly destroy small bowel mucosa the way celiac disease does, and it does not increase gastric acid production. Rapid transit would reduce rather than increase bacterial overgrowth risk, so the correct mechanism is impaired motility and bacterial clearance."}, {"id": 911, "domain": "other_disorders", "question": "A 48-year-old man with a history of multiple small bowel surgeries presents with bloating, excess flatulence, and watery diarrhea that worsens after carbohydrate-rich meals. Which of the following mechanisms best explains his bloating and gas symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Increased gastric acid secretion produces excess intraluminal gas production.", "B": "Colonic bacteria migrate proximally into the stomach causing symptoms.", "C": "Pancreatic enzyme excess accelerates carbohydrate digestion, producing gas.", "D": "Reduced bile acid production directly generates intestinal gas.", "E": "Premature bacterial fermentation of carbohydrates in the small bowel produces gas."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Premature bacterial fermentation of carbohydrates in the small bowel produces gas): In small intestinal bacterial overgrowth, bacteria that normally reside mainly in the colon ferment carbohydrates prematurely within the small bowel, producing bloating and gas along with diarrhea. Gastric acid changes do not directly explain this mechanism. The problem is bacteria overgrowing in the small bowel rather than migrating into the stomach, and pancreatic enzymes and bile acid levels are not the primary driver of the gas production described."}, {"id": 913, "domain": "other_disorders", "question": "A patient develops a painful, bluish, tender lump at the anal verge after straining, while a separate patient has painless bright red bleeding with bowel movements from tissue above the dentate line. Which of the following best explains the difference in pain between these two presentations?", "options": {"A": "Pain differences reflect hemorrhoid size rather than location or innervation.", "B": "External hemorrhoids are innervated by somatic nerves below the dentate line.", "C": "Internal hemorrhoids are more vascular and therefore more prone to pain.", "D": "External hemorrhoids never thrombose and are inherently a painless condition.", "E": "Internal hemorrhoids are innervated by somatic nerves above the dentate line."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (External hemorrhoids are innervated by somatic nerves below the dentate line): External hemorrhoids lie below the dentate line and are innervated by somatic nerves, so thrombosis there is exquisitely painful. Internal hemorrhoids lie above the dentate line, an area without somatic pain innervation, which is why they typically cause painless bleeding or prolapse rather than pain. External hemorrhoids can and do thrombose painfully, and the difference is explained by innervation and location rather than vascularity or size alone."}, {"id": 927, "domain": "other_disorders", "question": "A 78-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation not on anticoagulation presents with sudden severe abdominal pain that is markedly out of proportion to a benign-appearing abdominal exam. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Chronic mesenteric ischemia, managed with elective revascularization as an outpatient.", "B": "Acute diverticulitis, managed with oral antibiotics and outpatient follow up.", "C": "Sigmoid volvulus, managed initially with endoscopic decompression.", "D": "Acute mesenteric ischemia from embolic occlusion, requiring urgent CT angiography and surgery.", "E": "Ischemic colitis from a low flow state, managed with supportive bowel rest."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acute mesenteric ischemia from embolic occlusion, requiring urgent CT angiography and surgery): Pain out of proportion to exam findings in a patient with atrial fibrillation is classic for acute mesenteric ischemia from embolic occlusion of the superior mesenteric artery, a surgical emergency requiring urgent imaging and intervention. Ischemic colitis is usually a nonocclusive, lower flow process with a different clinical course. Chronic mesenteric ischemia presents with postprandial pain over time rather than acute severe pain, and diverticulitis and volvulus have distinct presentations not matching this vignette."}, {"id": 928, "domain": "other_disorders", "question": "A 74-year-old man reports postprandial abdominal pain for several months, leading him to avoid eating and lose significant weight. Angiography shows atherosclerotic narrowing of two of the three major mesenteric vessels. Which of the following best describes his condition?", "options": {"A": "Acute mesenteric ischemia from embolic occlusion of a single vessel.", "B": "Ischemic colitis from a transient nonocclusive low flow state.", "C": "Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth causing postprandial bloating and pain.", "D": "Chronic pancreatitis causing postprandial pain from enzyme insufficiency.", "E": "Chronic mesenteric ischemia, also called intestinal angina, from atherosclerotic disease."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Chronic mesenteric ischemia, also called intestinal angina, from atherosclerotic disease): Postprandial abdominal pain leading to food fear and weight loss, in the setting of atherosclerotic narrowing of at least two of the three mesenteric vessels, is the classic presentation of chronic mesenteric ischemia, also called intestinal angina. Acute mesenteric ischemia presents with sudden severe pain rather than a chronic postprandial pattern. Ischemic colitis, bacterial overgrowth, and chronic pancreatitis do not fit this vascular, multivessel atherosclerotic picture."}, {"id": 933, "domain": "other_disorders", "question": "A 5-year-old boy presents with painless rectal bleeding. A technetium-99m pertechnetate scan identifies ectopic gastric mucosa in a small bowel outpouching located about two feet from the ileocecal valve. Which of the following best describes this congenital anomaly?", "options": {"A": "Meckel diverticulum, the most common congenital small bowel anomaly, following the rule of twos.", "B": "Duodenal duplication cyst, an anomaly unrelated to the vitelline duct.", "C": "Congenital malrotation with an abnormal cecal position.", "D": "Annular pancreas causing partial obstruction of the duodenum.", "E": "Colonic diverticulum acquired later in life from chronic constipation."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Meckel diverticulum, the most common congenital small bowel anomaly, following the rule of twos): Meckel diverticulum is the most common congenital anomaly of the small bowel, resulting from incomplete obliteration of the vitelline duct, and follows the rule of twos, occurring in about two percent of the population, usually within two feet of the ileocecal valve, and often containing ectopic gastric or pancreatic tissue that can bleed. Duodenal duplication cysts, malrotation, and annular pancreas are distinct congenital anomalies, and colonic diverticula are acquired rather than congenital."}, {"id": 943, "domain": "other_disorders", "question": "A 55-year-old woman develops chronic watery diarrhea beginning shortly after laparoscopic cholecystectomy. Workup for infection, celiac disease, and inflammatory bowel disease is negative. She has lost no weight and denies any recent antibiotic use. Which of the following best explains her diarrhea?", "options": {"A": "Removal of the gallbladder causes secondary lactose intolerance in all patients.", "B": "Bile production stops entirely once the gallbladder has been removed.", "C": "Continuous bile flow into the duodenum overwhelms ileal reabsorption capacity.", "D": "Loss of the gallbladder directly causes pancreatic enzyme deficiency.", "E": "Cholecystectomy causes permanent small bowel bacterial overgrowth in most patients."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Continuous bile flow into the duodenum overwhelms ileal reabsorption capacity): After cholecystectomy, the gallbladder no longer buffers and stores bile between meals, so continuous bile flow into the duodenum can overwhelm the ileum's capacity to reabsorb bile acids, resulting in bile acid diarrhea. Cholecystectomy does not cause pancreatic enzyme deficiency, does not universally cause bacterial overgrowth or lactose intolerance, and the liver continues to produce bile after the gallbladder is removed."}, {"id": 959, "domain": "other_disorders", "question": "A 40-year-old woman has three months of localized abdominal pain that has not responded to acid suppression or antispasmodic therapy. On exam, the physician locates the point of maximal tenderness and asks her to lift her head off the table while palpating. Which of the following findings would support an abdominal wall source of pain?", "options": {"A": "The abdomen becomes diffusely rigid with rebound tenderness during the maneuver.", "B": "The pain worsens or stays the same when the abdominal muscles are tensed.", "C": "The pain completely resolves once the abdominal muscles are tensed.", "D": "The pain shifts to a completely different quadrant of the abdomen.", "E": "The patient develops new nausea and vomiting during the maneuver."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The pain worsens or stays the same when the abdominal muscles are tensed): The Carnett sign is positive, supporting an abdominal wall source of pain, when tensing the abdominal muscles brings the wall closer to the examiner's palpating finger and the pain worsens or stays the same. If the pain improves with tensing, the source is more likely intra-abdominal, since the tensed wall protects deeper viscera. A shift in location, new nausea, or diffuse rebound tenderness are not part of the Carnett maneuver's interpretation."}, {"id": 960, "domain": "other_disorders", "question": "A 45-year-old woman has chronic, sharp, focal abdominal pain reproducible by pressing on a single small point on her rectus abdominis muscle. Extensive endoscopy and imaging have been normal. Which of the following is the most appropriate diagnostic and therapeutic next step?", "options": {"A": "Abdominal CT with oral and intravenous contrast as the next step.", "B": "Referral for diagnostic laparoscopy to evaluate for adhesions.", "C": "Local anesthetic trigger point injection at the point of maximal tenderness.", "D": "Repeat upper and lower endoscopy given the chronicity of her symptoms.", "E": "Empiric proton pump inhibitor trial for presumed refractory reflux disease."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Local anesthetic trigger point injection at the point of maximal tenderness): Anterior cutaneous nerve entrapment syndrome causes focal, reproducible abdominal wall pain from a nerve trapped in the rectus sheath, and a local anesthetic trigger point injection is both diagnostic and therapeutic when it relieves the pain. Repeating endoscopy, another contrast CT, or an empiric PPI trial are unlikely to add new information after an already extensive normal workup. Diagnostic laparoscopy is more invasive and not the appropriate next step for a superficial, focal, reproducible trigger point."}, {"id": 961, "domain": "nutrition", "question": "A 60-year-old man with Crohn disease involving the terminal ileum develops macrocytic anemia, glossitis, and diminished vibratory sensation in his feet. Which of the following best explains the site of the nutrient deficiency responsible for his symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Folate, absorbed in the proximal jejunum independent of intrinsic factor.", "B": "Iron, absorbed in the duodenum through the DMT1 transporter.", "C": "Vitamin D, absorbed throughout the small bowel with bile acid assistance.", "D": "Vitamin K, absorbed in the jejunum along with other fat soluble vitamins.", "E": "Vitamin B12, absorbed almost exclusively in the terminal ileum after intrinsic factor binding."}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Vitamin B12, absorbed almost exclusively in the terminal ileum after intrinsic factor binding): Vitamin B12 is absorbed almost exclusively in the terminal ileum after binding to intrinsic factor made by gastric parietal cells, so terminal ileal Crohn disease disrupts this absorption, producing macrocytic anemia and neurologic symptoms from impaired myelin synthesis. Folate is absorbed proximally and does not cause neurologic disease when deficient. Iron and the fat soluble vitamins are absorbed at different specific sites not primarily affected by terminal ileal disease alone."}, {"id": 970, "domain": "nutrition", "question": "A 30-year-old woman newly diagnosed with celiac disease is found to have low vitamin D, low vitamin A, and a mildly elevated INR, all before starting a gluten free diet. Which of the following best explains why all these deficiencies occurred together?", "options": {"A": "Celiac disease selectively impairs only vitamin D absorption among the fat soluble vitamins.", "B": "The findings reflect a coincidental, unrelated autoimmune endocrine disorder.", "C": "Gluten itself directly destroys circulating vitamin A, D, and K molecules.", "D": "Diffuse small bowel mucosal damage impairs absorption of fat and fat soluble vitamins together.", "E": "Each vitamin deficiency arose from an entirely separate, unrelated dietary cause."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Diffuse small bowel mucosal damage impairs absorption of fat and fat soluble vitamins together): Celiac disease causes diffuse small bowel mucosal damage, which impairs absorption of fat and all fat soluble vitamins together, explaining why multiple deficiencies often appear at once rather than in isolation. It is not accurate that each deficiency has a separate unrelated cause when one mucosal disease explains all of them. Celiac disease does not selectively spare vitamins A, D, and K, this is not evidence of a coincidental separate endocrine disorder, and gluten does not directly destroy circulating vitamins."}, {"id": 971, "domain": "nutrition", "question": "A 14-year-old boy has significant weight loss and reliance on nutritional supplements because he avoids most solid foods due to a fear of choking after a prior episode. He denies any concern about his body shape or weight. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Generalized anxiety disorder with a coincidental unrelated eating pattern.", "B": "Anorexia nervosa, since significant weight loss is present regardless of the stated reason.", "C": "Avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder, distinct from anorexia due to absent body image concern.", "D": "Bulimia nervosa, given his significant reliance on nutritional supplementation.", "E": "Major depressive disorder presenting solely with a physical eating symptom."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder, distinct from anorexia due to absent body image concern): ARFID is distinguished from anorexia nervosa because it is not driven by body image concerns, and this patient's avoidance stems from a specific fear of choking rather than weight or shape concerns, fitting the diagnosis of ARFID. Anorexia nervosa specifically requires the body image disturbance that is denied here. Bulimia involves binge-purge behavior not described in this vignette, and while anxiety can coexist, the specific eating avoidance pattern described is best classified as ARFID."}, {"id": 974, "domain": "nutrition", "question": "A 55-year-old man with a history of chronic alcohol use presents with greasy, foul-smelling stools and weight loss. His physician wants a simple, noninvasive spot test to screen for pancreatic exocrine insufficiency. Which of the following tests is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "D-xylose testing as the preferred initial screening test.", "B": "Tissue transglutaminase IgA as the preferred initial screening test.", "C": "Hydrogen breath testing as the preferred initial screening test.", "D": "Fecal elastase, a spot test that screens for pancreatic exocrine insufficiency.", "E": "72 hour fecal fat collection as the preferred first line test."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Fecal elastase, a spot test that screens for pancreatic exocrine insufficiency): A spot fecal elastase has largely replaced the 72 hour fecal fat collection in practice as a convenient screening test for pancreatic exocrine insufficiency, with a low result pointing to a pancreatic cause of steatorrhea. The 72 hour fecal fat test is more cumbersome and less commonly used as a first line test today. D-xylose testing evaluates mucosal absorption rather than pancreatic function, tissue transglutaminase evaluates celiac disease, and breath testing evaluates bacterial overgrowth."}, {"id": 978, "domain": "nutrition", "question": "A patient undergoes resection of the mid-jejunum for a small bowel tumor, leaving the duodenum, terminal ileum, and colon intact. Which of the following nutrients is most likely to become deficient as a direct result of this specific resection?", "options": {"A": "Bile acids, which are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum that remains intact.", "B": "Iron, which is absorbed in the duodenum that remains intact.", "C": "Folate exclusively, since jejunal resection only affects folate absorption.", "D": "Fat and most macronutrients, which are primarily absorbed across the jejunum.", "E": "Vitamin B12, which is absorbed in the terminal ileum that remains intact."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Fat and most macronutrients, which are primarily absorbed across the jejunum): Fat, fat soluble vitamins, and most macronutrients are absorbed across the jejunum, so resecting this segment while preserving the duodenum, terminal ileum, and colon would primarily affect fat and macronutrient absorption. B12 and bile acids are absorbed in the terminal ileum, which remains intact in this scenario, and iron is absorbed in the duodenum, also intact. Folate absorption could also be affected since it occurs proximally, but the primary and broadest effect of a mid-jejunal resection is fat and macronutrient malabsorption rather than folate alone."}, {"id": 979, "domain": "nutrition", "question": "A 40-year-old man reports several months of unintended weight loss despite eating normally, along with greasy, foul-smelling stools that float in the toilet bowl. Which of the following best supports a diagnosis of malabsorption over a purely functional bowel disorder?", "options": {"A": "The combination of steatorrhea and unintended weight loss despite adequate intake.", "B": "The presence of any abdominal pain, regardless of stool characteristics.", "C": "A normal complete blood count without any nutrient deficiencies.", "D": "Symptoms that improve completely with a gluten free trial alone.", "E": "A history of intermittent bloating without any other findings."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The combination of steatorrhea and unintended weight loss despite adequate intake): Clinical clues to malabsorption include greasy, foul smelling, floating stools together with unintended weight loss despite adequate intake, which together strongly suggest a true malabsorptive process rather than a purely functional disorder. Abdominal pain alone is nonspecific and common in functional disorders too. A normal blood count without deficiencies would argue against malabsorption rather than support it, and isolated bloating without other findings is more typical of a functional disorder."}, {"id": 992, "domain": "nutrition", "question": "A 68-year-old malnourished man is scheduled for elective colon resection in six weeks. He has lost significant weight and appears visibly deconditioned on exam. Which of the following interventions before surgery has been shown to reduce postoperative complications in patients like him?", "options": {"A": "A low protein diet to reduce nitrogen load before surgery.", "B": "Strict fasting starting several weeks before the scheduled surgery.", "C": "Prehabilitation with protein supplementation and nutritional optimization before surgery.", "D": "Delaying surgery indefinitely until normal body mass index is achieved.", "E": "Preoperative bowel preparation alone without any nutritional intervention."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Prehabilitation with protein supplementation and nutritional optimization before surgery): Nutritional optimization before elective GI surgery, including prehabilitation with protein supplementation, has been shown to reduce postoperative complications in malnourished patients, making it an appropriate intervention in the weeks before his procedure. Prolonged fasting before surgery would worsen his nutritional status. Indefinitely delaying surgery until a normal BMI is reached is impractical and not evidence based, bowel preparation alone does not address nutritional status, and a low protein diet would worsen rather than help a malnourished patient."}, {"id": 995, "domain": "nutrition", "question": "A severely malnourished 55-year-old woman with a history of chronic alcohol use begins aggressive full calorie enteral feeding on hospital day one. On day two she develops arrhythmia and respiratory distress with a phosphate level of 1.0 mg/dL. Which of the following best explains this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Aspiration pneumonia from feeding tube misplacement into the airway.", "B": "An allergic reaction to a component of the enteral feeding formula.", "C": "Hospital-acquired sepsis unrelated to her nutritional status or feeding regimen.", "D": "Refeeding syndrome from an insulin surge driving phosphate into cells too rapidly.", "E": "Acute pancreatitis triggered by the sudden reintroduction of enteral feeding."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refeeding syndrome from an insulin surge driving phosphate into cells too rapidly): Refeeding syndrome occurs when nutrition is reintroduced too aggressively in a chronically malnourished patient, and the insulin surge that follows carbohydrate intake drives phosphate, along with potassium and magnesium, into cells, causing severe hypophosphatemia that can lead to arrhythmia and respiratory failure, matching this presentation and lab value. Pancreatitis, aspiration, an allergic reaction, and unrelated sepsis do not explain the specific combination of a very low phosphate level following aggressive refeeding in a malnourished patient."}, {"id": 996, "domain": "nutrition", "question": "A physician is planning to start nutritional support in a chronically malnourished patient with a history of prolonged starvation. He has been eating almost nothing for the past several weeks. Which of the following strategies is most important to prevent refeeding syndrome?", "options": {"A": "Delay all nutrition for one week before beginning any feeding.", "B": "Start nutrition immediately at full calculated caloric goals from day one.", "C": "Start nutrition at a low caloric rate with close electrolyte monitoring.", "D": "Avoid checking phosphate, potassium, and magnesium unless symptoms develop.", "E": "Restrict all carbohydrate intake indefinitely to avoid any insulin response."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Start nutrition at a low caloric rate with close electrolyte monitoring): Prevention of refeeding syndrome requires starting nutrition at a low caloric rate, aggressively repleting electrolytes before and during feeding, and monitoring phosphate, potassium, and magnesium daily during the first week. Starting at full caloric goals immediately significantly raises refeeding risk. Avoiding electrolyte monitoring would miss the exact abnormality this strategy aims to prevent, indefinitely restricting all carbohydrates is impractical and not the standard approach, and delaying all nutrition for a week is unnecessary and can worsen malnutrition."}, {"id": 998, "domain": "nutrition", "question": "A 70-year-old man with a stroke and persistent dysphagia is expected to need nutritional support for at least three months. He cannot safely swallow even thickened liquids on a swallow study. Which of the following is the most appropriate enteral access method?", "options": {"A": "Total parenteral nutrition through a central venous catheter.", "B": "Percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy tube for long term enteral access.", "C": "Oral nutritional supplements alone despite his persistent dysphagia.", "D": "Nasogastric tube maintained continuously for the full three month period.", "E": "Peripheral parenteral nutrition rather than any enteral access device."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy tube for long term enteral access): Long term enteral access, generally beyond four to six weeks, is achieved through a percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy tube, making it appropriate for a patient expected to need support for at least three months. A nasogastric tube is intended for short term use and is uncomfortable and prone to complications if left in for months. Parenteral nutrition is reserved for a nonfunctional gut, not simply for convenience, and oral supplements alone are not appropriate given his persistent dysphagia and aspiration risk."}];
